    "description": "# Future Directions: Chromatic Thresholds and Social-Network Palettes\n\n## 1. Clique minors as chromatic vanishing certificates\n\n**Conjecture.** If a finite graph contains a complete graph on `t` vertices as a minor, then its chromatic counting function vanishes at every nonnegative palette size below `t`. Conversely, determine natural minor-closed graph classes on which this vanishing pattern forces a complete `t`-minor.\n\nThe key insight is that Hadwiger-type structure can be phrased as an interval of forced zeros rather than only as a lower bound on a chromatic number.\n\n**Why now?** The exact minimal-positive-value characterization isolates the zero interval that must be controlled, while deletion\u2013contraction offers a natural mechanism for tracking it through graph reductions.\n\n## 2. Complexity of emotional-threshold detection\n\n**Conjecture.** For every fixed `k \u2265 3`, deciding whether the emotional chromatic number of a finite network is at most `k` remains computationally equivalent, under polynomial reductions, to deciding whether its chromatic counting function is positive at `k`; this equivalence persists for succinctly represented networks and preserves natural approximation gaps.\n\nThe key insight is that the semantic threshold and the algebraic positivity test are exactly the same decision boundary above the emotional floor.\n\n**Why now?** The threshold equivalence identifies a precise bridge on which reductions, parameterized algorithms, and counting-versus-decision separations can be compared without changing the underlying instances.\n\n## 3. High-girth networks with unbounded emotional diversity\n\n**Conjecture.** For every pair of integers `g \u2265 3` and `r \u2265 3`, there is a finite network with no cycle shorter than `g` whose emotional chromatic number exceeds `r`; moreover, such examples can be chosen so that the first positive chromatic evaluation occurs sharply after a quantitatively long zero interval.\n\nThe key insight is that local tree-like friendship geometry need not constrain the global positivity threshold of proper emotion assignments.\n\n**Why now?** The minimal-positive-value law turns the classical high-girth/high-chromatic phenomenon into a testable statement about an explicit sequence of vanishing evaluations.\n\n## 4. Stability of friendship-network assignment counts\n\n**Conjecture.** Among graphs formed from `n` triangles by identifying vertices while keeping a connected triangle-intersection pattern, the windmill graph extremizes the ratio between the number of six-emotion assignments and the number of minimum-palette assignments; characterize all equality cases.\n\nThe key insight is that the windmill\u2019s independent outer pairs produce the exact ratio `2 \u00b7 10^n`, suggesting that overlap geometry controls a multiplicative entropy loss.\n\n**Why now?** Closed formulas are available at both the minimum palette and the six-emotion palette, providing a calibrated baseline against which more complicated triangle gluing patterns can be tested.\n",
