    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## 1. Exact classification of the repaired trajectory\n\n**Conjecture.** The globally additive-avoiding greedy trajectory beginning at one is exactly the odd sequence: its term at index `n` is `2n+1`.\n\nThe key insight is that all sums of two earlier odd values are even, while the immediately preceding even candidate is forbidden as the sum of the initial one and the current value. **Why now?** The existence and growth-ceiling results isolate precisely the local admissibility argument needed for an induction, and the initial computations expose the parity invariant.\n\n## 2. Stability under changing the initial value\n\n**Conjecture.** For every positive initial value, the globally additive-avoiding greedy trajectory is eventually an arithmetic progression, with its common difference determined by the additive semigroup generated by the initial segment.\n\nThe key insight is that the forbidden set is a finite restricted sumset whose residue-class profile may stabilize even while its diameter grows. **Why now?** The explicit successor bound shows that all choices occur in a short moving interval, making residue stabilization a concrete and falsifiable mechanism rather than an unconstrained asymptotic claim.\n\n## 3. Higher-order additive avoidance\n\n**Conjecture.** If candidates avoid all sums of exactly `r` prior terms, with repetition allowed, then the greedy trajectory from one is eventually periodic modulo `r` and has linear growth.\n\nThe key insight is that an `r`-fold sum of a single stable residue class occupies one residue class, potentially leaving a neighboring class permanently admissible. **Why now?** Pair-sum avoidance demonstrates that a global exclusion rule need not force quadratic growth; varying the arity tests which part of the linear behavior is genuinely structural.\n\n## 4. Hypergraph sparsity under bounded perturbations\n\n**Conjecture.** Any increasing integer sequence whose successor differs from the globally greedy admissible choice by a uniformly bounded amount has a chronological additive hypergraph with zero upper edge density.\n\nThe key insight is that approximate greediness may preserve enough separation from the restricted sumset to suppress additive triples statistically even when exact sum-freeness is lost. **Why now?** The exact repaired rule produces an empty chronological hypergraph, providing the extremal endpoint from which a quantitative stability theory can be developed.\n\n## 5. Counting-function dichotomy\n\n**Conjecture.** A globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy sequence has either positive asymptotic density or a counting function bounded above by a constant multiple of the square root of the ambient cutoff, according to whether its stabilized residue support is sum-free in a finite cyclic group.\n\nThe key insight is that finite cyclic sum-free structure may govern the transition between linear and Sidon-like sparse growth. **Why now?** The repaired construction supplies both a concrete positive-density model and a restricted-sumset formulation suitable for comparison with additive-combinatorial density bounds.\n",
