    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nResearch thread `th_0d5dcecd`, cycle 1 (root direction `fd_0391`).\n\n## What was proved this cycle\n\nThe file `Novelty/RamseyProbabilistic.lean` formalizes the classical **first-moment\n(probabilistic method) lower bound for diagonal Ramsey numbers**, phrased entirely\nthrough finite cardinality counting (no measure theory). All results compile with only\nthe standard axioms `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound` and contain no `sorry`.\n\n* `card_const_on_le` \u2014 an abstract counting bound: the number of Boolean functions on a\n  finite type `\u03b1` that are constant on a nonempty finset `T` is at most\n  `2 ^ (card \u03b1 - card T + 1)`. Proof by an at-most-two-to-one restriction map to the\n  complement of `T`.\n* `exists_avoiding` \u2014 the abstract first-moment / union-bound principle: if a finite\n  family of \"bad\" subsets of a finite type has total size `< card`, some point avoids\n  all of them.\n* `ramsey_lower_bound` \u2014 **main theorem.** If `2 \u2264 k \u2264 n` and\n  `2 \u00b7 C(n,k) < 2^(C(k,2))`, then there is a 2-colouring `c : Sym2 (Fin n) \u2192 Bool` of\n  the edges of `K_n` such that every `k`-subset of vertices contains two internal\n  edges of different colours (i.e. no monochromatic `K_k`).\n* `no_mono_K4_in_K6` \u2014 concrete instance: `K_6` has a 2-colouring with no monochromatic\n  `K_4` (so `R(4,4) > 6`), obtained from the main theorem with the criterion checked by\n  `decide` (`2\u00b715 = 30 < 64 = 2^6`).\n* `two_pow_lt_factorial_sq` \u2014 arithmetic engine `2^(k+2) < (k!)^2` for `k \u2265 3`, proved by\n  induction.\n* `choose_sq_bound` \u2014 the finite Erd\u0151s criterion `2\u00b7C(n,k) < 2^C(k,2)` holds whenever\n  `k \u2265 3` and `n^2 \u2264 2^k`. Proof by comparing squares: `k!\u00b7C(n,k) \u2264 n^k` and `n^2 \u2264 2^k`\n  give `(2\u00b7C(n,k))^2\u00b7(k!)^2 \u2264 2^(k\u00b2+2)`, and `2^(k+2) < (k!)^2` closes the gap against\n  `(2^C(k,2))^2 = 2^(k(k-1))`.\n* `ramsey_lower_bound_exp` \u2014 **the uniform exponential bound.** For `k \u2265 3` and any `n`\n  with `k \u2264 n` and `n^2 \u2264 2^k` (i.e. `n \u2264 2^{k/2}`) there is a 2-colouring of `K_n` with\n  no monochromatic `K_k`. Since the largest such `n` is `\u230a2^{k/2}\u230b`, this is exactly the\n  classical `R(k,k) > 2^{k/2}` (Direction 3's headline claim).\n* `no_mono_K6_in_K8` \u2014 concrete instance of the exponential bound: `K_8` has a\n  2-colouring with no monochromatic `K_6` (so `R(6,6) > 8 = 2^{6/2}`), from\n  `8^2 = 64 = 2^6`.\n\nThe criterion `2 \u00b7 C(n,k) < 2^(C(k,2))` is exactly Erd\u0151s' inequality\n`C(n,k) \u00b7 2^{1-C(k,2)} < 1` cleared of denominators. This isolates the analytic content\nof the local/first-moment argument as a single arithmetic inequality, exactly the\n\"separate probabilistic estimate from combinatorial existence\" program of Direction 1 \u2014\nhere realized for the first-moment (union) bound.\n\n## How this advances the original list\n\n* **Direction 3 (quantitative Ramsey lower bound).** Now **fully proved at the classical\n  exponential scale.** The Boolean-lattice event count is done exactly, and the \"sole\n  remaining step\" of asymptotic arithmetic is discharged in `choose_sq_bound`, giving\n  the textbook `R(k,k) > 2^{k/2}` via `ramsey_lower_bound_exp` (integer form\n  `n^2 \u2264 2^k`).\n* **Direction 1 (local lemma from finite survivor ratios).** The abstract\n  `exists_avoiding` + `card_const_on_le` pair is precisely the \"combinatorial existence\n  mechanism\" separated from the probabilistic estimate, at the first-moment level.\n\n## Concrete next steps\n\n1. **~~Uniform asymptotic corollary.~~ DONE this cycle.** Proved as `choose_sq_bound`\n   + `ramsey_lower_bound_exp`: `k \u2265 3` and `n^2 \u2264 2^k` imply the criterion, hence\n   `R(k,k) > 2^{k/2}`. The remaining refinement is a purely cosmetic restatement over\n   the reals using `\u230aReal.sqrt (2^k)\u230b\u208a` instead of the integer condition `n^2 \u2264 2^k`.\n2. **Lov\u00e1sz Local Lemma (Direction 1).** Replace the union bound `card_biUnion_le` in\n   `exists_avoiding` by a dependency-degree induction: state a `d`-bounded dependency\n   hypothesis and prove `every finite survivor set keeps a positive fraction` under\n   `e\u00b7p\u00b7(d+1) \u2264 1`, reusing `card_const_on_le` as the per-constraint cardinality ratio.\n3. **Tur\u00e1n bridge (Directions 4\u20135).** Re-express `internalEdges` counts as an edge-count\n   functional and connect the exact `C(k,2)` bookkeeping here with clique-free extremal\n   counts.\n",
