    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions: Fourier Analysis as a Functor\n\n## What this phase establishes\n\n`Catalog/Bridges/FourierAsFunctor.lean` proves a self-contained finite-coordinate model of the requested bridge.\n\n1. **Contravariant character duality.** For finite free coordinate modules, transpose defines a functor from the opposite matrix category. The composition law is proved explicitly. The file also proves the corresponding composition-reversal theorem for Mathlib's actual circle-valued `PontryaginDual.map`.\n2. **Bidual equivalence.** Transposition twice is naturally isomorphic to the identity, yielding an equivalence between the finite-free matrix category's opposite and itself. This is a finite-dimensional algebraic analogue of Pontryagin biduality.\n3. **Fourier inversion.** The two-point DFT over `\u211a` has an explicit inverse and therefore gives an isomorphism in the matrix category.\n4. **Contrarian disproof: unrestricted naturality.** The two-point DFT does not commute with every endomorphism (a coordinate projection is a certified counterexample). Therefore Fourier matrices are not a natural endomorphism of the identity functor on a category whose arrows are all linear maps.\n5. **Contrarian disproof: variance is not uncertainty.** Contravariant duality alone does not imply a support uncertainty bound. The identity transform and a delta vector give support product `1 < 2`. Orthogonality or a suitable nondegenerate Fourier pairing is additional essential structure.\n\n## Recommended next formalization steps\n\n### 1. The category of locally compact abelian groups\n\nPackage commutative topological groups with local compactness and Hausdorffness as bundled objects, with continuous homomorphisms as arrows. Lift `PontryaginDual.map` to a functor from the opposite category. The composition theorem in the present file is the arrow-level core.\n\n### 2. Full Pontryagin biduality\n\nConstruct the evaluation morphism\n\n`A \u2192 PontryaginDual (PontryaginDual A)`, `a \u21a6 (\u03c7 \u21a6 \u03c7 a)`,\n\nprove continuity, and then prove it is a topological group isomorphism for LCA groups. The finite-free transpose equivalence here provides the exact categorical shape but not the analytic LCA theorem.\n\n### 3. Correct naturality domain for Fourier transform\n\nRather than naturality for all linear maps, identify morphisms compatible with Haar measure and character pullback. Candidate settings include finite abelian groups with group isomorphisms, or an indexed/kernel formalism where pushforward and pullback are tracked separately. State Fourier naturality between the appropriate function-space functors, not as an endomorphism of one identity functor.\n\n### 4. Categorical uncertainty with substantive hypotheses\n\nFormalize finite-group support uncertainty from an invertible character table plus a nonvanishing-minor or orthogonality hypothesis. The counterexample proved here should remain as a regression theorem showing that mere contravariance cannot discharge those assumptions.\n\n### 5. Analytic Fourier transform\n\nAfter Haar integration infrastructure is fixed, define the Fourier transform on `L\u00b9(A)` with values on `PontryaginDual A`, prove change-of-variables naturality for measure-compatible isomorphisms, and connect Fourier inversion to Pontryagin biduality.\n",
