    "description": "# Future Directions: Clique-Incidence Codegrees\n\nThe formal results in `Catalog/Algebra/RamseyTheory/CliqueIncidenceCodegrees.lean`\nestablish that, for `0 < r \u2264 k`, the clique-incidence construction has exactly\n`C(n,k)` distinct edges, and that two edges indexed by `k`-sets `S,T` have\nintersection size exactly `C(|S \u2229 T|,r)`.  Thus the possible collisions in the\nedge count disappear in the nondegenerate Ramsey range; the remaining structure\nis entirely in the overlap profile.\n\n## Conjecture 1: explicit symmetric local-lemma range\n\nFor each fixed `r \u2265 3`, there is a constant `c_r > 0` such that whenever\n\n`e \u00b7 2^(1-C(k,r)) \u00b7 (1 + \u03a3_{j=r}^{k-1} C(k,j) C(n-k,k-j)) \u2264 1`,\n\nthe diagonal Ramsey property `DiagonalRamsey n r k` fails; moreover, for\ninfinitely many `k`, this criterion permits an `n` at least\n`exp(c_r k^(r-1))` times larger than the raw edge-count criterion\n`C(n,k) < 2^(C(k,r)-1)` permits.\n\nThis is falsifiable by deriving the dependency degree from the proved disjointness\ncriterion and comparing the two explicit numerical inequalities.\n\n## Conjecture 2: lopsided improvement from exact codegrees\n\nFor every fixed `r \u2265 3`, weighting a pair of bad-clique events indexed by `S,T`\nby the proved codegree `C(|S \u2229 T|,r)` yields a cluster-expansion local-lemma\ncriterion that is strictly stronger than the symmetric criterion in Conjecture 1\nfor all sufficiently large `k`.\n\nA counterexample would be an infinite sequence of `k` for which optimization of\nthe overlap-weighted criterion gives no larger admissible `n` than the symmetric\ndependency-degree bound.\n\n## Conjecture 3: spectrum determined by overlap classes\n\nFix `0 < r \u2264 k \u2264 n`.  The Gram matrix of the inclusion matrix from `r`-sets to\n`k`-sets has entries `C(|S \u2229 T|,r)` and has exactly `r+1` distinct nonzero\neigenvalues whenever `n \u2265 k+r`.\n\nThe entry formula is now reduced to the formal codegree theorem.  The conjecture\nis a finite, exact spectral assertion and can be falsified by a single parameter\ntriple or symbolic rank/eigenvalue calculation.\n\n## Conjecture 4: strict overlap monotonicity in optimized certificates\n\nFor fixed `r \u2265 3` and sufficiently large `k`, every optimal permutation-invariant\nfractional certificate for splitting all clique-incidence edges assigns strictly\nlarger penalty to overlap class `j+1` than to class `j` for each\n`r \u2264 j < k`.\n\nThis can be tested as a finite linear or semidefinite optimization problem on the\n`k-r+1` overlap classes.  Failure of strict monotonicity at any arbitrarily large\n`k` disproves the conjecture.\n",
