    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## Scope of the proved connector\n\n`BourgainSlicingConnector.lean` proves a complete slicing theorem for positive axis-aligned boxes of volume one. It connects convex geometry to a finite multiplicative pigeonhole principle: the full volume factors as\n\n`(coordinate section volume) \u00d7 (perpendicular width)`.\n\nSince a finite product equal to one cannot have every factor strictly greater than one, some width is at most one; positivity and the factorization then force the corresponding section volume to be at least one.\n\nThis is a special case and structural model, not a proof of the requested dimension-free assertion for arbitrary convex bodies.\n\n## Natural extensions\n\n1. **Linear images (parallelotopes).** Define volume and hyperplane section measures for invertible linear images of boxes, then transport the product identity through determinants and restrictions of linear maps.\n2. **Ellipsoids.** Formalize ellipsoids as positive-definite linear images of Euclidean balls and derive explicit central-section formulas. This would connect slicing bounds with eigenvalues and determinant normalization.\n3. **General measurable sections.** Develop the measure-theoretic definition of `(n-1)`-dimensional hyperplane volume using Hausdorff measure or an isometric parametrization of affine hyperplanes.\n4. **Convex-body infrastructure.** Package compactness, convexity, nonempty interior, volume normalization, affine transformations, and central sections into reusable Lean definitions.\n5. **Isotropic normalization.** Formalize barycenters, covariance operators, and isotropic position. This is the standard bridge from geometric sections to analytic information about a normalized measure.\n6. **Analytic bridge.** Relate maximal hyperplane-section density to one-dimensional marginals (via an appropriate coarea/Fubini statement), making precise the connection between convex geometry and probability density bounds.\n7. **Dimension-dependent bounds.** Before attempting a universal constant for arbitrary convex bodies, formalize an available dimension-dependent lower bound. This would validate the full geometric interface without presupposing the unresolved dimension-free step.\n\nAny extension to arbitrary convex bodies must retain an honest distinction between established intermediate bounds and the universal-constant slicing conjecture itself.\n",
