    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nConcrete, falsifiable conjectures suggested by the formalisation in\n`Catalog/Algebra/DerivedFunctors/`. Each one is stated so that a single Lean statement would\nsettle it, and each is a strict strengthening of something already proved in this project.\n\nThe formalised material (all of it complete, with no `sorry`, and depending only on the standard\naxioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`) is:\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `Resolutions.lean` | the short exact sequences `0 \u2192 \u2124 --(\u00b7k)--> \u2124 \u2192 \u2124/k \u2192 0` and `0 \u2192 \u2124 \u2192 \u211a \u2192 \u211a\u29f8\u2124 \u2192 0` |\n| `ZModResolution.lean` | the bundled `ProjectiveResolution (\u2124/k)`; vanishing of `L\u2099\u208a\u2082F` on `\u2124/k`, in particular `Tor\u2099\u208a\u2082(G, \u2124/k) = 0` |\n| `IntInjectiveResolution.lean` | the bundled `InjectiveResolution \u2124`; vanishing of `R\u207f\u207a\u00b2F` on `\u2124` |\n| `LongExactSequence.lean` | the long exact (co)homology sequence of a short exact sequence of complexes, element-wise, plus consequences |\n| `ExtZMod.lean` | `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y) \u2243 Y/kY`, via an explicit surjection `Y \u2192 Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y)` with kernel `kY` |\n| `Ext.lean` | `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(\u2124/k, Y) = 0`, `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, \u2124) = 0`, `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y) = 0 \u2194 Y` is `k`-divisible, `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, \u211a) = 0`, `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, \u2124) \u2260 0` |\n| `ExtFinitelyGenerated.lean` | vanishing of `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, Y)` whenever `X` has a projective presentation with projective kernel, freeness of the kernel of `\u2124\u207f \u2192 X`, and `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, Y) = 0` for **every finitely generated** `\u2124`-module `X` |\n| `Tor.lean` | `Tor\u2080 \u2245 \u2297`, vanishing of higher `Tor` against flat modules, and the contrapositive |\n| `FlatCriterion.lean` | `Flat \u2124 G \u2194 G` torsion-free `\u2194 ker(k\u00b7) = 0` for all `k \u2260 0` `\u2194 Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) = 0` for all `k \u2260 0` `\u2194 Tor\u2099\u208a\u2081(G, \u2212) = 0`; `\u2124/k` is not flat for `k \u2265 2` |\n| `UniversalCoefficients.lean` | universal coefficients with flat coefficients, and failure of exactness after tensoring with `\u2124/k` |\n| `UCTTwoTerm.lean` | the universal coefficient isomorphisms `H\u2080(G \u2297 C) \u2245 G \u2297 H\u2080(C)` and `H\u2081(G \u2297 C) \u2245 Tor\u2081(G, H\u2080(C))` for the two-term free complex `C = (\u2124 --(\u00b7k)--> \u2124)` |\n| `TorZMod.lean` | `Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) \u2245 G[k]`, `G \u2297 \u2124/k \u2245 G/kG`, `Tor\u2081(\u2124/k, \u2124/k) \u2245 \u2124/k \u2260 0`, and the resulting non-exactness in degree one |\n\n## C1. Naturality of the isomorphism `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y) \u2245 Y/kY`\n\nThe isomorphism `Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y) \u2245 Y/kY` is natural in `Y`: for every morphism `f : Y \u27f6 Y'` of\n`\u2124`-modules the induced square, with vertical maps the isomorphisms and horizontal maps\n`Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, f)` and the map induced by `f` on the quotients, commutes.\n\n*Status.* The isomorphism itself is a theorem: `extOneZModEquiv` gives `Y \u29f8 kY \u2243+ Ext\u00b9(\u2124/k, Y)`\nfor every `k \u2260 0`, built from the explicit surjection `y \u21a6 extClass \u2218 (1 \u21a6 y)`\n(`extOneHom_surjective`) whose kernel is exactly `kY` (`ker_extOneHom`). What is not formalised is\nfunctoriality of this description in `Y`. Falsified by a morphism `f` for which the square fails\nto commute.\n\n## C2. Hereditarity of `\u2124`: all `Ext\u207f` vanish for `n \u2265 2`\n\nFor all `\u2124`-modules `X`, `Y` and all `n : \u2115`, `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, Y) = 0`.\n\n*Status.* Now proved in three cases. (i) `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, \u2124) = 0` for all `X` (`ext_int_eq_zero`;\ninjective dimension of `\u2124` is `\u2264 1`, and the bundled `intInjectiveResolution` gives `R\u207f\u207a\u00b2F(\u2124) = 0`\nfor every additive `F`). (ii) `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(\u2124/k, Y) = 0` for all `Y` (`ext_zmod_eq_zero`). (iii) More\ngenerally `Ext\u207f\u207a\u00b2(X, Y) = 0` for every **finitely generated** `X` and every `Y`\n(`ext_fg_eq_zero`), obtained from the free presentation `0 \u2192 ker f \u2192 \u2124\u1d50 \u2192 X \u2192 0` whose kernel is\nagain free (`free_ker_of_fin`) via the abstract criterion\n`ext_eq_zero_of_projective_presentation`. The remaining case is `X` of infinite rank; it needs the\nstatement \u2014 not currently in Mathlib \u2014 that *every* submodule of a free `\u2124`-module is free.\nFalsified by a single pair `X`, `Y` with `Ext\u00b2(X, Y) \u2260 0`.\n\n## C3. `Tor\u2081` against the prime cyclic groups already detects flatness\n\nFor a `\u2124`-module `G`: `G` is flat if and only if `Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/p) = 0` for every **prime** `p`.\n\n*Status.* The version quantified over all `k \u2260 0` is now a theorem:\n`flat_iff_isZero_torOne_zmod` proves `Flat \u2124 G \u2194 \u2200 k \u2260 0, Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) = 0`, via\n`flat_iff_torsionFree` (flat = torsion-free over a Bezout domain),\n`isZero_kernel_mulBy_iff` (`ker(k\u00b7) = 0` iff `k` acts injectively) and the computation\n`torOneZModIso : Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) \u2245 G[k]`; the strengthening\n`flat_iff_isZero_tor_succ` shows flatness is equivalent to the vanishing of *all* `Tor\u2099\u208a\u2081(G, \u2212)`.\nThe conjecture asks whether the test family can be shrunk to the primes, i.e. whether\n`G[p] = 0` for all primes `p` forces `G[k] = 0` for all `k \u2265 1`. Falsified by a group `G` with\n`G[p] = 0` for every prime `p` but `Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) \u2260 0` for some `k`.\n\n## C4. Balancing of `Tor`\n\nFor all `\u2124`-modules `G`, `H` and all `n`, the two left-derived functors of the tensor product\nagree: `Tor(ModuleCat \u2124) n \u2245 Tor'(ModuleCat \u2124) n`, and in particular\n`Tor\u2081(G, H) \u2245 Tor\u2081(H, G)`.\n\n*Status.* Mathlib defines both `Tor` (derived in the second variable) and `Tor'` (derived in the\nfirst) but does not prove them isomorphic. Our computation gives strong evidence in the cyclic\ncase: `Tor\u2081(G, \u2124/k) \u2245 G[k]` (`torOneZModIso`), which for `G = \u2124/m` has order `gcd(k, m)`,\nsymmetric in `k` and `m`; and `Tor\u2099\u208a\u2082(G, \u2124/k) = 0` (`isZero_Tor_two_le_zmod`) matches the\nvanishing of `Tor'\u2099\u208a\u2082` predicted by the same resolution used in the other variable. Falsified by\na pair `G`, `H` and a degree `n` with `Tor\u2099(G, H) \u2247 Tor'\u2099(G, H)`.\n\n## C5. Universal coefficient theorem with the `Tor` correction term\n\nLet `C` be a chain complex of free `\u2124`-modules and `G` an abelian group. Then for every `n` there\nis a short exact sequence\n`0 \u2192 H\u2099(C) \u2297 G \u2192 H\u2099(C \u2297 G) \u2192 Tor\u2081(H\u2099\u208b\u2081(C), G) \u2192 0`,\nnatural in `C` and `G`, and split (non-naturally).\n\n*Status.* We proved the theorem for the two-term free complex `C = (\u2124 --(\u00b7k)--> \u2124)` in both\ndegrees: `H\u2080(G \u2297 C) \u2245 G \u2297 H\u2080(C)` (`uctHomologyZeroIso`, no correction term since `H\u208b\u2081 = 0`) and\n`H\u2081(G \u2297 C) \u2245 Tor\u2081(G, H\u2080(C))` (`uctHomologyOneIso`, pure correction term since `H\u2081(C) = 0`,\n`isZero_resComplex_homology_one`); the correction term is genuinely nonzero\n(`not_isZero_uct_tor_term`, `torOneZMod_self_ne_zero`). We also proved the flat-coefficient case\n`H\u2099(C \u2297 G) \u2245 H\u2099(C) \u2297 G` for arbitrary complexes over any commutative ring\n(`homologyTensorFlatIso`). Proving the general statement needs freeness of submodules of free\n`\u2124`-modules (cf. C2) and the splitting `C \u2245 Z \u2295 B[-1]`. Falsified by a free complex `C`, a group\n`G` and a degree `n` where the displayed sequence is not exact.\n",
