    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## What is formalized\n\nFour Lean files, all compiling with `lake build` and free of `sorry`:\n\n* `Geometry/RomanDomination/Variants.lean` \u2014 the six Roman-type domination\n  parameters (`\u03b3`, `\u03b3_R`, `\u03b3_I`, `\u03b3_dR`, `\u03b3_p`, `u`), defined uniformly as\n  infima of weights over classes of labellings, the full chain of comparison\n  inequalities between them (`roman_chain`), and `\u03b3_R(\u22a5) = n`.\n* `Geometry/RomanDomination/ConvexBipartite.lean` \u2014 convex bipartite graphs\n  (`IsConvexBipartite`), their `2`-colourability, convexity of `K_{m,n}`, and the\n  exact values\n  `\u03b3_R(K_{m,n}) = min 4 (min (m+1) (n+1))` (`m, n \u2265 1`) and\n  `\u03b3_I(K_{m,n}) = min 4 (min m n)` (`m, n \u2265 2`).\n* `Geometry/RomanDomination/DoubleRoman.lean` \u2014 the general bound `\u03b3_dR(G) \u2265 3`\n  for graphs with at least two vertices, and the exact double Roman domination\n  number of `K_{m,n}` (`gammaDR_K_eq`): `3` if `min m n = 1`, `4` if\n  `min m n = 2`, and `6` if `min m n \u2265 3` \u2014 the value `5` never occurs.\n* `Geometry/RomanDomination/PerfectUnique.lean` \u2014 the exact perfect Roman\n  domination number `\u03b3_p(K_{m,n}) = min 4 (min (m+1) (n+1))`, which coincides\n  with `\u03b3_R(K_{m,n})`, and the exact unique response Roman domination number\n  `u(K_{m,n}) = min (m+1) (n+1)`, together with the resulting unbounded gap\n  `u - \u03b3_p = min m n - 3` for `m, n \u2265 3`.\n\nThe key structural fact behind the unique response value is that in a complete\nbipartite graph a vertex labelled `2` forbids *every* vertex on the opposite side\nfrom carrying a positive label, so one side collapses to `0` and the other side\nmust avoid `0` entirely.\n\n## Remaining directions\n\n### 1. The interval structure of convex bipartite graphs\n\n`IsConvexBipartite` is defined and `K_{m,n}` is shown to satisfy it.  The next\nstructural step is to extract, for a convex bipartite `G`, the two functions\n`lo, hi : Fin n \u2192 \u2115` with `N(b) = [lo b, hi b]`, and to prove the monotonicity\nlemma the left-to-right dynamic programming algorithms rely on: after processing\nthe first `k` left vertices, the only information about the past needed by the\nfuture is which right vertices with `lo \u2264 k` are still undominated.\n\n### 2. Formalizing the dynamic programming algorithms\n\nThe eventual goal is a verified polynomial-time algorithm computing `\u03b3_R`, `\u03b3_I`,\n`\u03b3_dR`, `\u03b3_p` and `u` on convex bipartite graphs.  A realistic decomposition:\n\n1. define the DP state and the transition function as a Lean `def`;\n2. prove *soundness* \u2014 every DP value is realised by some labelling;\n3. prove *completeness* \u2014 every labelling is dominated by some DP run;\n4. conclude that the DP optimum equals the `sInf` used in the definitions here.\n\nSteps (2) and (3) are the mathematical content; the `sInf`-based definitions in\n`Variants.lean` were chosen precisely so that such an equality is expressible\nwithout any further glue.\n\n### 3. Other structured graph families\n\n`K_{m,n}` is the extreme convex bipartite graph.  Complete bipartite graphs minus\na perfect matching, bipartite paths and cycles, and stars with subdivided edges\nare all convex bipartite and all have closed-form Roman-type parameters; each\nwould exercise the same lower-bound technique (a `0`-labelled vertex on one side\nforces mass on the other side) already used here.\n\n### 4. Chordal bipartite graphs and hardness\n\nChordal bipartiteness (every cycle of length at least six has a chord) is not yet\nformalized here, because Mathlib's cycle API makes the \"induced cycle\"\nformulation awkward.  A workable route is to define chordal bipartite via\n*bisimplicial edge elimination orderings*, a purely finite combinatorial\nstatement equivalent to the cycle definition.  The NP-hardness side of the paper\nwould require a formal model of computation and polynomial-time reductions; this\nis a much larger undertaking and is best kept separate.\n\n### 5. Sharpness of the inequality chain\n\nAll the inequalities in `roman_chain` are known to be sharp.  Explicit witnessing\nfamilies make the chain a complete picture: `gammaR_bot` witnesses `\u03b3_R = n`,\n`gammaR_K` and `gammaDR_K_eq` witness `\u03b3_dR = 3\u03b3` for `K_{m,n}` with `m, n \u2265 3`,\nand `gammaUR_sub_gammaPR_K` witnesses the unbounded `u`-versus-`\u03b3_p` gap.  Stars\nwould give the remaining case `\u03b3_R = 2\u03b3`.\n",
