    "description": "# Future directions: falsifiable conjectures from the group-model Schnorr formalization\n\nThe new files are\n\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/ZeroKnowledge/SchnorrGroupProtocol.lean` \u2014 Schnorr \u03a3-protocol in a\n  commutative group of exponent `q` (`gexp` API, completeness, knowledge soundness /\n  extraction, perfect HVZK as an equality of `PMF`s, soundness error `\u2264 1/q`);\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/ZeroKnowledge/SchnorrGroupFiatShamir.lean` \u2014 the Fiat\u2013Shamir\n  transform, forking extraction, oracle programming, and the random-oracle bounds `1/q` and\n  `Q/q`;\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/ZeroKnowledge/SchnorrGroupInstances.lean` \u2014 exact soundness error\n  `= 1/q` in a cyclic group of prime order, unique responses, and the concrete instance\n  `Multiplicative (ZMod q)`;\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/ZeroKnowledge/SchnorrGroupBridge.lean` \u2014 identification of the\n  catalog's additive model (`Cryptography.SchnorrIdentification`) with the group model at\n  `Multiplicative (ZMod p)`, plus the resulting `PMF`-level HVZK statement for the additive\n  model.\n\nEach conjecture below is a precise Lean-statable claim that can be proved or refuted.\n\n## C1. The `Q/q` random-oracle bound is tight, and `Q/q` is attained only by distinct commitments\n\n`rom_union_bound` shows that an adversary precommitting to `Q` pairs `(a i, z i)` succeeds\nwith probability at most `Q/q` over the oracle answer vector.  Conjecture: in a cyclic group\nof prime order `q`, equality holds **iff** the commitments `a i` are pairwise distinct (and\n`Q \u2264 q`); if two indices share a commitment, the success probability drops by at least\n`1/q\u00b2`.  Falsifiable: exhibit a family with repeated commitments attaining `Q/q`.\n\n## C2. Perfect HVZK survives adaptive challenge choice but not adaptive statement choice\n\n`hvzk_pmf` fixes the challenge `c` before sampling.  Conjecture: for every function\n`C : G \u2192 ZMod q` (a verifier choosing the challenge from the commitment), the honest\ntranscript distribution `r \u21a6 (g^r, C (g^r), r + C(g^r)\u00b7x)` is **not** in general equal to any\nwitness-free simulator's output distribution \u2014 concretely, there exists `q`, `x` and `C` for\nwhich no simulator matching `hvzk` exists, while for every *constant* `C` one does.  This\nwould formally separate honest-verifier ZK from malicious-verifier ZK inside the catalog's\nframework.\n\n## C3. Special soundness is equivalent to the `1/q` soundness error in this model\n\nConjecture: for a fixed public key `pub \u2260 1` in a group of prime order `q`, the following are\nequivalent for the predicate `Accepts g pub`: (i) any two accepting transcripts with a common\ncommitment and distinct challenges determine a unique extracted exponent; (ii) for every\n`(a, z)` the accepting-challenge set has cardinality exactly one.  We proved (ii) \u21d2 tight\nsoundness error; the converse direction, stated as an abstract property of an arbitrary\nacceptance predicate `P : G \u2192 ZMod q \u2192 ZMod q \u2192 Prop`, is open in the formalization and is\nthe natural definition of a \"generic \u03a3-protocol with knowledge error `1/q`\".\n\n## C4. Exponent-`q` hypothesis can be weakened to `pub \u2208 Subgroup.zpowers g`\n\nAll extraction results assume `pub ^ q = 1`.  Conjecture: `special_soundness_witness` remains\ntrue, with the same extractor, under the strictly weaker hypothesis\n`pub \u2208 Subgroup.zpowers g` in an arbitrary (possibly infinite, possibly non-commutative but\nwith `g` central) group, provided `orderOf g = q`.  Falsifiable: a group, `g` of order `q`,\nand `pub` a power of `g` for which the extractor fails.\n\n## C5. Fiat\u2013Shamir with commitment-only hashing is insecure for multi-message signing\n\n`FSAccepts` hashes `(pub, a, m)`.  Conjecture: if the message is dropped from the hash input\n(oracle of the form `H : G \u00d7 G \u2192 ZMod q`), then there is an explicit adversary that, given a\nsingle valid signature on `m\u2080`, produces a valid signature on every message `m`, i.e.\nuniversal forgery with probability `1`; whereas with the message included, the `Q/q` bound of\n`rom_union_bound` continues to hold.  This is a concrete, checkable separation between the\ntwo hashing conventions inside the present formalization.\n",
