    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Rotated Laplacians and periodicity ratios: results and future directions\n\nTwo self-contained Lean files were produced, both building with no `sorry` and\ndepending only on the standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`,\n`Quot.sound`):\n\n* `Computation/RotatedLaplacianPeriodicity.lean`\n* `Computation/RotatedLaplacianQuantization.lean` (imports the first)\n\nThe setting is a finite weighted digraph `w : V \u2192 V \u2192 \u211d` with rotation\n`om \u2208 \u2102`, the rotated Laplacian quadratic form\n`rotEnergy w om x = \u03a3_{u,v} w u v \u2016x v \u2212 om \u00b7 x u\u2016\u00b2` (the Hermitian form of the\nrotated Laplacian `D \u2212 A_om` studied by Lange\u2013Liu\u2013Peyerimhoff\u2013Post), the volume\n`vol w x = \u03a3_v deg(v) \u2016x v\u2016\u00b2`, and the periodicity ratio\n`periodicityRatio w om = inf { rotEnergy/vol : x a nonzero phase vector }`,\nwhich generalizes Trevisan's bipartiteness ratio (the case `p = 2`, where the\nphase vectors are exactly the `0/\u00b11` vectors).\n\n## What was proved\n\n**Structure of the zero set (main characterization).**\nFor a strongly connected, nonnegatively weighted digraph and `p \u2265 1`:\n\n> `exists_zero_energy_phase_iff` \u2014 a unimodular `p`-phase vector of zero rotated\n> energy exists **iff** `p` divides the length of every closed walk.\n\nConsequently the set `{p : \u03b2_p = 0}` is closed under divisors\n(`exists_zero_energy_phase_of_dvd`) and under least common multiples\n(`exists_zero_energy_phase_lcm`), i.e. it is exactly the set of divisors of the\nperiod.\n\n**Disproof of the bold converse.** `not_period_eq_of_zero_energy` shows that a\nvanishing `2`-periodicity ratio does *not* mean the period is `2`: the directed\n`4`-cycle carries a zero-energy `\u00b11` phase vector while all of its closed walks\nhave length divisible by `4`.  Zero ratio detects *divisors* of the period only.\n\n**Reversal invariance.** `rotEnergy_reverse`, `vol_reverse`,\n`phaseRatios_reverse`, `periodicityRatio_reverse`: reversing every arc of a\ndigraph leaves the whole periodicity ratio unchanged (conjugating the phase\nvector).\n\n**Universal upper bound.** `rotEnergy_le_two_mul_vol` / `rotRayleigh_le_two`:\nthe Rayleigh quotient of the rotated Laplacian is always at most `2`.\n\n**Markov chains.** With `w u v = \u03c0 u \u00b7 P u v` for a stationary distribution `\u03c0`:\n\n* `rotEnergy_eq_zero_of_eigenvector` \u2014 a right eigenvector of `P` with\n  *unimodular* eigenvalue has rotated energy exactly `0` (proved through the\n  variance identity `row_energy_eq`, `E\u2016Z \u2212 EZ\u2016\u00b2 = E\u2016Z\u2016\u00b2 \u2212 \u2016EZ\u2016\u00b2`).\n* `eigenvector_of_rotEnergy_eq_zero` \u2014 conversely, if `\u03c0 > 0` then any\n  zero-energy vector is such an eigenvector.\n* `dvd_of_eigenvalue_rotWeight` \u2014 hence a `p`-th root of unity in the spectrum of\n  an irreducible chain forces `p` to divide every closed-walk length.\n\n**Quantization / rigidity (the boldest conjecture, proved).**\n`rotEnergy_eq_zero_or_rootGap_le`: if every nonzero weight is `\u2265 1` and `x` is a\nunimodular `p`-phase vector (`p \u2265 2`), then\n\n> `rotEnergy = 0`  or  `rotEnergy \u2265 rootGap p`,\n\nwhere `rootGap p` is the minimal squared distance from `1` to a nontrivial `p`-th\nroot of unity (`= 4 sin\u00b2(\u03c0/p)`, `rootGap_pos`).  So there is no *strictly nearly\nperiodic* integral digraph below the threshold; `dvd_of_rotEnergy_lt_rootGap`\nturns any sub-threshold energy into exact `p`-periodicity.  Numerics show the\nconstant is attained, hence sharp.  `exists_small_positive_rotEnergy` shows the\nweight hypothesis is necessary: scaling the weights of the `4`-cycle produces\narbitrarily small positive `3`-energies.\n\n## Future directions\n\n1. **The Cheeger-type inequalities.**  Neither direction relating `\u03b2_p` to the\n   smallest eigenvalue `\u03bb_min` of the rotated Laplacian is formalized here (the\n   eigenvalue itself is not yet defined in these files; see item 5).  The easy\n   direction `\u03bb_min \u2264 \u03b2_p` is immediate once `\u03bb_min` is introduced as an infimum\n   of Rayleigh quotients, since phase vectors are particular test vectors; the\n   substantial content of the paper is a Trevisan-style bound `\u03b2_p \u2264 C \u221a\u03bb_min`\n   obtained by rounding.\n   Formalizing the rounding argument (a random threshold on the phase of the\n   eigenvector) is the natural next target, and needs a Lean development of the\n   `p`-phase sweep cut.\n2. **The Louis\u2013Raghavendra\u2013Tetali\u2013Vempala style algorithm.**  Formalizing the\n   randomized algorithm that outputs many nearly-periodic components requires a\n   probabilistic-analysis layer (expectation bounds over random projections) that\n   is not yet present.\n3. **Quantitative rigidity beyond unit weights.**  Replace \"nonzero weights \u2265 1\"\n   by a general lower bound `w_min` and derive the threshold `w_min \u00b7 rootGap p`;\n   then obtain a *normalized* rigidity statement in terms of\n   `\u03b2_p < w_min \u00b7 rootGap p / vol`.\n4. **Exact value of `rootGap`.**  We only prove `rootGap p > 0`; identifying it\n   with `4 sin\u00b2(\u03c0/p)` requires the monotonicity of `cos` on `[0, \u03c0]` applied to\n   the arguments `2\u03c0k/p`, which is routine but was not needed here.\n5. **Spectral side.**  Relate `rotEnergy` to the eigenvalues of the actual\n   matrix `D \u2212 A_om` in `Matrix.IsHermitian` form, and prove\n   `\u03bb_min \u2264 periodicityRatio` as a statement about `Matrix.IsHermitian.eigenvalues`.\n6. **Almost-periodic decompositions.**  Formalize \"many nearly-periodic\n   components\": a partition of the vertex set into parts each with small\n   periodicity ratio, with guarantees in terms of the higher eigenvalues of the\n   rotated Laplacian.\n",
