    "description": "# Future directions\n\nThis project formalises a bridge between three areas that are usually kept apart:\n\n* **combinatorics of permutations** (inversions, adjacent transpositions, sign-reversing\n  involutions),\n* **noncommutative algebra / quantum groups** (Manin's right-quantum relations, the\n  algebra `M_q(2)` and its quantum determinant),\n* **linear algebra** (the determinant and its alternating property).\n\nThe two Lean files are self-contained (they import only Mathlib) and contain no `sorry`.\n\n## What is proved\n\n`Physics/QuantumCayleyDeterminant.lean`\n\n* `cdet` \u2014 the Cayley (column-ordered) determinant over an arbitrary, possibly noncommutative,\n  ring, defined through `List.prod` so that the order of the factors is part of the data.\n* `IsRightQuantum` \u2014 Manin's relations, with `IsRightQuantum.cross'` the unrestricted\n  symmetric form and `IsRightQuantum.submatrix_col` stability under column permutations.\n* `cdet_swap_adjacent_cols` \u2014 an adjacent column swap negates `cdet`.  The proof is a\n  sign-reversing involution `\u03c3 \u21a6 \u03c3\u00b7s` on `Perm (Fin n)`, where the crossing relation is exactly\n  what makes each orbit cancel.\n* `cdet_col_perm` \u2014 the full alternating property `cdet (A \u2218 \u03c4) = sgn \u03c4 \u2022 cdet A`, obtained by\n  induction along the generation of the symmetric group by adjacent transpositions.\n* `cdet_add_self_eq_zero_of_col_eq`, `cdet_eq_zero_of_col_eq` \u2014 two equal columns kill the\n  determinant (in a ring without additive `2`-torsion).\n* `cdet_row_perm` \u2014 the row version, valid with no hypothesis at all.\n* `invCount`, `invCount_mul_swap`, `invCount_eq_zero_iff`, `sign_eq_neg_one_pow_invCount` \u2014\n  the inversion statistic and the identification of the sign homomorphism with the parity of\n  the number of inversions.\n* `qcdet`, `IsQRightQuantum`, `qcdet_swap_adjacent_cols` \u2014 the `q`-deformed theory: an adjacent\n  column swap multiplies the `q`-Cayley determinant by `-q\u207b\u00b9`.  `qcdet_one_eq_cdet` recovers\n  the undeformed statement at `q = 1`.\n* `cdet_eq_det`, `isRightQuantum_of_comm`, `det_col_perm_of_cdet` \u2014 the bridge to Mathlib's\n  commutative determinant, of which the noncommutative theorem is a strict generalisation.\n* Concrete `2 \u00d7 2` evaluations, noncommutative examples, and a verified counterexample showing\n  the right-quantum hypothesis cannot be dropped.\n\n`Physics/QuantumGroupDeterminant.lean`\n\n* `IsQuantumMatrixTwo` \u2014 the defining relations of `M_q(2)` in hypothesis form (any ring that\n  is an algebra over a commutative ring `K`, any invertible `q : K\u02e3`).\n* `qdet_comm_a/b/c/d`, `qdet_central`, `qdet_mem_centralizer` \u2014 the quantum determinant\n  `ad - q\u207b\u00b9 bc` is central, in fact central for the whole subring generated by the entries.\n* `qdet_eq_qCayley` \u2014 the quantum determinant is the `q`-Cayley determinant of `!![a,b;c,d]`.\n* `entries_commute_of_q_eq_one` \u2014 at `q = 1` the relations collapse to commutativity.\n\n## Natural next steps\n\n1. **Full column antisymmetry in the `q`-case.**  For `q \u2260 1` an adjacent swap multiplies\n   `qcdet` by `-q\u207b\u00b9`, but a general column permutation is not simply `(-q\u207b\u00b9)^{\u2113(\u03c4)}` times the\n   original, because the swapped matrix satisfies the relations with `q` replaced by `q\u207b\u00b9` in\n   the affected pair.  Working out the exact cocycle (presumably `(-q\u207b\u00b9)^{inv \u03c4}` after a\n   suitable multiparameter bookkeeping) would give the `q`-analogue of `cdet_col_perm`.\n\n2. **Multiparameter deformations.**  Chan\u2013Pak also treat matrices with a parameter `q_{kl}`\n   attached to each pair of columns.  The involution used here is local (it touches only two\n   adjacent columns), so it should adapt verbatim once the weight of a permutation is defined\n   as a product over inversions of the corresponding `q_{kl}`.\n\n3. **Cayley = Moore determinant.**  The heart of the Chan\u2013Pak paper is a bijective proof that\n   for right-quantum matrices the Cayley determinant coincides with the Moore determinant (and\n   with Valiant's determinant).  Our `cword_split_pair` lemma, which factors a column word\n   around two adjacent positions, is the natural starting point for such word manipulations.\n\n4. **Mahajan\u2013Vinay and algebraic branching programs.**  The paper's polynomial-time algorithm\n   comes from the clow-sequence expansion of the determinant.  Formalising\n   `det A = \u2211_{clow sequences} (-1)^{n+k} weight` (a sign-reversing involution on clow\n   sequences) would be a substantial and independently valuable addition: it connects graph\n   walks with determinants, and combined with item 3 gives the polynomial-size ABP for the\n   Cayley determinant of a right-quantum matrix.\n\n5. **Laplace expansion and Cramer-type identities.**  `cdet` satisfies a cofactor expansion\n   along the first column for *any* matrix; for right-quantum matrices one expects the full\n   set of quantum Cramer identities and a quantum Jacobi ratio theorem.\n\n6. **`sign_eq_neg_one_pow_invCount` for Mathlib.**  The identification of `Equiv.Perm.sign`\n   with the parity of `invCount` is stated here for `Fin n` and proved from scratch; a\n   polished version (together with `\u2211_{\u03c3} q^{inv \u03c3} = [n]_q!`) would be a reasonable Mathlib\n   contribution.\n\n7. **Higher quantum groups.**  Centrality of `det_q` is proved here for `M_q(2)`.  The same\n   hypothesis-style approach should scale to `M_q(n)` once the `q`-Cayley determinant is known\n   to be alternating in both rows and columns.\n",
