    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nEstablished in `Catalog/Pythagorean/EMLBerggrenEnergyNeuron.lean`: the Berggren\n(Barning\u2013Hall) ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples is driven by a\n*single* exponential\u2013logarithmic neuron\n\n    emlNeuron \u03c3 \u03b5 x = log (2 + \u03c3 \u00b7 exp (\u03b5 \u00b7 x))\n\nacting on the node energy `E(a,b,c) = \u00bd\u00b7log((c+a)/(c\u2212a))`, with the three\nbranches corresponding to the parameter pairs `(\u03c3,\u03b5) = (\u22121,\u22121), (1,\u22121), (1,1)`.\nThe neuron's images on the physical range `(0,\u221e)` are exactly the disjoint\nintervals `(0, log 2)`, `(log 2, log 3)`, `(log 3, \u221e)`, which makes descent\n(inverse traversal) an `O(1)` two-comparison decision and shows the tree is\ngenuinely rooted at energy `log 2`.\n\nNatural continuations:\n\n1. **Full primitivity certificate.** We prove that the Euclid-parameter\n   substitutions preserve `0 < n < m`, coprimality and opposite parity. Adding\n   the classical converse \u2014 that `IsCoprime m n` plus opposite parity implies\n   the triple `(m\u00b2\u2212n\u00b2, 2mn, m\u00b2+n\u00b2)` is primitive, and that every primitive\n   triple arises this way \u2014 would upgrade `energy_step` into a statement about\n   *all* primitive triples, and give a bijection between the tree and the free\n   ternary monoid.\n\n2. **Iterated dynamics and equidistribution.** The energy dynamics is a\n   piecewise-M\u00f6bius interval map conjugated by `log`. Studying the invariant\n   measure of the induced map on `(0,\u221e)` (or on `r \u2208 (1,\u221e)`) would quantify how\n   the \"energy spectrum\" of level-`k` nodes distributes as `k \u2192 \u221e`, connecting\n   the tree to Gauss-map / continued-fraction ergodic theory: the inverse\n   branches `r \u21a6 1/(2\u2212r)`, `1/(r\u22122)`, `r\u22122` are exactly continued-fraction-type\n   maps.\n\n3. **Depth from energy.** Because descent is `O(1)`, the depth of a node is the\n   number of energy iterations needed to return to `log 2`. Bounding this by\n   `O(log c)` (via the growth `r \u21a6 r+2` and `r \u21a6 2 \u00b1 1/r`) would give a formal\n   `O(log c)`-time membership/indexing algorithm for the Berggren tree derived\n   purely from the neuron.\n\n4. **Other ternary trees.** The same conjugation trick applies to trees whose\n   generators are M\u00f6bius maps with a common fixed shape `r \u21a6 k \u00b1 1/r`,\n   `r \u21a6 r + k`. Which integer trees (Markov triples, Stern\u2013Brocot, Apollonian\n   circle packings) admit a *single* two-parameter EML neuron, and what\n   invariant plays the role of the energy?\n\n5. **Learnability.** The bridge says the branch parameters `(\u03c3,\u03b5)` live in a\n   two-dimensional space and take only three values. A quantitative separation\n   result \u2014 e.g. a margin bound showing the three branch outputs are separated\n   by at least `min(log 2 \u2212 E, \u2026)` \u2014 would turn the range-partition theorem into\n   a robustness statement for a trained neuron reading noisy energies.\n\n6. **Higher-dimensional analogues.** Pythagorean quadruples and the Cayley\n   quaternion trees have similar matrix generators; identifying the correct\n   scalar energy (if one exists) is the natural next test of how far a\n   single-neuron encoding of a Diophantine tree can reach.\n",
