    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions\n\nConcrete, falsifiable conjectures arising from the results proved this cycle, on top of the\nframework of weak and strong $B_d$-free families, layers, level families and the extremal\nnumbers $\\mathrm{La}$ and $\\mathrm{La}^*$.\n\nNotation: $B_d$ is the Boolean lattice on $d$ atoms, $n$ the size of the ground set,\n$\\mathrm{La}(n,P)$ and $\\mathrm{La}^*(n,P)$ the weak and strong extremal numbers, and\n$\\mathcal L(S)$ the family of all subsets whose size lies in $S$.\n\n## Resolved this cycle (no longer conjectures)\n\n* **Strict monotonicity, qualitative part, in full.** $\\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) < \\mathrm{La}(n,B_{d+1})$\n  for *every* $n \\ge d$ \u2014 previously known only for $n = d+1$ \u2014 and the same for the strong\n  extremal number. Sharpened to an equivalence: strictness holds precisely when $d \\le n$,\n  since for $d > n$ both sides equal $2^n$. Iterating,\n  $\\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) + k \\le \\mathrm{La}(n,B_{d+k})$ whenever $d + k \\le n+1$.\n* **A quantitative version.** $2^n + n\\,\\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) \\le (n+1)\\,\\mathrm{La}(n,B_{d+1})$ for\n  all $n$ and $d$; equivalently, the gain $\\mathrm{La}(n,B_{d+1}) - \\mathrm{La}(n,B_d)$ is at\n  least $(2^n - \\mathrm{La}(n,B_d))/(n+1)$. (The conjectured gain of a full central binomial\n  coefficient remains open; see **F2**.)\n* **The mechanism behind both**: adjoining *any* antichain to a weak $B_d$-free family produces\n  a weak $B_{d+1}$-free family, and likewise for strong copies. The proof constructs, for every\n  antichain $A$ of $B_{d+1}$, an order embedding $B_d \\hookrightarrow B_{d+1}$ avoiding $A$ \u2014\n  adjoin the last atom exactly on an up-set.\n* **Height criteria.** Any family with no chain of $d+1$ sets is weak $B_d$-free \u2014 a\n  generalisation of the layer construction beyond layer families \u2014 and any family whose\n  members realise at most $d$ distinct sizes is weak $B_d$-free, with no completeness or\n  permutation-invariance hypothesis. Combined with the converse, weak $B_d$-freeness is\n  sandwiched between height $\\le d$ and height $\\le 2^d - 1$.\n* **Both height thresholds are sharp**: there is a family of height $d+1$ containing a copy of\n  $B_d$ (for $n \\ge d$), and a weak $B_d$-free family containing a chain of $2^d - 1$ sets\n  (for $2^d \\le n+2$), the latter simply because a copy of $B_d$ needs $2^d$ sets.\n\n## F1. Subadditivity of the free dimension\n\n**Conjecture.** If $\\mathcal F$ is weak $B_d$-free and $\\mathcal G$ is weak $B_e$-free, then\n$\\mathcal F \\cup \\mathcal G$ is weak $B_{d+e}$-free; likewise for strong freeness.\n\n*Status.* The case $e = 1$ is the Antichain Union Theorem proved this cycle (a weak $B_1$-free\nfamily is exactly an antichain). Exhaustively verified with no counterexample for $n = 3$ and\n$(d,e) \\in \\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}$, and for $n = 4$ with $d = e = 1$. Equivalently, in poset\nform: for every subset $A \\subseteq B_{d+e}$ containing no weak copy of $B_e$, there is an\norder embedding $B_d \\hookrightarrow B_{d+e}$ whose image avoids $A$.\n\n## F2. A full central binomial coefficient of gain\n\n**Conjecture.** $\\mathrm{La}(n,B_{d+1}) - \\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) \\ge \\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$\nfor all $n \\ge d+1$.\n\n*Status.* Open. The pigeonhole bound above gives $2^n/(n+1)$, short by a factor of order\n$\\sqrt n$ exactly in the regime where the extremal number is already a constant times the\ncentral binomial coefficient. For the level-restricted problem the quantitative form is a\ntheorem: the level optima for consecutive $d$ differ by exactly one central binomial\ncoefficient. *Falsifiable* by one pair $(n,d)$ with a smaller gain.\n\n## F3. The headline bound: an $\\varepsilon$-gain for the three-atom cube\n\n**Conjecture.** There are $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $n_0$ such that for all $n \\ge n_0$ there is a\nweak $B_3$-free family $\\mathcal F \\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ with\n$|\\mathcal F| \\ge (3+\\varepsilon)\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$, and likewise a strong\n$B_3$-free family of that size.\n\n*Status.* The framework pins down what has to be beaten: three consecutive layers, and nothing\nelse of a symmetric nature. The improvement cannot come from adding sets to a layer family\n(layer families are maximal: adjoining any set whose size lies outside the window creates a\nstrong copy), and cannot come from a level family or from a permutation-invariant family at\nall. Hence such a construction must break the symmetry of the cube. *Falsifiable*: a proof of\n$\\mathrm{La}(n,B_3) \\le 3\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor} + o\\bigl(\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\bigr)$\nrefutes it.\n\n## F4. Sharpening the chain upper bound from $2^d - 1$ to $O(d)$\n\n**Conjecture.** The factor $2^d - 1$ in the chain bound is far from optimal:\n$\\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) \\le (d+c)\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for an absolute constant $c$\nindependent of $d$ and $n$. Concretely for $d = 3$:\n$\\mathrm{La}(n,B_3) \\le 4\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for all $n$.\n\n*Status.* Open. Proved: $\\le 7\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for $d = 3$, and the exact value\n$\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for $d = 1$. *Falsifiable* by a construction beating\n$4\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for some $n$; F3 only asks for $3+\\varepsilon$, so F3 and F4\nare compatible.\n\n## F5. Weak and strong extremal numbers differ\n\n**Conjecture.** For every $d \\ge 2$ there is $n_0$ with\n$\\mathrm{La}(n,B_d) < \\mathrm{La}^*(n,B_d)$ for all $n \\ge n_0$: the proved inequality\n$\\mathrm{La} \\le \\mathrm{La}^*$ is eventually strict, even though the layer invariants coincide.\n\n*Status.* Open; the first cases go the other way. Equality is proved for $n = d$, for\n$n = d+1$, for all $n$ when $d = 1$, and on level families for all $n$ and $d$. So any such\n$n_0$ must exceed $d+1$, and the separating families cannot be level families. *Falsifiable*\nby showing that some extremal strong $B_d$-free family is always weak $B_d$-free.\n\n## F6. Stability: an $\\varepsilon$-gain forces a large distance from every level family\n\n**Conjecture.** There is an absolute constant $c > 0$ such that for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ and\nall large $n$, every weak $B_3$-free family $\\mathcal F \\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ with\n$|\\mathcal F| \\ge (3+\\varepsilon)\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ satisfies\n$\\min_S |\\mathcal F \\,\\triangle\\, \\mathcal L(S)| \\ge c\\,\\varepsilon\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$,\nthe minimum being over all sets $S$ of levels. More generally the same should hold with\n$\\mathcal L(S)$ replaced by any permutation-invariant family.\n\n*Status.* The degenerate case $\\mathcal F = \\mathcal L(S)$ is a theorem (a level family never\nexceeds the central-layer bound), so the conjecture is precisely a robust, quantitative version\nof it: an $\\varepsilon$-gain must not merely break the symmetry of the cube, it must break it on\na positive proportion of the middle layer. *Falsifiable* by a weak $B_3$-free family of size\n$(3+\\varepsilon)\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ that differs from some level family in\n$o\\bigl(\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\bigr)$ sets.\n\n*Remark (a related claim we deliberately do not conjecture).* One might guess that no gain is\npossible from families of *bounded width*, i.e. contained in $d+t$ consecutive layers for\nconstant $t$. Since the known $\\varepsilon$-constructions for $B_d$ with $d \\ge 4$ appear to\nlive on a bounded number of layers, we expect that guess to be false, and record it only as a\nquestion: for which $t$ is the maximum size of a weak $B_d$-free family inside $d+t$\nconsecutive layers equal to the sum of the $d$ largest of those binomial coefficients? The case\n$t = 0$ is the layer construction; the case \"$t = 1$, one extra set\" is the maximality of layer\nfamilies.\n",
    "domains": [
      "Combinatorics",
      "Cryptography"
    ],
