    "description": "# Future directions\n\nConcrete, falsifiable conjectures suggested by the formalization in\n`Catalog/Logic/ConstructiveAnalysis/` (files `BishopReals.lean`,\n`ConstructiveIVT.lean`, `BrouwerianCounterexamples.lean`, `ComputableReals.lean`,\n`ConstructiveOrder.lean`, `ConstructiveSup.lean`, `Sharpness.lean`, `RootLocation.lean`).  Each is stated\nso that a single Lean proof (or a single counterexample) settles it.\n\nFive earlier conjectures of this list have since been settled inside the project;\nthey are recorded, with the theorem that settles them, in the last section.\n\n## C6. `k` oracle calls per step cannot beat the ratio `1/(k+1)`\n\n`Bishop.one_query_contraction_gt_half` and\n`Bishop.exists_one_query_contraction_lt_half_add` show that the optimal contraction\nratio of a *one-query* located search is the infimum `1/2`, never attained.  The\nnatural generalization queries the locatedness oracle `k` times per step, at the\npairs `(p + \u03b1_i (q\u2212p), p + \u03b2_i (q\u2212p))` with `\u03b1_1 < \u03b2_1 \u2264 \u03b1_2 < \u03b2_2 \u2264 \u2026 < \u03b2_k`, keeping\non the answers the smallest enclosure they certify.\n\n> **Conjecture.** The worst-case contraction factor of any such `k`-query scheme is\n> `> 1/(k+1)`, and for every `\u03b7 > 0` some `k`-query scheme achieves a factor\n> `< 1/(k+1) + \u03b7`.  In particular the per-oracle-call efficiency\n> `(contraction)^(1/k)` tends to `0` as `k \u2192 \u221e`, i.e. batching queries strictly pays.\n\nFalsifiable: a `k`-query scheme (for a concrete `k`, e.g. `k = 2`) that provably\npreserves `Bishop.Enclosing` and contracts by a factor `\u2264 1/(k+1)` refutes the first\nhalf; a proof that no `2`-query scheme beats, say, `2/5` refutes the second.\n\n## C7. The cotransitivity threshold of `lt_cotrans` is exactly `6`\n\n`Bishop.Reg.lt_cotrans` decides `x < z` or `z < y` at any index `m` with\n`1/(m+1) \u2264 gapAt x y n / 8`; the arithmetic of the proof only needs\n`1/(m+1) < gapAt x y n / 6`.\n\n> **Conjecture.** `6` is optimal: the hypothesis `1/(m+1) \u2264 gapAt x y n / \u03ba` with\n> `\u03ba < 6` is insufficient \u2014 there are `x, y, z` and indices `n, m` satisfying it for\n> which the midpoint test at `m` decides neither `x < z` nor `z < y` \u2014 while the\n> conclusion of `lt_cotrans` does hold under `1/(m+1) < gapAt x y n / 6`.\n\nFalsifiable: a Lean proof of `lt_cotrans` with a constant `\u03ba < 6` refutes the first\nhalf; an explicit triple defeating the constant `6` refutes the second.\n\n## C8. The canonical bound in `Reg.mul` can be lowered to `\u2308|x\u2080|\u2309 + 1`\n\n`Bishop.Reg.bound x = \u2308|x.approx 0|\u2309 + 2` is used to choose the index shift in the\ndefinition of multiplication.  Since `|x_n \u2212 x_0| \u2264 1/(n+1) + 1 \u2264 2`, with strict\ninequality for `n \u2265 1`, only `n = 0` is at issue.\n\n> **Conjecture.** Replacing `bound` by `\u2308|x.approx 0|\u2309 + 1` still yields a regular\n> sequence in `Reg.mul`, and `toReal_mul` continues to hold; the constant `1` is then\n> optimal, i.e. `\u2308|x.approx 0|\u2309` alone fails for some pair of Bishop reals.\n\nFalsifiable: a pair `x, y` for which the sequence defined with `\u2308|x.approx 0|\u2309 + 1`\nviolates the regularity inequality refutes the first half; a proof of regularity with\n`\u2308|x.approx 0|\u2309` refutes the second.\n\n## C9. Local non-constancy with modulus `\u03bd` gives root location only through brackets\n\n`Bishop.exists_grid_near_root` locates a genuine root within one mesh, using only the\n*sign change* of `f` across a grid cell, and `Bishop.local_nonconstancy_insufficient`\nshows a bound on `|f x|` alone cannot locate a root even under local non-constancy.\nWhat remains open is the intermediate, quantitative statement.\n\n> **Conjecture.** If `f` has modulus `\u03c9` on `[a,b]`, `f a \u2264 0 \u2264 f b`, and satisfies\n> local non-constancy with modulus `\u03bd`, then *every* root of `f` in `[a,b]` is within\n> `2 (b\u2212a)/N` of a grid point `k` of the sign-change search of `Bishop.\n> exists_grid_near_root`, provided the mesh satisfies `(b\u2212a)/N \u2264 \u03c9 (\u03bd \u03b4)`; and the\n> number of roots of `f` in `[a,b]` is at most `\u2308(b\u2212a)/\u03b4\u2309` whenever `\u03bd \u03b4 > 0`.\n\nFalsifiable: a function with modulus `\u03c9` and local non-constancy modulus `\u03bd` having\ntwo roots closer than `\u03b4` with `\u03bd \u03b4 > 0` refutes the second half; a root far from\nevery bracketing grid point refutes the first.\n\n## Settled inside the project\n\n* **C1 (root modulus `\u03b5/c` is sharp) \u2014 proved.**  `Bishop.root_modulus_attained`\n  exhibits, for every `c, \u03b5 > 0` with `\u03b5/c \u2264 1`, a function with modulus of uniform\n  continuity and slope bound `c` having an `\u03b5`-approximate root at distance *exactly*\n  `\u03b5/c` from the exact root; `Bishop.no_root_modulus_below_one` shows no constant\n  `\u03ba < 1` can replace the factor `1`.\n* **C2 (the diagonal shift is necessary) \u2014 proved.**\n  `Bishop.Reg.unshifted_diagonal_not_regular` exhibits a regular sequence of Bishop\n  reals (`Bishop.Reg.diagWitness`, denoting `1/(k+1)` with `k`-alternating error\n  signs) whose unshifted diagonal violates regularity already at `m = 0, n = 1`\n  (difference `2` against the allowed `3/2`).  For the same family the shifted\n  diagonal converges to the correct limit `0`\n  (`Bishop.Reg.diagWitness_limit_toReal`).\n* **C3 (the ratio `2/3` is optimal for one-query located searches) \u2014 refuted.**  The\n  general one-query scheme is formalized as `Bishop.searchGen`; it preserves the\n  enclosure invariant (`Bishop.searchGen_enclosing`) and contracts by\n  `max \u03b2 (1\u2212\u03b1)` (`Bishop.searchGen_width_le`).  `Bishop.trisection_not_optimal`\n  instantiates `\u03b1 = 2/5, \u03b2 = 1/2`, contracting by `3/5 < 2/3`.  The corrected sharp\n  statement is `Bishop.one_query_contraction_gt_half` (every one-query ratio exceeds\n  `1/2`) together with `Bishop.exists_one_query_contraction_lt_half_add` (ratios\n  arbitrarily close to `1/2` occur), so `1/2` is the unattained infimum.\n* **C5 (every shelf root selector oscillates by at least `1` at `0`) \u2014 proved.**\n  `Bishop.shelf_selector_oscillation_ge_one` shows that for *every* function `r`\n  choosing a root of `shelf t` for each `t \u2208 [-1,1]` and every `\u03b7 > 0`, the difference\n  `sSup (r '' {|t| \u2264 \u03b7}) - sInf (r '' {|t| \u2264 \u03b7})` is at least `1`; the roots are `1`\n  for `t > 0` (`Bishop.shelf_root_of_pos`) and `2` for `t < 0`\n  (`Bishop.shelf_root_of_neg`), and every root lies in `[1,2]`\n  (`Bishop.shelf_root_mem_Icc`).  Selectors do exist classically\n  (`Bishop.exists_shelf_root_selector`), so the statement is not vacuous.\n* **C4 (Bishop's local non-constancy suffices, with an explicit modulus) \u2014 refuted in\n  the \"small value \u27f9 small distance\" reading.**\n  `Bishop.local_nonconstancy_insufficient` exhibits, for every `\u03b4 \u2208 (0,2)`, a\n  `1`-Lipschitz function on `[0,4]` with a unique root, satisfying local\n  non-constancy with the explicit modulus `\u03bd h = h/8`, and a point at distance `2`\n  from the root where `|f| \u2264 \u03bd \u03b4 / 2`.  What does survive without any non-degeneracy\n  hypothesis is the bracketing statement `Bishop.exists_grid_near_root`: the\n  sign-change grid search returns a grid point within one mesh `(b\u2212a)/N` of a genuine\n  root.\n",
