    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nThis file continues the research thread on the Chebotarev geodesic theorem in the non-split\ncase.  It first records the fate of the five conjectures C1\u2013C5 of the previous cycle \u2014 all five\nare now **theorems** in `Catalog/Shared/ChebotarevGeodesic*.lean` (C5 for abelian Galois\ngroups) \u2014 and then states five new falsifiable conjectures D1\u2013D5 for the next cycle.\n\n---\n\n## Status of the previous cycle's conjectures\n\n| | Conjecture | Status | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | The joint optimal exponent drops along a quotient `G \u21a0 H`, strictly in general | **Proved** (both halves) | `ChebotarevGeodesicQuotientExponent.lean`: `jointOptimalExponent_pushforward_le`, `chebotarev_exponent_can_be_strictly_worse` |\n| C2 | Exponent `0` is characteristic of finite superpositions of single-torus counts | **Proved** | `ChebotarevGeodesicIntegrality.lean`: `optimalExponent_torusFamily` |\n| C3 | The Linnik-type threshold `(2C/c)^{2/(\u03b2\u2212\u03b8)}` is optimal | **Proved** | `ChebotarevGeodesicThresholdSharp.lean`: `effective_threshold_sharp` |\n| C4 | Geodesics in every window `[x, x+x^{\u03b3}]` exactly for `\u03b3 > 1 \u2212 (\u03b2 \u2212 \u03b8)` | **Proved** (both halves) | `ChebotarevGeodesicGaps.lean`: `eventually_lt_of_additive_window`, `sparseCount_critical_window_exponent` |\n| C5 | The character table is an invertible transfer matrix, so non-split \u27fa split | **Proved for abelian covers** | `ChebotarevGeodesicCharacter.lean`: `charMatrix_left_inverse`, `chebotarev_abelian_character_reduction` |\n\nTwo structural patterns emerged from the proofs and drive the new conjectures below.\n\n* **Integrality versus continuity.** Every obstruction to a *small* exponent found in this\n  cycle is a clash between an integer valued counting function and a continuous main term\n  (`not_hasErrorExponent_of_intValued`), never an analytic obstruction.  The intermediate value\n  theorem replaces all the ad-hoc test sequences used previously.\n* **The exponent lives in the size of the main term, not in the arithmetic.** The critical\n  window exponent, the Linnik threshold and the failure of short gaps for one torus are all\n  decided by comparing two powers of `x`; the arithmetic of the quaternion order enters only\n  through the constants `c`, `C`.\n\n---\n\n## D1. The non-abelian character table transfers exponents with no loss\n\n**Conjecture.** For every finite group `G` the complex matrix `A \u03c7 C = \u03c7(C)` indexed by\nirreducible characters and conjugacy classes admits a left inverse; consequently the twisted\ncounting functions `\u03c0_\u03c7 = \u2211_C \u03c7(C) \u03c0_C` satisfy the estimate with exponent `\u03b8` **iff** every\nclass counting function `\u03c0_C` does \u2014 the non-split Chebotarev geodesic theorem is *equivalent*\nto a finite set of split estimates, with the same exponent.\n\n**The key insight is** that the exponent half of this statement is already index-type agnostic:\n`exponentC_of_inverse_transform` is stated for a rectangular complex transform with a left\ninverse, and the abelian case is closed by feeding it the orthogonality relation\n`\u2211_\u03c8 \u03c8(a) = |\u03b1|\u00b7[a = 0]`.  Only the invertibility of the general character table is missing.\n\n**Why now?** `chebotarev_abelian_character_reduction` is a complete proof of the pattern; the\nsecond orthogonality relation for irreducible characters is the single missing lemma, and it is\na statement about a finite square matrix, not about geodesics.\n\n---\n\n## D2. The critical window exponent is `1 \u2212 (\u03b2 \u2212 \u03b8)` for every `\u03b2`, including logarithmic\nmain terms\n\n**Conjecture.** `sparseCount_critical_window_exponent` establishes the critical window exponent\n`1 \u2212 (\u03b2 \u2212 \u03b8)` for `\u03b2 = 1`.  The same threshold is critical for every `\u03b2 \u2265 1`, and \u2014 with the\nmain term `li x` rather than `c\u00b7x` \u2014 the critical exponent is unchanged, i.e. logarithmic\nfactors in the main term do not move it.\n\n**The key insight is** that the `\u03b4`-sparse counter `\u230ax^\u03b4\u230b^{1/\u03b4}` is the `\u03b2 = 1` member of a\nfamily: composing it with `x \u21a6 x^{\u03b2}` produces a counting function with main term `x^\u03b2` whose\njumps are spaced `\u224d x^{\u03b8}` apart, so the extremal example transports; conversely the positive\nhalf already holds for all `\u03b2 \u2265 1`.\n\n**Why now?** Both halves are proved for `\u03b2 = 1` with explicit constants, and\n`rpow_bernoulli_add_le` / `rpow_sub_bernoulli_le` give two-sided control of `(x+h)^p \u2212 x^p`,\nwhich is all the composition argument needs.\n\n---\n\n## D3. A Lambert-`W` shape for the least geodesic with a `li`-type main term\n\n**Conjecture.** With main term `li x \u224d x/log x` instead of `c x^\u03b2`, the effective threshold of\n`effective_lower_bound` becomes the solution of a transcendental crossing equation, and the\nleast geodesic in a class of density `\u03b4_C` is\n`\u224d exp(W((2C/\u03b4_C)^{...}))`-shaped rather than a pure power; in particular the exponent\n`2/(\u03b2 \u2212 \u03b8)` of `effective_threshold_sharp` is replaced by a strictly larger effective exponent\nfor every fixed range of `x`.\n\n**The key insight is** that `threshold_rpow` isolates the entire content of the power case in\none identity `T^{(\u03b2\u2212\u03b8)/2} = 2C/c`; replacing the main term by `x/log x` replaces it by\n`T^{(\u03b2\u2212\u03b8)/2} = (2C/c)\u00b7log T`, which is exactly a Lambert-`W` equation.\n\n**Why now?** The sharp power case is proved (both the threshold and its optimality), so the\ncomparison is a clean one-variable analysis, and Mathlib has the `Real.log`/`Real.exp` API\nneeded for the crossing estimate.\n\n---\n\n## D4. Gaps of an infinite family of tori shrink at rate `exp(1/c)`\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `\u03b5_1, \u03b5_2, \u2026` be the fundamental units of a family of non-split tori and\nput `c_N = \u2211_{i \u2264 N} 1/(2 log \u03b5_i)`.  Then the largest multiplicative gap of the total counting\nfunction of the first `N` tori is `exp(1/c_N)(1 + o(1))`, and it is *attained* infinitely often;\nconsequently the family has geodesics in every window `[x, \u03bbx]` with `\u03bb > exp(1/c_N)` and in no\nsmaller one.\n\n**The key insight is** that for a single torus the answer is exactly `\u03b5\u00b2`\n(`torusCount_mul_sq`, `torusCount_no_short_gaps`), i.e. `exp(1/c_1)`, and that the jump points\nof the family are the union of `N` geometric progressions, so the gap statistics are a\nthree-distance problem for `N` incommensurable frequencies.\n\n**Why now?** `torusCount_eq_of_mem_Ico` pins the counting function on each gap exactly, and the\nintegrality obstruction (`not_hasErrorExponent_of_intValued`) already provides the mechanism\nthat converts gap information into exponent information.\n\n---\n\n## D5. The positive exponent is a class-number growth exponent\n\n**Conjecture.** Write `T(x)` for the number of tori (equivalently, of discriminants) with\nfundamental unit `\u03b5` satisfying `\u03b5\u00b2 \u2264 x`.  Then the optimal error exponent of the full\n(infinite) family is a monotone function of the growth exponent of `T`, and equals `0` exactly\nwhen `T` is bounded; in particular the paper's `25/36` is a bound for the growth exponent of\nthe discriminant counting function, not for anything attached to an individual geodesic.\n\n**The key insight is** that `optimalExponent_torusFamily = 0` for **every** finite family, so\nthe positive exponent must be produced in the limit `N \u2192 \u221e`; the only datum that survives the\nlimit is the counting function of the tori themselves.\n\n**Why now?** The finite case is completely settled, and `HasErrorExponent.sum` plus the\nintegrality obstruction give matching upper and lower bounds for each truncation, so the whole\nquestion is the uniformity of the constants in `N`.\n",
