    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## What is now settled\n\nThe previous cycle left six open directions. Five of them are now proved\noutright, and the sixth is settled in part together with an explicit\nobstruction that explains what the remaining part must look like.\n\n| previous direction | status | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. tighten the certified interval to `[6/5, 5/4]` | **proved** | the unique positive root of `|log(1 + t i)| = 1` lies in `[6/5, 5/4]`, since the radius is strictly below `1` at `6/5` and strictly above `1` at `5/4` |\n| 2. strict monotonicity of `t \u21a6 |log(1 + t i)|`, hence uniqueness | **proved** | the radius is strictly increasing on `[0, \u221e)`, so there is exactly one positive solution |\n| 3. lift the scalar factor to matrix C\u22c6-algebras | **proved** | a unimodular scalar times the unit of any complex star algebra is unitary, so the certified root gives a unitary scalar matrix in every dimension |\n| 4. exponential surjectivity onto unitaries | **proved** | every unitary with finite spectrum in a unital C\u22c6-algebra is `exp(i x)` for a self-adjoint `x`; in particular every unitary matrix is `exp(i H)` with `H` Hermitian |\n| 5. determinants and traces towards `SU(2)` | **partly proved, with an obstruction** | every unitary matrix factors as a unimodular scalar times a determinant-one unitary, but the scalar logarithmic factor `log(1 + t i) \u00b7 I` is never special unitary in dimension two |\n| 6. polar-normalized logarithmic factor | **proved** | `log(1 + t i) / |log(1 + t i)|` is unitary for every `t \u2260 0`, not merely at the certified root |\n\nA bonus result generalises the whole unit-circle statement: the radius map\n`t \u21a6 |log(1 + t i)|` is a bijection of `[0, \u221e)` onto itself, so *every* circle \u2014\nnot just the unit circle \u2014 is met exactly once by the logarithm along the line\n`1 + t i`.\n\n## Next-cycle conjectures\n\n### C1. The determinant\u2013trace bridge: `det (exp A) = exp (tr A)`\n\n**Conjecture.** For every complex square matrix `A`, `det (exp A) = exp (tr A)`;\nconsequently a Hermitian `H` satisfies `exp (i H) \u2208 SU(n)` if and only if\n`tr H \u2208 2\u03c0 \u2124`, which is the exact trace congruence class asked for in\ndirection 5.\n\n*The key insight is* that the missing ingredient is not the determinant\nidentity itself but a **norm-independence lemma for the exponential on a\nfinite-dimensional algebra**: the matrix exponential is usually developed\nagainst the `L^\u221e` operator norm, while the C\u22c6-structure \u2014 and hence the\ncontinuous functional calculus used for exponential surjectivity \u2014 lives on the\n`L^2` operator norm. The two exponentials are the same function but are not the\nsame object as written. Establishing that the exponential depends only on the\ntopological ring structure unlocks both frameworks simultaneously.\n\n*Why now?* This cycle produced the first statement that mixes the two worlds \u2014\nthe matrix corollary of exponential surjectivity is stated in the `L\u00b2` setting \u2014\nso the mismatch is now an explicit, isolated, provable lemma rather than a\ndiffuse inconvenience.\n\n### C2. Sharp two-sided algebraic bounds for the scalar root\n\n**Conjecture.** The unique positive root `t*` of `|log(1 + t i)| = 1` satisfies\n`t* \u2208 [1.229, 1.2291]`, and `t*` is transcendental.\n\n*The key insight is* that `t*` is the unique positive solution of the\ntranscendental equation\n`(log(1 + t\u00b2))\u00b2 / 4 + arctan(t)\u00b2 = 1`,\nwhich couples the real and imaginary parts of a single complex logarithm. The\ninterval refinement is within reach of the present method \u2014 the same reduction\nidentities with higher-precision certified enclosures of `log 2` and `\u03c0` \u2014 while\ntranscendence would presumably require a Schanuel-type input; even irrationality\nappears open.\n\n### C3. Further structural targets\n\n3. Extend exponential surjectivity beyond finite spectrum: the present proof\n   already covers every unitary whose spectrum omits at least one point of the\n   circle, and spectral measures should remove the restriction entirely.\n4. Develop the operator-valued analogue: replace the line `1 + t i` by\n   `1 + t N` for a normal or nilpotent operator `N` and characterise when\n   `\u2016log (1 + t N)\u2016 = 1` in operator norm.\n5. Characterise the image of the polar-normalized factor\n   `{ log(1 + t i) / |log(1 + t i)| : t > 0 }` as an explicit subarc of the unit\n   circle, with endpoints identified.\n",
