    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions\n\nFive falsifiable conjectures distilled from what survived (and what failed) in this cycle.\nThroughout, `F` is a finite field with `q = #F` elements and odd characteristic,\n`\u03c7 = quadraticChar F`, and for `a b : F`\n\n* `cardPoints a b` = number of points of `y\u00b2 = x\u00b3 + a x + b` (affine plus infinity),\n* `a(a,b) = frobTrace a b = q + 1 - cardPoints a b`,\n* `S(a,b) = charSum a b = \u2211_x \u03c7(x\u00b3+ax+b) = -a(a,b)`.\n\nWhat is already **proved** here: the counting formula, the parity/2-torsion criterion,\n`0/1/3` root dichotomy, the two supersingular families, twisting, the first moment\n`\u2211 a(a,b) = 0`, the exact second moment `\u2211_{a,b} a(a,b)\u00b2 = q\u00b3 - q\u00b2`, the exact *vertical*\nmoments, the bridge \"cubing bijective \u27fa \u03c7(-3) = -1\", and supplementary reciprocity for\n`-3` over `F_p`.  What resisted: any *pointwise* bound on `a(a,b)` \u2014 i.e. Hasse itself.\nThat gap shapes the conjectures below.\n\n---\n\n## C1. The fourth moment is exactly `2q\u2075 - 3q\u2074 + O(q\u00b3)` with an explicit closed form\n\n**Conjecture.** There are integers `c\u2083, c\u2082` (depending only on `\u03c7(-3)` and `\u03c7(-1)`) with\n\n`\u2211_{a,b \u2208 F} a(a,b)\u2074 = 2q\u2075 - 3q\u2074 + c\u2083 q\u00b3 + c\u2082 q\u00b2`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the fourth moment reduces, exactly as the second moment did in\n`sum_char_shift_pair`, to the `b`-sum `\u2211_b \u03c7((b+u\u2081)(b+u\u2082)(b+u\u2083)(b+u\u2084))`, and that this sum\nis *elementary* whenever the `u\u1d62` collide in pairs \u2014 the genuinely quartic case is the only\nWeil-type input, and it is governed by a single hyperelliptic curve of genus 1 whose own\ncount is again in the family.  So the fourth moment should be self-referential and\ncomputable by the same collision bookkeeping as `collisions_formula`.\n\n**Why now?** The second-moment proof in `EllipticSecondMoment.lean` already contains the\nentire machinery (`sum_char_quadratic`, collision counts by inclusion\u2013exclusion); only the\ndegree-4 shift sum is missing, and the numerical table for `q = 5, 7, 11, 13` can pin the\nconstants `c\u2083, c\u2082` before a line of Lean is written. A verified fourth moment would give\n`#{(a,b) : |a| \u2265 \u03bb\u221aq} = O(q\u00b2/\u03bb\u2074)`, a genuine improvement on\n`card_large_frobTrace_le`.\n\n## C2. Hasse's bound follows from the moments plus twist symmetry\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `a, b` with `disc a b \u2260 0`, `a(a,b)\u00b2 \u2264 4q`, and this is provable\nfrom (i) the exact second moment, (ii) `frobTrace_twist`, and (iii) the multiplicativity of\nthe count under base change `F \u2192 F_{q^n}` \u2014 with no Riemann\u2013Roch and no Jacobian.\n\n**The key insight is** that `a(a,b)\u00b2 \u2264 4q` is equivalent to the positivity of the quadratic\nform `n \u21a6 q^n + 1 - a_n` on all extensions, and `a_n` satisfies the Newton recursion\n`a_{n+1} = a\u2081a_n - q a_{n-1}` that can be *derived* from moment identities over each\n`F_{q^n}` rather than from the Frobenius eigenvalue picture.\n\n**Why now?** `EllipticPointCount.lean` is stated for an arbitrary finite field, not just a\nprime field, so the base-change tower is already expressible; and `hasse_F5 \u2026 hasse_F13`\nshow the bound holds exhaustively in the accessible range, so a counterexample search is\nalready closed off for small `q`.\n\n## C3. The vertical distribution of `\u03c7(-a/3)` forces an exact \"vertical Sato\u2013Tate defect\"\n\n**Conjecture.** For fixed `a \u2260 0` the normalised traces `a(a,b)/\u221aq`, `b \u2208 F`, have\n*third* vertical moment `\u2211_b a(a,b)\u00b3 = q \u00b7 T(a)` where `T(a)` is again an elliptic\npoint count \u2014 explicitly, `T(a) = -(#E_a(F) - q - 1)` for `E_a : y\u00b2 = x\u00b3 + a x`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the cube of the character sum sums over triples `(x,y,z)` and\nthe `b`-sum of `\u03c7((b+u)(b+v)(b+w))` is exactly the count of an elliptic curve, so the odd\nmoments of the family close up on the family itself: the vertical moment hierarchy is a\n*fixed point*, not an expansion.\n\n**Why now?** `vertical_second_moment` gives the even case in closed form and shows the\nanswer depends only on `\u03c7(-3)` and `\u03c7(-a/3)`; the odd case is the first place where the\nelementary method must produce an elliptic count rather than a constant, which makes the\nconjecture sharply falsifiable by a single `#eval` over `F_13`.\n\n## C4. A purely combinatorial criterion for supersingularity in every residue class\n\n**Conjecture.** For each `m \u2265 2` there is a finite \"collision signature\" `\u03c3_m(a) \u2208 \u2124^m`,\ncomputable from the counts `#{(x,y) : x^m + a x = y^m + a y}`, such that the curve\n`y\u00b2 = x\u00b3 + a x + b` is supersingular for all `b` iff `\u03c3\u2083(a)` takes one of two explicit\nvalues, generalising `cube_bijective_iff_char_neg_three`.\n\n**The key insight is** that supersingularity of a whole *line* in the family is equivalent\nto the vanishing of one vertical second moment, and that this moment is a linear function\nof a collision count \u2014 a combinatorial quantity that never mentions the curve.\n\n**Why now?** The equivalence \"cubing bijective \u27fa `\u03c7(-3) = -1`\", proved here from a\ncollision count alone, is the `m = 3` case, and it already produced supplementary quadratic\nreciprocity for `-3` as a corollary; the same argument shape should run for `x^m`.\n\n## C5. Extremal traces are attained on the diagonal `b = a`\n\n**Conjecture.** `max_{a,b} a(a,b)\u00b2` over `F_p` is attained at some pair with `b\u00b2 = a\u00b3`\n(equivalently `j = 0` or `j = 1728` up to twist) for infinitely many `p`, and never\nexceeds `4p - 3`.\n\n**The key insight is** that `exists_frobTrace_sq_ge` gives the lower bound `q - 1` for the\nmaximum, while the curves with extra automorphisms are exactly the ones whose traces are\nconstrained by the twist involution `frobTrace_twist` to lie in a thin set \u2014 so extremal\nbehaviour and extra symmetry should coincide.\n\n**Why now?** Both endpoints are now formal: the existence of a large trace is proved from\nthe second moment, and twisting is proved to negate the trace, so the conjecture is a\nstatement about the interaction of two theorems already in the library, testable by\nexhaustive kernel computation for `p \u2264 40`.\n",
