    "description": "# Future directions\n\nDerived from the formalization in `Catalog/Pythagorean/SchurIdempotentGammaTwo.lean`, `SchurIdempotentGap.lean`,\n`SchurIdempotentAlgebra.lean` and `SchurIdempotentEQCost.lean`.\n\nWhat is already machine-checked (0 sorries):\n\n* `isBlowUp_iff_gammaTwoLE_one` / `contractive_idempotent_iff_isBlowUp` \u2014 a boolean matrix\n  has `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 1` iff it is a blow-up of a partial identity matrix (the `L = 1` case of\n  the conjecture; Livshits' description of contractive idempotent Schur multipliers).\n* `gammaTwoLE_of_signedSum` \u2014 the easy direction: a signed sum of `L` blow-ups has\n  `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 L`.\n* `gammaTwoLE_tri2_iff` \u2014 `\u2016[[1,1],[1,0]]\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} = 2\u221a3/3` exactly.\n* `gammaTwo_gap` \u2014 no boolean matrix has `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082}` strictly between `1` and `2\u221a3/3`.\n* `GammaTwoLE.hadamard`, `IsBlowUp.hadamard`, `IsSignedSumOfBlowUps.{add,neg,hadamard,\n  complement}` \u2014 submultiplicativity and closure of the class of signed sums of blow-ups.\n* `eqCost` \u2014 the blow-up number, with `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 eqCost A \u2264 m`, `eqCost A \u2264 1 \u2194 A` is a\n  blow-up, and `idempotentSchurConjecture_below_gap`: the conjecture holds with `L = 1`,\n  uniformly in the matrix size, for every `\u03b3 < 2\u221a3/3`.\n\n## Conjecture 1 (second gap). The set of `\u03b3\u2082`-norms of boolean matrices is discrete near its bottom: after `1` and `2\u221a3/3` the next attained value is `\u03b3\u2082(T\u2083)`, where `T\u2083 = [[1,1,1],[1,1,0],[1,0,0]]`, and no boolean matrix has norm strictly between `2\u221a3/3` and `\u03b3\u2082(T\u2083)`.\n\n(Crude numerical searches put `\u03b3\u2082(T\u2083)` somewhere in the range `1.40`\u2013`1.48`; the two searches\ndisagreed, so no numeric value is claimed \u2014 pinning it down exactly is part of the\nconjecture.)\n\nThe key insight is that the gap proof reduces to a *pattern-containment* statement: a boolean\nmatrix of norm `< 2\u221a3/3` contains no `2`-staircase, and the same forbidden-pattern reduction\nshould turn \"norm `< \u03b3\u2082(T\u2083)`\" into \"contains no `3`-staircase\", after which the finitely many\nstaircase-free structures can be classified exactly as `RowRigid` classifies the\nstaircase-free case. Why now? The `2`-staircase case is fully formalized here, both the\nprimal factorization and the dual sum-of-squares certificate; the same two-sided scheme\n(explicit `k`-dimensional vector configuration versus an explicit SOS certificate coming from\nthe dual SDP) transfers verbatim to `T\u2083`, whose SDP has only six free Gram parameters.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (staircase growth is the only obstruction). For boolean `A`, `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 \u03b3` implies that `A` contains no staircase of length `k > 2^{O(\u03b3\u00b2)}`, and conversely a boolean matrix with no staircase of length `k` satisfies `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 f(k)` for an explicit `f`.\n\nThe key insight is that `RowRigid` \u2014 the condition characterizing `\u03b3\u2082 \u2264 1` \u2014 is exactly\n\"no staircase of length `2`\", so the whole conjecture on idempotent Schur multipliers may be\na statement about a forbidden-pattern hierarchy rather than about norms. Why now? The `k = 2`\ninstance is proved in both directions in this project (`isBlowUp_iff_rowRigid` and\n`gammaTwo_ge_of_triPattern`), giving a template for the induction, and the \"no staircase\"\nhypothesis is a decidable combinatorial condition that can be tested exhaustively on small\nmatrices before any proof attempt.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (degree of a boolean matrix). Define `L(A)` as the least number of blow-ups in a signed decomposition. Then `L(A) \u2264 2^{O(\u03b3\u2082(A)^c)}` with **no** dependence on the matrix size \u2014 i.e. the `log*` term in the current best bound is removable \u2014 and moreover `L` is submultiplicative: `L(A \u2299 B) \u2264 L(A)\u00b7L(B)`.\n\nThe key insight is that the closure properties proved here (`IsSignedSumOfBlowUps.hadamard`,\n`.add`, `.complement`) already give the submultiplicative half for free, so the whole content\nis the size-free upper bound, and the natural attack is to bound `L` by the number of\nequivalence classes of a rigidity relation defined by *approximate* rather than exact row\nequality. Why now? The exact-equality version of that relation is precisely what the\nformalized `L = 1` proof uses, and quantitative Cauchy\u2013Schwarz (the same\n`\u2211 (x_i \u2212 y_j)\u00b2 \u2264 0` computation, run with a slack term) turns it into an approximate one.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (arithmetic rigidity of the gap constant). The set `\u0393 = {\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} : A boolean}` is contained in the field `\u211a(\u221ad : d \u2208 \u2115)` \u2014 every attained value is an algebraic number of degree `\u2264 2` over `\u211a`, and each is attained by a matrix of size polynomial in the degree of the minimal polynomial.\n\nThe key insight is that the extremal factorizations arise from semidefinite programs with\nrational data whose optimal faces are, in the examples computed so far, one-dimensional,\nforcing quadratic irrationalities such as `2\u221a3/3`. Why now? This project produces the first\nformally verified exact value of a `\u03b3\u2082`-norm of a boolean matrix (`2\u221a3/3`), including a\nmatching primal/dual pair, so the methodology for confirming or refuting further exact values\nis in place.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (equality-oracle complexity, effective form). Every boolean matrix with `\u2016A\u2016_{\u03b3\u2082} \u2264 \u03b3` is computed by a decision list of at most `poly(\u03b3)` equality queries `f(i) = g(j)`, and this is optimal up to polynomial factors.\n\nThe key insight is that a signed sum of `L` blow-ups is literally a `\u00b11`-combination of `L`\nequality tests \u2014 the formal statement `IsSignedSumOfBlowUps` is already a syntactic\ndescription of an equality-oracle protocol \u2014 so the communication-complexity consequence is\nan unfolding of the decomposition once the size-free bound of Conjecture 3 is available. Why\nnow? The translation is formalized (labels are `\u2115`-valued functions of the row and column\nindex only), so only the quantitative step remains.\n",
