    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Singmaster multiplicity in Pascal's triangle\n\nDerived from the verified results in `Catalog/Novelty/`:\n`SingmasterSmoothness.lean`, `SingmasterSharpLog.lean`, `SingmasterSmoothHierarchy.lean`,\n`SingmasterDensity.lean`, `AdjacentBinomialLucas.lean`, `AdjacentBinomialFibonacci.lean`,\n`SingmasterMinimalValues.lean`.\nEach conjecture below is falsifiable: a single counterexample `t` (or `(n,k)`) kills it.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (grand challenge). Singmaster boundedness follows from the smoothness hierarchy\n\n> There is an absolute constant `m\u2080` such that no integer `t \u2265 2` satisfies `mult t \u2265 2m\u2080 + 2`.\n> Concretely: `mult t \u2264 8` for all `t \u2265 2`, with equality only at `t = 3003`.\n\n**The key insight is** that `choose_prime_le_of_mult` converts multiplicity directly into\narithmetic smoothness: `mult t \u2265 2m+2` forces `C(p, m+1) \u2264 t` for *every* prime `p \u2223 t`, so a\nnumber of large multiplicity must be `t^{1/(m+1)}`-smooth; combining this with a lower bound on the\nnumber of prime factors needed to build `t` out of small primes (a Stirling/`\u03a9(t) \u226b log t / log log t`\ncount) should close the gap between \"smooth\" and \"impossible\".\n\nA first quantitative step in this direction is already proved:\n`value_ge_of_mult` gives `t \u2265 C(2m+3, m+1)` whenever `mult t \u2265 2m+2`, and the sharp\nthresholds `min_value_of_six_le_mult` (`t \u2265 120`) and `min_value_of_eight_le_mult`\n(`t \u2265 3003`) show what the true growth looks like at the bottom of the range.\n\n**Why now?** The two halves are already formal: the smoothness side is proved\n(`choose_prime_le_of_mult`, `prime_factor_bound_of_three_le_mult`) and the counting side is\nproved in the aggregate (`card_highMult_le`). What remains is a single quantitative comparison\nbetween the smoothness ceiling and the size of `t`, which is exactly the kind of statement Lean's\n`Nat.log`/`Nat.factorization` API now supports.\n\n**Falsifier:** an explicit `t` with nine or more representations as a binomial coefficient.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2. `mult t = 5` and `mult t = 7` are impossible\n\n> For every `t \u2265 2`, `mult t \u2209 {5, 7}`.\n\n**The key insight is** the parity decomposition already formalized as\n`mult_eq_two_add_two_mul_leftInt`: multiplicity equals `2 + 2\u00b7(number of left-interior\noccurrences)` plus a correction supported only on the central column, and\n`centerOcc_card_le_one` shows that correction is at most one. Odd multiplicity therefore\n*requires* a central binomial coefficient `C(2c, c) = t`, so the conjecture reduces to\n\"no central binomial coefficient has two other non-central occurrences\", a statement about\n`C(2c,c) = C(n,k)` with `k < c`.\n\n**Why now?** The reduction is already a theorem; the residual problem is a Diophantine one of the\nsame shape as `adjacent_iff_luc`, which we solved completely by descent. The same descent template\n(reduce to a Pell-like form, classify solutions by a Lucas/Fibonacci recursion) is now available\nin the catalog and can be re-instantiated.\n\n**Falsifier:** a `t` with exactly five or exactly seven representations.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3. Non-adjacent repetition families are finite\n\n> For each fixed `d \u2265 2`, the equation `C(n, k) = C(n \u2212 d, k + d)` has only finitely many\n> solutions with `1 \u2264 k`, and for `d \u2265 3` it has none at all with `k \u2265 2`.\n\n**The key insight is** that `d = 1` is *not* finite \u2014 `adjacent_iff_luc` shows it is exactly a\nLucas orbit \u2014 precisely because the case `d = 1` linearises into the quadratic form\n`x\u00b2 \u2212 xy \u2212 y\u00b2 = \u00b15`, whose automorphism group is infinite; for `d \u2265 2` the analogous elimination\nproduces a form of degree `\u2265 3` (a Thue equation), whose solution set is finite by rigidity rather\nthan by size.\n\n**Why now?** The `d = 1` classification is complete and formal, including the descent machinery\n(`luc_of_sol`, `dioph_to_form`), so the `d \u2265 2` cases can reuse the same elimination step; and for\n`d \u2265 3` the smoothness bound `prime_factor_bound_of_three_le_mult` already rules out most residues,\nturning an infinite search into a finite one.\n\n**Falsifier:** a solution of `C(n,k) = C(n\u22123,k+3)` with `k \u2265 2`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4. The true counting exponent is `1/3`, not `1/2`\n\n> `#{t \u2264 X : mult t \u2265 3} = X^{1/3 + o(1)}`, and more precisely\n> `#{t \u2264 X : mult t \u2265 4} \u224d X^{1/3}`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the proved bound `card_highMult_le` counts one occurrence per\n*column*, and column `k \u2265 2` contributes only `O(X^{1/k})` values \u2014 so the `X^{1/2}` term comes\nentirely from column 2 (`C(n,2)`), whose values coincide with column-3 values only along a thin\nPell family; discarding the triangular numbers that are *not* also higher-column values should drop\nthe exponent to `X^{1/3}`.\n\n**Why now?** `card_highMult_le` and `exists_interior_occ_of_three_le_mult` already isolate the\n\"one occurrence per column\" mechanism in Lean, so the refinement is a matter of proving that the\ncolumn-2 contribution to multiplicity `\u2265 3` is itself sparse \u2014 a statement about\n`C(n,2) = C(m,3)`, a genuinely finite-type Mordell equation.\n\n**Falsifier:** numerics showing `#{t \u2264 X : mult t \u2265 4}` growing faster than `X^{0.34}`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5. A `log t / log log t` bound\n\n> `mult t \u2264 C \u00b7 log t / log log t` for an absolute constant `C` and all `t \u2265 3`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the current bound `mult t \u2264 log\u2082 t + log\u2082(2 log\u2082 t + 1) + 1`\n(`mult_le_log_add_log_log`) spends one unit of multiplicity per *column*, whereas the smoothness\ntheorem shows that occupying `m` columns forces `t` to be divisible by roughly `m` distinct small\nprimes; the number of admissible prime factorisations below `t` is `t^{o(1)}`, and balancing the\ntwo constraints gives the `log/log log` shape.\n\n**Why now?** Both ingredients exist as theorems here (`mult_le_log_mul` for the column count,\n`choose_prime_le_of_mult` for the arithmetic cost of each column); the missing piece is an\n`\u03a9`-function lower bound of the form `\u03a9(t) \u2264 log\u2082 t`, which is already in Mathlib.\n\n**Falsifier:** a sequence `t_j \u2192 \u221e` with `mult t_j \u00b7 log log t_j / log t_j \u2192 \u221e`.\n\n---\n\n## Three concrete next-cycle sub-conjectures\n\n1. `C(n,2) = C(m,3)` has only the known solutions (`10, 120, 1540, 7140`) \u2014 this is the exact\n   statement needed for Conjecture 4 and is a Mordell curve of rank 1.\n2. Every `t` with `mult t \u2265 6` has at least three distinct prime factors \u2014 consistent with all\n   seven such `t \u2264 10^6` (see `ComputationalEvidence.md`; note `120 = 2^3\u00b73\u00b75` has exactly three,\n   so \"four\" would be false) and implied by a modest strengthening of\n   `descFactorial_three_le_of_six_le_mult`.\n3. If `C(2c, c) = C(n, k)` with `k < c` then `c \u2264 3` \u2014 the central-column rigidity statement that\n   would settle Conjecture 2 outright.\n",
