    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nDerived from the formal development in `Catalog/Novelty/WeightMonodromy*.lean`, which proves:\npurity of a weight grading (weight `= \u03b1 \u00b7` degree, `\u03b1 > 0`) on the cohomology of a bigraded\ndg-algebra implies strict formality (`A \u2287 A' \u21a0 A'/J`, zero differential on the quotient), that\nformality and purity each force triple Massey products to contain `0`, and that a genuinely\nnon-vanishing Massey product obstructs purity. Two explicit bigraded group-algebra examples show\nthe purity hypothesis is satisfiable and not automatic.\n\nEach conjecture below is falsifiable, is stated so that it can be attacked directly in Lean on\ntop of the existing files, and is *not* implied by what is already proved.\n\n---\n\n## C1. Higher Massey products are killed by purity, with an exact excess formula\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `A` be a weight-graded dg-algebra which is `wt`-pure with `wt 1 = \u03b1 > 0`.\nThen for every `n \u2265 3` and every defining system for an `n`-fold Massey product of diagonal\ncocycles `x_1, \u2026, x_n`, the resulting representative is a coboundary; more precisely, every\nentry `a_{i,j}` of a bihomogeneous defining system has weight excess\n`w \u2212 deg = \u03b1 \u00b7 (j \u2212 i)`, and the final representative has excess `\u03b1 (n \u2212 2) > 0`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the weight excess is an additive \"defect counter\": products add it and\neach primitive raises it by exactly `\u03b1`, so the excess of a Massey representative counts the\nnumber of primitives used and can never return to `0`.\n\n*Why now?* The `n = 3` case is fully formalised (`massey_zero_of_weightPure`,\n`massey_zero_of_pureFor`) and its proof is exactly this excess count; what remains is the\ncombinatorics of defining systems and Koszul signs, which is now the only missing ingredient.\nFalsifiable: exhibit a pure weight-graded dg-algebra with a nonzero 4-fold Massey product.\n\n## C2. Purity is detected by the diagonal subalgebra alone\n\n**Conjecture.** A weight-graded dg-algebra `A` is pure if and only if the inclusion of the\ndiagonal cocycle subalgebra `diagAlg \u2286 A` (already constructed, with zero differential) induces\na *surjection* on cohomology in every degree **and** the off-diagonal part of `A` is exact as a\ncomplex in each fixed weight. In particular purity is equivalent to a statement about a single\nstrict subalgebra, with no reference to truncations.\n\n*The key insight is* that `diagAlg \u2192 H(A)` is always multiplicative and its surjectivity is the\nonly place where purity was used in `diagonal_strict_section`; the converse direction should\nrecover weight-wise acyclicity from surjectivity plus a dimension/graded-Nakayama argument.\n\n*Why now?* `diagSubalg_surjective_on_cohomology` supplies one direction verbatim; the converse\nis a finite bookkeeping statement about the same objects. Falsifiable by an example where\n`diagAlg` surjects on cohomology but some off-diagonal weight-complex has cohomology.\n\n## C3. Non-formality of rigid-analytic surfaces has an explicit finite-dimensional witness\n\n**Conjecture.** There is an explicit finite-dimensional bigraded dg-algebra over `Q` \u2014 of\nPoincar\u00e9 type of a surface, with `dim H^1 = 2`, `dim H^2 = 2` \u2014 carrying a triple Massey product\nthat is nonzero for *every* choice of primitives. By `not_weightPure_of_massey` such an algebra\nadmits no pure weight grading, giving a fully formal, self-contained counterpart of the\nnon-formal smooth proper rigid-analytic surfaces.\n\n*The key insight is* that non-formality can be certified purely by a finite linear-algebra\ncomputation (the Massey representative is not in the image of `d` restricted to one graded\npiece), so the certificate is decidable once the multiplication table is fixed.\n\n*Why now?* The obstruction theorem is proved; only the construction of one concrete bigraded\nalgebra instance (a `GradedAlgebra` structure on a finite-dimensional model) is missing, and\nMathlib's `AddMonoidAlgebra.gradeBy` machinery \u2014 used already in\n`Catalog/Novelty/WeightMonodromyExamples.lean` \u2014 gives a template. Falsifiable: a proof that\nevery finite-dimensional bigraded model of a surface with these Betti numbers is formal.\n\n## C4. Purity with `\u03b1 = 0` fails: sharpness of the normalisation hypothesis\n\n**Conjecture.** The hypothesis `0 < wt 1` in `formality_of_pureFor` cannot be dropped: there is\na weight-graded dg-algebra whose cohomology is concentrated in weight `0` in every degree\n(i.e. `wt = 0`-pure) which is *not* formal, and indeed has a nonzero triple Massey product.\n\n*The key insight is* that with `\u03b1 = 0` the weight excess of a Massey representative is `0`, so\nthe weight count that drives every proof in this development degenerates precisely there \u2014 the\ntruncation `A'` also collapses, since the line `w = wt n` becomes horizontal.\n\n*Why now?* Both proofs isolate the use of `\u03b1 > 0` to a single inequality\n(`wt (p+q+r) \u2260 wt (p+q+r\u22121)`), so the boundary of the theorem is completely explicit and a\ncounterexample would settle sharpness. Falsifiable in the other direction by proving formality\nfor `\u03b1 = 0`.\n\n## C5. Formality descends along filtered colimits and tensor products of pure algebras\n\n**Conjecture.** If `A` and `B` are weight-graded dg-algebras that are `wt`-pure, then the graded\ntensor product `A \u2297 B` (bigraded by the sum of bidegrees) is `wt`-pure, hence formal; and purity\npasses to filtered colimits of weight-graded dg-algebras with weight-preserving transition maps.\nConsequently formality for spaces satisfying weight-monodromy is stable under products and under\npassage to limits of admissible covers.\n\n*The key insight is* that purity is a *pointwise* condition on bidegrees and the diagonal\n`w = wt n` is a subgroup of `\u2124\u00b2`, so it is preserved by every operation that adds bidegrees.\n\n*Why now?* The definitions in this development are already stated bidegree-wise, and the\nK\u00fcnneth-type step needed is exactly the statement that the diagonal is closed under addition,\nwhich is used (and proved) in `sPiece_mul_le`. Falsifiable: a pure `A` and pure `B` whose\ntensor product has cohomology off the diagonal.\n",
