    "description": "# Future directions\n\nDerived from this cycle's results in\n\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/PrivateAverageDistortion.lean` \u2014 the private\n  rate\u2013distortion function `D_priv(p) = min_c E_p[d(c,X)]`, its invariance under\n  randomization, its exact Hamming value `\u2211_i min(mass_i(0), mass_i(1))` (majority\n  vote), and the uniform value `|\u03b1|/2`;\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/RelabelingPrivacy.lean` \u2014 orbit distortion under the full\n  relabeling group equals the weight gap, the sharp relabeled threshold `\u2308|\u03b1|/2\u2309`,\n  the exact orbit (Burnside) ball volume, and the surviving rate converse;\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/PrivacyMeasureConverse.lean` \u2014 the measure/Fano-type\n  excess-distortion converse `1 \u2212 \u03b5 \u2264 rate \u00b7 \u03b2` and its uniform Hamming form\n  `(1\u2212\u03b5)\u00b72^{|\u03b1|} \u2264 rate \u00b7 \u2211_{i\u2264D} C(|\u03b1|,i)`, with the sharp threshold recovered at\n  `\u03b5 = 0`;\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/PrivacyTensorization.lean` \u2014 additivity of the private\n  worst-case distortion across product components.\n\nEach conjecture below is falsifiable and stated so that a single Lean theorem (or a\nsingle explicit counterexample) settles it.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (relabeled *average* distortion is a binomial mean deviation)\n\nFor the uniform source on `\u03b1 \u2192 Bool` with `n = |\u03b1|`, the private **expected**\ndistortion modulo relabeling is\n\n```\nmin_c E_unif[ orbDist c X ] = min_{0 \u2264 k \u2264 n} E[ |k \u2212 W| ],   W ~ Bin(n, 1/2),\n```\n\nand this minimum equals `n \u00b7 C(n\u22121, \u230a(n\u22121)/2\u230b) / 2^{n\u22121}`, i.e. `\u0398(\u221an)` rather than\n`\u0398(n)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the two reductions already proved compose: `orbDist`\ndepends on its arguments only through the Hamming weight\n(`orbDist_eq_dist_wt`), so the average-case private optimization collapses from a\nminimization over `2^n` reconstructions to a minimization over `n+1` integers of the\nmean absolute deviation of a binomial law \u2014 a one-dimensional problem.\n\n*Why now?*  Both halves are in place and formal: the weight reduction is\n`orbDist_eq_dist_wt`, and the average-case framework (`privDist`, its `IsLeast`\ncharacterization, and the invariance under randomization) is\n`PrivateAverageDistortion.lean`.  What remains is a binomial identity, not a new\ninformation-theoretic idea.  Falsified by any `n` where brute force disagrees.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (marginal determinacy fails for non-additive distortion)\n\nAverage-case tensorization is now a theorem: for an *additive* distortion on a\nproduct, `D_priv(p) = \u2211_i D_priv(marginal_i(p))` with **no** independence\nassumption (`privDist_prodDist`).  The conjecture is that additivity is exactly\nwhat makes this work: for the *max* distortion `d(c,s) = max_i d_i(c_i, s_i)` on a\ntwo-component product,\n\n* the worst-case private threshold is still marginal-determined, namely\n  `coveringRadius d = max_i coveringRadius d_i`; but\n* the average-case one is **not**: there are two source laws on `\u03c3_1 \u00d7 \u03c3_2` with\n  identical marginals and different `D_priv`, and the gap is bounded by a coupling\n  (total-variation) distance between `p` and the product of its marginals.\n\n*The key insight is* that `privDist_prodDist` works only because the expected\ndistortion of a single reconstruction is linear in the source and additive in the\ncomponents, so the optimization decouples; under a `max` distortion the expectation\nstill is linear but no longer decouples, so joint structure must resurface.\n\n*Why now?*  Both endpoints are formalized: the additive case is closed, and the\ncovering-radius machinery (`coveringRadius_le_iff`, `exists_far_of_coveringRadius`)\ntransfers verbatim to a `max` distortion.  A single explicit pair of laws on\n`Bool \u00d7 Bool` settles the second bullet either way.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (entropy form of the measure converse)\n\nLet `p` be a source law on `S`, let a channel with record alphabet `M` have\nexcess-distortion probability `\u03b5` at radius `D`, and let `\u03b2 = max_c p(B(c,D))`.\nThen\n\n```\nH(X) \u2212 H(X | Y) \u2265 (1 \u2212 \u03b5)\u00b7(\u2212log \u03b2) \u2212 h(\u03b5) \u2212 \u03b5\u00b7log|S|,\n```\n\nand the counting statement `mass_good_le_rate_mul_ballMass` is exactly its\n`\u2212log`-linearization at `\u03b5 = 0`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the fibre decomposition proved here is already the\ncombinatorial core of Fano's inequality: the fibres of `obs` are the conditioning\nevents, and the ball-mass bound `\u03b2` is precisely the term that becomes `log vol_D`\nafter taking logarithms.\n\n*Why now?*  The measure version is proved with no measure-theoretic overhead\n(finite sums only), so the remaining work is Mathlib's finite-entropy API plus the\nconcavity of `\u2212x log x`; no new combinatorics is needed.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (partial relabeling interpolates between the two thresholds)\n\nFix a subgroup `G \u2264 Perm \u03b1` and let `orbDist_G x y = min_{g \u2208 G} hdist(x \u2218 g, y)`.\nThen the private worst-case distortion `coveringRadius orbDist_G` is a monotone\nfunction of `G` interpolating between `|\u03b1|` (for `G = 1`) and `\u2308|\u03b1|/2\u2309` (for\n`G = Perm \u03b1`), and for `G` acting with orbits of sizes `n_1, \u2026, n_r` on the\ncoordinates it equals\n\n```\n\u2211_{j=1}^{r} \u2308n_j / 2\u2309.\n```\n\n*The key insight is* that the invariants of an intransitive coordinate action are\nthe per-orbit weights, so the covering problem again becomes a product of\none-dimensional interval-covering problems \u2014 which is exactly the tensorization\ntheorem `coveringRadius_prod` applied to the orbit decomposition.\n\n*Why now?*  The two extreme cases are proved (`hamming_coveringRadius` and\n`orbit_coveringRadius`) and the additivity machinery needed to glue orbits is\nproved (`coveringRadius_prod`); a single lemma identifying `orbDist_G` with a\nproduct distortion would close it.  A subgroup whose value differs from the formula\nfalsifies it.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Markov sources give an exponential deficit)\n\nFor histories generated by a two-state Markov chain on snapshots with mixing\nparameter `\u03b4`, the ambient count `2^{T n\u00b2}` in the converse can be replaced by\n`2^{T n\u00b2 \u00b7 H(\u03b4) + o(T)}`: a private observer at radius `D` must satisfy\n\n```\n(1 \u2212 \u03b5) \u00b7 2^{T n\u00b2 H(\u03b4)} \u2272 \u2211_{i \u2264 D} C(T n\u00b2, i),\n```\n\nso the privacy threshold drops from `T n\u00b2` to `T n\u00b2 H(\u03b4)` to first order.\n\n*The key insight is* that the measure converse proved here is already stated for an\n*arbitrary* source law `p` \u2014 only the ball-mass bound `\u03b2` changes \u2014 so a structured\nsource enters solely through the maximal ball mass, i.e. through a typical-set\nestimate, not through the covering argument.\n\n*Why now?*  `mass_good_le_rate_mul_ballMass` removes the counting restriction that\nblocked this direction; what is missing is a typical-set bound on `max_c p(B(c,D))`\nfor Markov measures, which is a self-contained large-deviation estimate.\n\n---\n\n### Three concrete next-cycle sub-conjectures\n\n1. **(1a)** `min_{0\u2264k\u2264n} E|k \u2212 Bin(n,1/2)| = n\u00b7C(n\u22121,\u230a(n\u22121)/2\u230b)/2^{n\u22121}` \u2014 a purely\n   binomial identity, checkable by brute force for `n \u2264 20`.\n2. **(4a)** For `G = \u27e8(1 2)\u27e9 \u2264 Perm (Fin n)` the relabeled threshold equals\n   `1 + (n \u2212 2)`, i.e. only the moved pair contributes a saving.\n3. **(3a)** The `\u03b5 = 0` case of Conjecture 3: `log rate \u2265 log|S| \u2212 log vol_D` as a\n   statement about `Real.logb`, obtained from `card_le_rate_mul_ball` by taking\n   logarithms \u2014 the smallest genuinely information-theoretic corollary of the\n   present counting results.\n",
