    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions: the rational star pencils of the Berggren tree\n\nThis cycle established that the radial lines seen when the Berggren tree is plotted in the\nPoincar\u00e9 half-plane are not confined to the ideal points `0` and `1`. Every rational\nboundary point `p/q` (in lowest terms) carries a fan, indexed by the integral **charge**\n`k = p\u00b7m \u2212 q\u00b7n`; the ray of charge `k` is a hypercycle at hyperbolic distance\n`arsinh(|k|/q)` from the vertical geodesic over `p/q`; the fan is *full* when `p + q` is odd\nand consists of the *odd* charges only when `p + q` is even; each ray carries `2\u00b7\u03c6(|k|)`\nnodes per window of `2|k|` parameters; the innermost rays are exactly the Farey neighbours\nof `p/q`; and only the fans with `q \u2264 y/\u03b5` are resolved at plot height `y` and resolution\n`\u03b5`, so the visible centres form the Farey set of level `\u230ay/\u03b5\u230b`, of cardinality\n`\u2211_{q \u2264 Q} \u03c6(q)`. Finally the tree *transports* fans: the three moves act linearly on the\nstar parameter `(p,q)`, preserving primitivity and the parity of `p + q`, which is why the\n`0`-star and the `1`-star can never be exchanged.\n\nThe following conjectures are the next targets. Each is stated so that a single computation\nor a single counterexample would refute it.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Farey visibility asymptotic). The number of fans resolved at resolution `\u03b5` and height `y` is `(3/\u03c0\u00b2)(y/\u03b5)\u00b2 + O((y/\u03b5) log(y/\u03b5))`.\n\nFormally: with `Q = \u230ay/\u03b5\u230b`, the number of resolved star centres in `(0,1]` is\n`\u2211_{q \u2264 Q} \u03c6(q) = 3Q\u00b2/\u03c0\u00b2 + O(Q log Q)`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the visibility law has already converted a *geometric* question\n\u2014 which fans a plot at a given resolution can display \u2014 into the summatory totient, so what\nremains is a purely analytic statement about `\u03a6(Q) = \u2211_{q\u2264Q} \u03c6(q)` and nothing about\nhyperbolic geometry survives in it.\n\n**Why now?** The reduction is complete and exact: the fan at `p/q` is resolved if and only if\n`q \u2264 y/\u03b5`, and the number of such centres is exactly the Farey count. The resulting statement\nwould be the first quantitative prediction about the *picture* itself (\"doubling the\nresolution quadruples the number of visible fans\").\n\n**Falsifiable by:** any enumeration whose ratio `\u03a6(Q)/Q\u00b2` stabilises away from\n`3/\u03c0\u00b2 = 0.30396\u2026`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Exact node count on a ray). On the ray of odd charge `k` at an odd/odd rational `p/q`, the number of nodes with first coordinate at most `M` is `(\u03c6(|k|)/|k|)\u00b7(M/q) + O(\u03c3\u2080(|k|) + q)`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the window law already gives the *exact* periodic count \u2014\n`2\u03c6(|k|)` nodes per window of `2|k|` consecutive parameters \u2014 so the only missing ingredient\nis the translation from the parameter `s` to the seed size `m`, which is the linear map\n`m = k\u00b7b + s\u00b7q`; the density is therefore forced and the error term is an edge effect.\n\n**Why now?** The window law is proved and the parametrisation `(m,n) = (k b + s q, k a + s p)`\nis explicit and unimodular, so the conversion is a finite computation with an explicit\nstarting bound.\n\n**Falsifiable by:** any ray whose measured count deviates from `(\u03c6(|k|)/|k|)(M/q)` by more\nthan a bounded multiple of `\u03c3\u2080(|k|) + q`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3 (The four-case density law). The arithmetic density of the ray of charge `k` at `p/q` is `\u03c6(K)/K` when `p + q` is even, and `\u03c6(K)/(2K)` when `p + q` is odd and `K` is odd, with the remaining case governed by the parity split `2\u03c6(K) \u2212 \u03c6(2K)`.\n\nThe totient density law was established for `p` and `q` both odd, where the parity condition\non the node holds automatically and coprimality is the sole obstruction. When `p + q` is odd\nthe parity condition instead selects a single residue class of the parameter modulo `2`, and\nenumeration shows the density halving exactly when `K` is odd. Formulating and proving the\ncomplete four-case law \u2014 parity of `p + q` against parity of `K` \u2014 would close the section.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Angular equidistribution). The nodes with hypotenuse in `[N, 2N]` equidistribute over the corresponding hyperbolic annulus, after averaging past the resolution scale.\n\nThe star structure shows that at any *finite* resolution the answer is far from a smooth\ndensity: the fans impose a Farey-type filtration on the angular variable. The question is\nwhether that filtration washes out in the limit, and at what rate. The slope model \u2014 the\ndistribution of `n/m` among Euclid seeds \u2014 is the natural formulation.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Modular interpretation of transport). The three transports generate an explicitly identifiable subgroup of `GL\u2082(\u2124)` acting on primitive star parameters, whose orbits are exactly the two parity classes.\n\nThe maps `T\u2081(p,q) = (2p\u2212q, p)`, `T\u2082(p,q) = (2p\u2212q, \u2212p)`, `T\u2083(p,q) = (p, q\u22122p)` preserve\nprimitivity and the parity of `p + q`, and the ladder `k/(k+1) \u2192 0` is one explicit orbit.\nIdentifying the group and matching its fundamental domain against the seed trichotomy would\nplace the whole fan system inside modular-curve geometry, where the parity dichotomy should\nappear as a cusp-width phenomenon, and might yield the equidistribution statement for free.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 6 (A second-order star structure). The star centres themselves carry a canonical tree structure inherited from the transport action.\n\nEvery rational carries a fan, and every node lies on one ray of infinitely many fans \u2014 in\nparticular, every node is an innermost node of two fans of smaller denominator. The ladder\ntheorem, which collapses infinitely many fans onto the fan at `0`, is a first hint that the\ncentres are organised into a tree of their own; making this precise, and relating it to the\nStern\u2013Brocot structure of the rationals, is open.\n",
