    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions (cycle 1 of thread th_2130c620)\n\nThe five conjectures inherited from cycle 0 have now been formalised and proved in a precise\nmodel (see `Catalog/Novelty/MultiwaySortingRadix.lean`, `SortingDirectSum.lean`,\n`SortingFluctuationPenalty.lean`, `PriorSensitiveSorting.lean`).  What follows are the\n*next* conjectures, each derived from a gap that only became visible after those proofs.\n\n## 1. Semantic realisability of transcript profiles (the comparison-availability gap)\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `A(n, q)` be the set of transcript maps realisable by *adaptive\ncomparison* algorithms, where the `k`-th query is a function of the previous answers and is\none of the `q`-outcome order queries on `n` items.  Then `A(n, q)` is strictly smaller than\nthe set of injective maps `Perm (Fin n) \u2192 Fin d \u2192 Fin q` for every `q \u2265 3` and every\n`d < \u2308log_q (n!)\u2309 + c` with an absolute constant `c \u2265 1`, and the exact minimal adaptive\ndepth exceeds the counting bound `\u2308log_q (n!)\u2309` for infinitely many `(n, q)`.\n\nThe key insight is that our proved lower bound `Sorter.clog_le_depth` uses only injectivity,\nwhereas realisable transcripts must additionally be *consistent*: the fibre of any prefix of\na transcript is an order ideal cut out by the queries asked so far, and multiway order\nqueries cannot split a permutation class into `q` arbitrary pieces. **Why now?** The counting\nbound is proved and its achievability (`exists_sorter_clog_depth`) is proved to hold in the\n*abstract* transcript model, so the gap between the two models is now a sharply posed,\nfinitely testable question for small `(n, q)`.\n\n## 2. Uncomputation quantifies the equality case of the direct-sum theorem\n\n**Conjecture.** For block sorting, define the *garbage index* of a reversible protocol as\n`|Aux| / (m!\u00b7n!)`.  Then the minimal number of elementary uncomputation steps needed to\nbring a protocol to the garbage-free case is `\u0398(log (garbage index))`, and the class of\nprotocols with garbage index `1` is exactly the class characterised by\n`block_history_equality_iff_no_garbage` \u2014 i.e. bijectivity of the history map is not only\nnecessary and sufficient but *quantitatively stable*: a protocol within a factor `1 + \u03b5` of\nthe minimum has history map surjective on a `(1 \u2212 \u03b5)`-fraction of `Aux`.\n\nThe key insight is that our equality theorem is an all-or-nothing pigeonhole statement, while\n`block_history_strict_of_garbage` shows the failure mode is a cardinality defect that should\ncarry a metric, not just a boolean. **Why now?** Both endpoints (equality iff bijective, and\nstrictness under non-surjectivity) are proved, so the interpolating stability statement is\nthe only missing piece and is falsifiable on `(m, n) = (3, 2)` by exhaustive search.\n\n## 3. A fluctuation\u2013depth uncertainty relation\n\n**Conjecture.** For a finite-time sorting protocol executing `d` binary comparisons with\nper-comparison relaxation time `\u03c4`, the dissipation identity\n`\u27e8W\u27e9 \u2212 F = kT \u00b7 D(p \u2016 p^R)` (proved, `dissipated_work_eq_relEntropy`) is bounded below by\n`kT \u00b7 Var(W) / (2 (kT)\u00b2 + C d \u03c4\u207b\u00b9)`, so that the *product* of the excess work and the total\nprotocol time `d\u03c4` is bounded below by an absolute multiple of `Var(W)`.\n\nThe key insight is that our proof extracts the excess from the strict `log x \u2264 x \u2212 1` defect\nat each trajectory; second-order expansion of that defect converts the divergence into a\nvariance, which is the quantity a finite-time protocol cannot suppress. **Why now?** The\nexact divergence identity removes all ambiguity about the equilibrium term, so any\nspeed-versus-dissipation trade-off must live entirely in the second-order remainder, which is\nalready isolated in the proof.\n\n## 4. Prior-sensitive history is incompressible below the entropy, even with side information\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `p` be a prior on orderings and let the sorter be allowed a free\nside-information register correlated with the input (a \"catalyst\" that must be returned\nunchanged).  Then the expected retained history is still at least `H(p)` bits, and equality\nholds iff the prior is dyadic; in particular catalysts cannot beat\n`PriorSorter.entropy_le_expectedComparisons`, and the one-comparison slack of\n`exists_priorSorter_within_one_comparison` is unavoidable for non-dyadic priors.\n\nThe key insight is that a returned-unchanged catalyst cannot change the Kraft budget of the\ntranscript code, so the Shannon bound should survive verbatim, while the sharp equality case\n(dyadic priors) is the equality case already isolated in the catalog's\n`expected_length_eq_entropy_iff`. **Why now?** The prior-sensitive floor and its\none-comparison achievability are both proved, so the only remaining question is whether\nauxiliary correlated resources move the floor \u2014 a clean yes/no with a concrete dyadic\nboundary.\n\n## 5. Radix-mixing and the optimal query alphabet\n\n**Conjecture.** Allow the `k`-th query to have its own radix `q_k`.  Then the minimal\n\"mixed-radix depth vector\" for sorting `n` items satisfies `\u220f_k q_k \u2265 n!`, the ideal work\n`kT \u2211_k log q_k` is minimised exactly when `\u220f_k q_k` is the smallest product of the allowed\nradices exceeding `n!`, and the resulting minimum work exceeds `kT log (n!)` by strictly less\nthan `kT log (max_k q_k)` \u2014 a strict improvement over the fixed-radix sandwich\n`optimal_radix_work_sandwich` whenever `n!` is not a power of a single allowed radix.\n\nThe key insight is that the fixed-radix sandwich loses up to one full query register because\n`\u2308log_q n!\u2309` overshoots; mixing radices makes the overshoot a number-theoretic approximation\nproblem for `n!` by products from a fixed multiplicative set. **Why now?** The fixed-radix\ntheorem gives the exact baseline and the exact loss term, so the mixed-radix optimum is a\nwell-posed integer-programming question that can be solved exactly for `n \u2264 8`.\n",
