    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nDerived from the verified results in\n\n* `Catalog/Novelty/CollatzSpectralNormalized.lean` \u2014 limit law, modulus formula,\n  resonance classification, `3n+1` vs `5n+1` vs `7n+1` discriminator, `L\u00b2` mean;\n* `Catalog/Novelty/CollatzSpectralUniform.lean` \u2014 explicit `O((1+|\u03c9| log N)/N)`\n  error bound, uniform convergence on compact frequency sets, and the\n  density-zero blindness no-go;\n* `Catalog/Novelty/CollatzSpectralResonance.lean` \u2014 arithmetic of the resonance\n  sets and pairwise separation of the three classical multipliers.\n\nWhat this cycle settled. The one-step phase `T(n)/n` splits into a constant even\nbranch `1/2` and an odd branch `a + 1/n`; the odd perturbation `1/n` is summable\nafter Ces\u00e0ro averaging, so `F_N(\u03c9)/N \u2192 (e(\u03c9/2)+e(a\u03c9))/2` with modulus\n`|cos(\u03c0(a\u2212\u00bd)\u03c9)|`.  Every question about \"cancellation for the `an+1` map at a\nfixed frequency\" is therefore *completely answered* at the level of the one-step\nsum, and the answer contains no dynamical information whatsoever\n(`spectral_blind_to_finite_modification`).  The following conjectures push into\nthe regimes where information can survive.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Second-order spectral law: the `log N` term is a genuine invariant)\n\nFor every `a \u2265 1` and every `\u03c9 \u2260 0`,\n`F a \u03c9 N \u2212 N \u00b7 limitAmp a \u03c9 \u2212 c(a,\u03c9) \u00b7 log N` converges as `N \u2192 \u221e`, with\n`c(a,\u03c9) = \u03c0 i \u03c9 e(a\u03c9)` (the coefficient produced by the odd-branch expansion\n`e(\u03c9/n) = 1 + 2\u03c0i\u03c9/n + O(\u03c9\u00b2/n\u00b2)` summed against the odd harmonic series).\nEquivalently, `(F a \u03c9 N \u2212 N\u00b7limitAmp a \u03c9)/log N \u2192 c(a,\u03c9)`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the first-order term erases the multiplier's\narithmetic (it depends only on `e(a\u03c9)` through a modulus that is a single\ncosine), while the *second*-order term is an honest half-harmonic sum whose\ncoefficient is linear in `\u03c9` and carries the branch phase `e(a\u03c9)` undamped \u2014\nso the subleading spectrum sees strictly more than the leading one.\n\n**Why now?** `norm_F_div_sub_limitAmp_le` already isolates the deviation sum\n`\u03a3 dseq` and bounds it by `2\u03c0|\u03c9|(1+log N)`; the conjecture asserts that this\nbound is attained with an explicit constant.  The Lean infrastructure\n(`dseq_eq`, `sum_one_div_le_one_add_log`) needs only a matching *lower* bound\nplus `Real.log` asymptotics for the odd harmonic sum, both of which are in reach\nof the existing `harmonic` API.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Iterated transforms are not blind)\n\nDefine the `m`-step transform `F^{(m)} a \u03c9 N = \u03a3_{n\u2264N} e(\u03c9 \u00b7 T_a^m(n)/n)`.\nThen for each fixed `m`, `F^{(m)} a \u03c9 N / N` converges to an explicit finite\ncombination `\u03a3_{j} 2^{\u2212m} \u00b7 (multiplicity) \u00b7 e(\u03c9 \u00b7 r_j)`, where `r_j` ranges over\nthe finitely many limiting `m`-step ratios `a^{k} / 2^{m\u2212k}` (`k` = number of odd\nsteps), weighted by the density of residue classes mod `2^m` following that\nparity pattern.  Moreover, for `m \u2265 2` the resonance set of `F^{(m)}` is **not**\na coset of a lattice, and its zero set determines `a` uniquely.\n\n**The key insight is** that one step gives two branches and hence a cosine, i.e.\none Diophantine condition; `m` steps give `2^m` branches with ratios\n`a^k/2^{m\u2212k}`, so the limiting amplitude becomes an exponential polynomial in\n`\u03c9` whose zero set is an intersection of many conditions \u2014 rigid enough to\nrecover `a`, and no longer invariant under sparse modification of the map.\n\n**Why now?** The proof template is exactly the one verified here: split by the\nparity pattern of the first `m` steps (a residue class mod `2^m` of known\ndensity), show the ratio converges on each class, and Ces\u00e0ro-average.\n`summand_eq` + `dseq_eq` + `Filter.Tendsto.cesaro_smul` generalize verbatim.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3 (A density-zero blindness threshold)\n\n`spectral_blind_to_density_zero` is sharp in the following strong sense: for\nevery function `h : \u2115 \u2192 \u211d` with `h N / N \u2192 0` and `h N \u2192 \u221e` there exist a\nmultiplier `a` and a modification of `ratio a` supported on a set `S` with\n`#(S \u2229 [1,N]) \u224d h N` such that the modified transform still satisfies\n`F\u0303/N \u2192 limitAmp a \u03c9` for all `\u03c9`, yet the modified map has an orbit structure\n(e.g. an extra cycle of length `\u224d h N` at scale `N`) that differs from the\noriginal.  Conversely, any modification on a set of positive upper density\nchanges the limit for a set of frequencies of positive measure.\n\n**The key insight is** that \"invisible to the normalized spectrum\" is exactly\n\"supported on a density-zero set\", so the spectral method has a precise\ninformation-theoretic ceiling: it can only ever certify statements that are\nthemselves invariant under density-zero surgery \u2014 and cycle existence is not.\n\n**Why now?** The forward half is already proved (`norm_Fgen_sub_le` gives the\n`2\u00b7#S/N` bound, which is the whole content).  The converse needs a lower bound\non `\u2016\u03a3_{k \u2208 S} (e(\u03c9 r\u2081) \u2212 e(\u03c9 r\u2082))\u2016` after averaging in `\u03c9`, i.e. a Plancherel\nargument on `[0,2]` for which `meanSquare_limitAmp` is the model computation.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Resonance sets classify the multiplier, and pairwise\nintersections are governed by `gcd(2a\u22121, 2b\u22121)`)\n\nFor all `a, b \u2265 1`, the common resonance set\n`R a \u2229 R b = {\u03c9 : (2a\u22121)\u03c9 and (2b\u22121)\u03c9 are both odd integers}` equals\n`{\u03c9 = (2m+1)\u00b7g / ((2a\u22121)(2b\u22121)/g') ...}` \u2014 precisely, writing\n`d = gcd(2a\u22121, 2b\u22121)`, `R a \u2229 R b = (d / ((2a\u22121)(2b\u22121))) \u00b7 (2\u2124+1) \u2229 R a`, and in\nparticular `R a \u2229 R b \u2286 2\u2124+1` exactly when `d = 1`.  Hence `R a = R b` iff\n`a = b`, and the map `a \u21a6 R a` is a complete invariant of the multiplier.\n\n**The key insight is** that the resonance sets are cosets of the lattices\n`(1/(2a\u22121))(2\u2124+1)`, so their intersection theory is pure elementary number\ntheory of the odd numbers `2a\u22121` \u2014 an arithmetic invariant hiding inside a\npurely analytic (Fourier) statistic.\n\n**Why now?** The three instances `d = gcd(5,9) = gcd(5,13) = gcd(9,13) = 1` are\nalready proved in `common_resonance_three_five`, `common_resonance_three_seven`,\n`common_resonance_five_seven`, each via a two-line `omega` certificate.  The\ngeneral statement is the same argument with `d` carried symbolically, and\n`limitAmp_eq_zero_iff` reduces it entirely to `\u2124`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Orbit transforms: a genuine spectral\u2013dynamical bridge)\n\nLet `\u03c3(n)` be the stopping time of `n` under `T_3` and define the *orbit\ntransform* `G \u03c9 N = \u03a3_{n\u2264N} e(\u03c9 \u03c3(n)/log n)`.  Then `G \u03c9 N / N` converges for\nevery `\u03c9` to the characteristic function of the limiting distribution of\n`\u03c3(n)/log n`, and the Collatz conjecture restricted to `[1,N]` is *equivalent*\nto `\u03c3` being everywhere finite, i.e. to `G` being well defined \u2014 whereas no\nstatement about `F a \u03c9 N` has this property (by Conjecture 3 / the proved\nblindness theorem).\n\n**The key insight is** that spectral information about a dynamical system can\nonly be dynamical if the *phase itself* is an orbit functional; replacing the\none-step ratio `T(n)/n` by the normalized stopping time turns the transform into\nthe characteristic function of the (conjecturally log-normal) stopping-time\ndistribution, where cancellation genuinely encodes orbit behaviour.\n\n**Why now?** The present cycle proves the negative half rigorously \u2014 the\none-step transform is provably blind \u2014 which is precisely the justification for\nmoving the phase from one step to the whole orbit.  The positive half is a\nL\u00e9vy-continuity statement, and Mathlib now has the characteristic-function and\nweak-convergence API needed to state it.\n",
