    "description": "# Future directions (Cycle 2, thread th_3b515382)\n\nCycle 2 delivered, with complete Lean proofs and no `sorry`:\n\n* explicit periodic left/right shifts, translation equivariance of every elementary\n  rule, and the Rule 110 singleton theorem as a corollary of a general\n  right-transitivity criterion;\n* the rule-number bijection `Fin 256 \u2243 LocalRule` and a census showing that exactly\n  `16` rules provably have a unique fixed configuration on every cyclic lattice;\n* `MvPolynomial` models: `eval s (rulePoly g i) = step g s i`, polynomial-level shift\n  equivariance, and the fixed-point ideal (update equations plus the Boolean relations\n  `X i ^ 2 - X i`) whose `GF(2)`-points are exactly the fixed configurations;\n* exact fixed-point counts as arithmetic functions: `#V(150,n) = 4` for `n` even and\n  `2` for `n` odd; `#V(90,n) = 4` when `3 \u2223 n` and `1` otherwise, via a periodic\n  descent principle that reduces an infinite family to one finite check;\n* simulation embeddings `Config d \u21aa Config n` for `d \u2223 n` preserving exact orbit\n  lengths, the existence of a periodic orbit for every rule, and an exact 2-cycle of\n  Rule 110 on every lattice with `4 \u2223 n`;\n* the Klein four-group of reflection/complementation symmetries realised as\n  *conjugacies of the dynamics*, hence invariance of both the fixed-point count and\n  the exact orbit spectrum, which enlarges the certified census from `16` to `48`\n  rules (rules `124`, `137`, `193` inherit the Rule 110 singleton theorem);\n* a companion criterion (zeros travel left, ones travel right) certifying that the\n  four rules `176, 178, 184, 186` \u2014 including the traffic rule `184` \u2014 have exactly\n  the two constant configurations as fixed points, on every lattice.\n\nThe following conjectures are the sharpest open questions those results raise.  Each\nis falsifiable by a finite computation or by a single counterexample.\n\n## C1. The singleton criterion is *characterised* by an eventual-orbit condition\n\n**Conjecture.**  For an elementary rule `g` the following are equivalent: (i)\n`#V(g,n) = 1` for every `n \u2265 1`; (ii) `#V(g,n) = 1` for `n = 1,2,3,4,5,6,7`; (iii) the\nonly bi-infinite `g`-fixed word is the all-zero word.  In particular the set of rules\nwith `#V \u2261 1` has exactly `78` elements; `48` of them are already certified by the\nsymmetry-closed criterion, so the conjecture predicts exactly `30` further rules whose\nunit count needs a non-local argument.\n\n*The key insight is* that `#V(g,n)` counts closed walks of length `n` in the de Bruijn\ngraph of `g` restricted to the \"fixed\" edges `g a b c = b`; a count that stays `1`\nforever means that this subgraph has a unique cycle, which is decidable from a graph\nwith `4` vertices \u2014 hence a finite test must exist, and the only question is how small\nit can be.\n\n*Why now?*  The census machinery (`ruleEquiv`, `singletonRules`,\n`mem_singletonRules_fixedPoints_ncard`) already exists, and the transfer-matrix\nstatement below would turn (i) \u21d4 (ii) into a linear-algebra computation over `\u2115`.\n\n## C2. Transfer-matrix trace formula for every rule and every size\n\n**Conjecture.**  Let `T g : Matrix (Cell \u00d7 Cell) (Cell \u00d7 Cell) \u2115` have entry\n`T[(a,b),(b',c)] = 1` iff `b = b'` and `g a b c = b`, and `0` otherwise.  Then for all\n`n \u2265 1`,\n`#V(g,n) = trace (T g ^ n)`.\nConsequently `n \u21a6 #V(g,n)` satisfies a linear recurrence of order at most `4` with\ninteger coefficients, and is a sum of `n`-th powers of the eigenvalues of a `4\u00d74`\n`0/1`-matrix.  In particular the sequences `1,1,4,1,1,4,\u2026` (rule 90), `2,4,2,4,\u2026`\n(rule 150) and `1,3,1,3,\u2026` (rule 30), each now proved individually, would become\ninstances of one eigenvalue phenomenon rather than three separate arguments.\n\n*The key insight is* that a fixed configuration on `ZMod n` is precisely a closed walk\nof length `n` in the fixed-edge de Bruijn graph, so counting is a trace, and the\narithmetic periodicity observed for rules 90, 150 and 30 comes from roots of unity in\nthe spectrum.\n\n*Why now?*  The descent theorem `ncard_fixed_eq_of_periodic` already proves the\nspecial case where the graph is a disjoint union of cycles; the general case only\nneeds the \"matrix power counts walks\" lemma, which is the missing infrastructure.\n\n## C3. Rule 110 has a non-fixed periodic configuration exactly when `4 \u2223 n`\n\n**Conjecture.**  For `n \u2265 1`, rule 110 has a configuration of minimal period `> 1` on\n`ZMod n` if and only if `4 \u2223 n`.  The \"if\" direction is proved\n(`rule110_exists_minimalPeriod_two`); the \"only if\" direction is verified for\n`n \u2264 8` computationally.\n\n*The key insight is* that the smallest rule-110 glider has spatial period `4`, so all\nshort orbits should be pullbacks along `ZMod n \u2192 ZMod 4`, and the conjecture says that\nthe entire short-orbit structure of the universal rule on a torus is generated by a\nsingle four-cell pattern.\n\n*Why now?*  The simulation-embedding theorem `minimalPeriod_pull` gives one direction\nfor free, and it also says what a proof of the converse must produce: a retraction of\nthe periodic part of the dynamics onto the four-site lattice.\n\n## C4. The fixed-point coordinate ring is `0`-dimensional but its length separates rules\n\n**Conjecture.**  For every rule `g` and every `n \u2265 1` the quotient\n`MvPolynomial (ZMod n) (ZMod 2) / fixedIdeal g n` is a finite-dimensional\n`GF(2)`-algebra \u2014 hence of Krull dimension `0` \u2014 and its `GF(2)`-dimension equals\n`#V(g,n)`.  Thus Krull dimension is a *useless* invariant here, while the length of\nthe coordinate ring recovers exactly the point count.\n\n*The key insight is* that the Boolean relations `X i ^ 2 - X i` force the quotient to\nbe a product of copies of `GF(2)`, one per `GF(2)`-point, so all the information sits\nin length, not dimension \u2014 which explains why the \"dimension tracks complexity\"\nheuristic of cycle 0 had to fail.\n\n*Why now?*  `fixedIdeal` and `mem_zeroLocus_iff_isFixed` are in place; what is missing\nis the Chinese-remainder decomposition of a Boolean quotient ring, a self-contained\npiece of commutative algebra.\n\n## C5. A sheaf on the lattice site, specified before use\n\n**Specification (to be formalised, then tested).**  Site: the category whose objects\nare the arcs `[i, i+\u2113]` of the cyclic lattice `ZMod n` and whose morphisms are\ninclusions, with the Grothendieck topology generated by finite covers by overlapping\narcs.  Presheaf: `F(U) = { partial configurations on U that satisfy the update\nequation at every site whose full neighbourhood lies in U }`, with restriction the\nobvious truncation.\n\n**Conjecture.**  `F` is a sheaf, and `\u0393(ZMod n, F) \u2245 V(g,n)`; moreover `F` is flasque\nif and only if `g` is right-transitive in the sense of\n`CAShiftsPolynomialsOrbits.RightTransitive`, so the `16` census rules are exactly the\nones with vanishing higher cohomology on the cyclic site.\n\n*The key insight is* that gluing partial fixed configurations across overlapping arcs\nis exactly the propagation argument used in\n`fixedPoints_eq_singleton_zero_of_right_transitive`, so the obstruction to gluing is\nthe failure of that local criterion.\n\n*Why now?*  The propagation lemma is already isolated and proved; the sheaf language\nwould turn it from an ad-hoc induction into a cohomological statement, and gives a\nconcrete falsifiable prediction (flasqueness \u21d4 right transitivity) rather than a vague\nanalogy.\n",
