    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Diophantine\u2013Lattice: Spectral Bounds on Non-Homogeneous Quadratic Forms\n\nAll conjectures below are falsifiable statements about the objects formalised in\n`Catalog/Applications/DiophantineLatticeSpectral.lean` (`QF`, `HasSpectralBounds`,\n`inhomEval`, `sqDistLattice`, `inhomMin`, `theta`).  They arose from the four\nverification cycles recorded in that file and from the numerical experiments in\n`ComputationalEvidence.md`.\n\n## What is already settled (so the conjectures are not restatements)\n\n* Spectral gap: `m\u00b7d(t,\u2124\u207f)\u00b2 \u2264 Q(x \u2212 t)` for all `x \u2208 \u2124\u207f` (`spectral_gap_lower`),\n  strict positivity off the lattice, and the resulting non-solvability\n  (`no_integer_solution_below_gap`).\n* Sandwich for the inhomogeneous minimum: `m\u00b7d(t,\u2124\u207f)\u00b2 \u2264 \u03bc(Q,t) \u2264 M\u00b7n/4`\n  (`inhomMin_sandwich`).\n* Effective rational gap `m/q\u00b2` (`Cycle2.gap_rational_shift`).\n* Two-sided counting `(2\u221a(R/(Mn)) \u2212 1)\u207f \u2264 N(R) \u2264 (2\u221a(R/m) + 1)\u207f`\n  (`Cycle3.counting_two_sided`).\n* Theta series: convergence, factorisation over diagonal forms, decay at the gap\n  rate, and `\u0398(s) \u2192 0` (`summable_theta`, `Cycle2.theta_diagonal`,\n  `theta_decay`, `Cycle2.theta_tendsto_zero`).\n* Exact extremal set for the half-shifted sum of squares: the `2\u207f` vertices of\n  the unit cube (`Cycle4.half_shift_minimizer_count`).\n\n---\n\n## C1. Sharp covering constant: the inhomogeneous minimum is controlled by the *trace*, not by `n\u00b7\u03bb_max`\n\n**Conjecture.** For every symmetric positive definite `A` with spectral bounds\n`m, M` and every shift `t`,\n`\u03bc(Q, t) \u2264 (1/4)\u00b7tr(A)`, and this is attained iff `A` is diagonal and `t` is a\nhalf-integral shift.  In particular `\u03bc(Q,t) \u2264 M\u00b7n/4` (the proved bound) is never\nsharp unless all eigenvalues coincide.\n\n*The key insight is* that the rounding point `x = round(t)` used in\n`exists_le_covering` wastes information: it bounds `Q(x \u2212 t)` by\n`\u03bb_max\u00b7\u2016x \u2212 t\u2016\u00b2`, whereas rounding *coordinatewise* gives\n`Q(x \u2212 t) = \u2211\u1d62\u2c7c A\u1d62\u2c7c (x\u1d62\u2212t\u1d62)(x\u2c7c\u2212t\u2c7c)` whose expectation over a random half-integral\ndisplacement is exactly `tr(A)/4`, the off-diagonal terms cancelling in mean.\n\n*Why now?* The averaging argument only needs the finite probability space\n`{\u00b11/2}\u207f`, which is directly expressible with the existing `Finset` machinery in\nthe file; no measure theory is required, so it is a self-contained next step that\nwould upgrade every corollary depending on `M\u00b7n/4`.\n\n## C2. Gap\u2013theta duality: the decay rate of `\u0398` *equals* the inhomogeneous minimum\n\n**Conjecture.** For a spectrally sandwiched form with `m > 0`,\n`lim_{s\u2192\u221e} \u2212(1/s)\u00b7log \u0398(s) = \u03bc(Q, t)`, and moreover\n`\u0398(s)\u00b7exp(s\u00b7\u03bc(Q,t)) \u2192 r(\u03bc)`, the number of integer points attaining the minimum\n(finite by `card_solutions_le`).\n\n*The key insight is* that `theta_decay` proves one inequality\n(`\u2265 m\u00b7d(t,\u2124\u207f)\u00b2` for the rate) purely from the term bound, while the matching\nupper bound must come from isolating the minimising terms and dominating the rest\nby the counting estimate `N(R) \u2264 (2\u221a(R/m)+1)\u207f` \u2014 i.e. the *counting* theorem and\nthe *decay* theorem are two halves of one Laplace-type asymptotic.\n\n*Why now?* Both halves are already formalised; the missing step is a dominated\ntail estimate `\u2211_{Q(x\u2212t) \u2265 \u03bc+\u03b4} exp(\u2212sQ) \u2264 C\u00b7exp(\u2212s(\u03bc+\u03b4))`, which follows from\npolynomial coefficient growth by a summation-by-parts argument on `\u2124\u207f` \u2014 a\nfinite-combinatorial lemma, not new analysis.\n\n## C3. Rational shifts are extremal: a Diophantine dichotomy for the gap\n\n**Conjecture.** For `m > 0` fixed, the gap function `t \u21a6 m\u00b7d(t,\u2124\u207f)\u00b2` restricted\nto shifts with denominator dividing `q` attains its minimum *exactly* at shifts\nwith a coordinate of exact denominator `q`, with value `m/q\u00b2`\n(`Cycle2.gap_rational_shift` is the inequality); and for irrational `t` the\nsequence of best rational approximations `p_k/q_k` of a coordinate produces gaps\n`\u224d m/q_k\u00b2`, so that `liminf q\u00b2 \u00b7 gap` over the convergents is positive iff the\ncoordinate is badly approximable.\n\n*The key insight is* that `distZ_rat_ge` is the Diophantine statement\n`\u2016a/q\u2016_\u2124 \u2265 1/q` in disguise, so the spectral gap of a shifted lattice form is a\n*continued-fraction invariant* of the shift, not merely a metric quantity.\n\n*Why now?* Mathlib has continued fractions and the Hurwitz/Dirichlet\napproximation theorems; combining them with `distZ_rat_ge` turns the qualitative\n\"off-lattice \u21d2 positive gap\" theorem into a quantitative badly-approximable\ndichotomy with no new lattice theory required.\n\n## C4. Reduction stability: the gap is invariant, the covering bound is not\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `U \u2208 GL\u2099(\u2124)`, the transformed form `Q_U(y) = Q(Uy)`\nwith shift `U\u207b\u00b9t` has the *same* inhomogeneous minimum `\u03bc(Q_U, U\u207b\u00b9t) = \u03bc(Q, t)`\nand the same solution counting function, but its spectral bounds `m_U, M_U` can\ndiffer arbitrarily; consequently the LLL-reduced representative minimises the\nratio `M_U\u00b7n/4 : m_U\u00b7d(U\u207b\u00b9t,\u2124\u207f)\u00b2` and the sandwich `inhomMin_sandwich` becomes\nasymptotically tight after reduction.\n\n*The key insight is* that `inhomMin` is a `GL\u2099(\u2124)`-invariant of the pair\n`(form, shift)`, whereas both ends of the proved sandwich are not \u2014 so lattice\nreduction is precisely the act of choosing coordinates in which the two\n*non-invariant* bounds pinch the *invariant* quantity.\n\n*Why now?* The invariance half is a short bijection argument on `\u2124\u207f` (the map\n`x \u21a6 U\u207b\u00b9x` is a `Finset`-preserving equivalence), so the conjecture can be split\nimmediately into a provable invariance lemma and a genuinely open optimisation\nstatement about reduced bases.\n\n## C5. Theta factorisation obstructs off-diagonality (a converse to `theta_diagonal`)\n\n**Conjecture.** If for some shift `t` with all coordinates irrational the theta\nseries of `A` factorises as a product of `n` one-dimensional shifted Gaussian\nsums for all `s > 0`, then `A` is diagonal.  Equivalently: the failure of\nmultiplicativity of `\u0398` is a faithful invariant detecting off-diagonal mass.\n\n*The key insight is* that `Cycle2.theta_diagonal` proves factorisation from\ndiagonality by Fubini on `\u2124\u207f`, and the reverse direction should follow by\ncomparing the `s \u2192 \u221e` expansions: the first two exponents of `\u0398` recover the two\nsmallest values of `Q(x \u2212 t)`, and matching all of them across the product\nforces the Gram matrix to split.\n\n*Why now?* The proved ingredients (`tsum_pi_prod`, `summable_theta`, and the\ncounting bound that makes coefficientwise comparison legitimate) already give\nuniqueness of Dirichlet-type expansions in this setting; the remaining work is\ncombinatorial bookkeeping of exponents, not new convergence theory.\n",
