    "description": "# Future Directions (cycle 1, thread th_ed2afa00)\n\nDerived from the verified results in `Catalog/Logic/GEBGradedLawvere.lean`:\ngraded (partial) universality, the obstruction spectrum\n(`representable \u27fa pointwise fixed points`), the refutation of the\nmonoid-closure conjecture, the vacuity of full universality, the counting law\n`n^n - (n-1)^n` with density `1 - e\u207b\u00b9`, and the explicit pitch-class triad.\n\n## 1. A relative obstruction spectrum for *iterated* self-application\n\nExtend `RepresentsDiagonal` to depth `k`: a table represents the composite\n`f \u2218 (x \u21a6 eval x (eval x x))` and its higher analogues.  Conjecture: depth-`k`\nrepresentability of `F` holds iff every `f \u2208 F` has a point of period dividing\n`k` under some auxiliary transformation, so the spectrum stratifies into a\nstrictly increasing chain whose `k`-th level over `Fin n` has cardinality\n`n^n - #{f : f has no point of period dividing k}`, and the chain is strict for\nevery `n \u2265 3`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the depth-1 argument consumes the semantic\nendomorphism exactly once, which is precisely why pointwise fixed points \u2014 and\nnot the generated monoid \u2014 control representability; increasing the depth\nshould trade fixed points for periodic points in a controlled way.\n\n*Why now?* The depth-1 spectrum is now a proved equivalence with an exact\nfinite count, so any deviation at depth `k` is immediately measurable against\n`n^n - (n-1)^n`, and the `Fin 3` monoid counterexample supplies the smallest\ndomain where strictness can be tested exhaustively.\n\n## 2. Density laws as an invariant of the semantic category\n\nThe representable density over `Fin n` tends to `1 - e\u207b\u00b9`.  Conjecture: for the\ncategory of finite semantic domains with a fixed extra structure (monoid\nactions, partial orders, metric quantisations), the representable density\nconverges to a structure-dependent constant, and this constant is a complete\ninvariant separating the structures: e.g. monotone endomorphisms of a finite\nchain have density `1` (every monotone self-map of a finite chain has a fixed\npoint) while permutations have density `1 - e\u207b\u00b9 + o(1)` in the derangement\nsense.\n\n*The key insight is* that the obstruction is quantitative: `e\u207b\u00b9` of all\nsemantic dynamics is permanently outside self-applicative syntax, and this\nproportion is computed from the fixed-point predicate alone, so it should vary\npredictably when the ambient class of endomorphisms is restricted.\n\n*Why now?* `card_maximal_representable_family` and\n`representable_density_tendsto` give the unrestricted baseline in closed form,\nso restricted classes can be compared to a proved reference value.\n\n## 3. Coherence hierarchy for weak simulations\n\nThe current file proves: equivalences transport diagonal codes and values,\ncoherent retractions lift codes, embeddings preserve values but not code-hood\n(explicit `Unit \u21aa Bool` counterexample), bisimulations preserve only\nobservational equality (explicit parity counterexample).  Conjecture: these are\nthe only four levels \u2014 i.e. there is a four-element lattice of coherence laws\nsuch that each strictly weaker law admits a two-element counterexample and each\nlaw is necessary and sufficient for the corresponding transport statement.\n\n*The key insight is* that transport uses preservation of the evaluation table\nin both arguments simultaneously; dropping preservation in the second argument\nkills code-hood while preserving values, and dropping equality to a congruence\nkills values while preserving observations.\n\n*Why now?* Each level already has a proved positive statement and, where it\nfails, a minimal finite counterexample, so the remaining work is exactly the\ncompleteness of the lattice.\n\n## 4. Uniqueness from guardedness in ordered and metric domains\n\n`contraction_code_independence` shows that uniqueness of semantic fixed points\nmakes all diagonal codes agree across unrelated presentations.  Conjecture: the\nsame conclusion holds for guarded endomorphisms of ultrametric or\ndomain-theoretic semantics (Banach fixed points in complete ultrametric spaces,\nor unique fixed points of guarded recursive definitions), and moreover the\nresulting canonical value is computable as the limit of the diagonal iterates\nof *any* code.\n\n*The key insight is* that presentation transport is canonical while code choice\nis not; uniqueness is the exact hypothesis that collapses the second source of\nambiguity, and guardedness is its syntactic shadow.\n\n*Why now?* The Lipschitz case is proved with no isomorphism hypothesis\nwhatsoever, so the statement is already in the strongest possible \"unrelated\ncodes\" form and can be ported verbatim to any uniqueness principle.\n\n## 5. Maximal *graded* triads with genuine domain morphisms\n\nThe explicit triad over `ZMod 12` uses evaluation-preserving equivalences of\nthree three-constructor syntaxes.  Conjecture: for the free graded model on a\nfamily `F`, the group of evaluation-preserving automorphisms is isomorphic to\nthe group of permutations of `F` fixing the chosen fixed-point assignment,\ntimes the stabiliser of the literals; consequently two graded triads over the\nsame `F` are equivalent iff their fixed-point assignments agree, and the number\nof inequivalent triads over `Fin n` with `|F| = m` is computable in closed form.\n\n*The key insight is* that the free graded model is determined by nothing but a\nchoice function selecting one fixed point per endomorphism, so classification\nof triads reduces to classification of such choice functions.\n\n*Why now?* `exists_table_of_fixed_points` builds the free model explicitly and\n`geb_triad_second_value` shows that different choices give genuinely different\ndiagonal witnesses (`0` versus `3`), so the moduli problem is nonempty and\nfinite.\n",
