    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Strange Attractors as Algebraic Objects\n\nDerived from the analysis and adversarial review of this research thread.\n\n## What is established\n\n**Structure.** The orbit space of any finite directed graph **is** the inverse limit of its finite path\ndiagram, with no hypotheses; the limit is compact, Hausdorff, totally disconnected and perfect, and\ncontains a closed-embedded Cantor set as soon as the graph branches, with an outright homeomorphism\nwith the space of binary sequences for the Lorenz template.\n\n**Counting.** Periodic points of the shift are closed walks for every length $n \\ge 1$; closed walks are\ntraces of powers of the transfer matrix; and that trace sequence obeys the Cayley\u2013Hamilton recurrence of\nthe characteristic polynomial \u2014 the finite-graph form of rationality of the orbit-counting generating\nfunction.\n\n**Dynamics.** Entropy exists by Fekete subadditivity, is monotone under adding edges, is bounded by\n$\\log \\#V$, and equals $\\log 2$ for the Lorenz template and $\\log\\varphi$ for the pruned template;\nprimitivity of the graph forces topological mixing and dense periodic orbits, hence Devaney chaos.\n\n**New in this cycle \u2014 the spectral bridge:**\n\n* **Spectral form of the entropy.** For every finite dead-end-free graph carrying a Perron datum (a\n  strictly positive eigenvector of the transfer matrix), the entropy equals the logarithm of the\n  eigenvalue. This was Conjecture 1 of the previous cycle, now proved in Perron form: the analytic\n  invariant is computed by linear algebra. The proof pinches the number of length-$n$ paths between two\n  constant multiples of $\\lambda^n$ using only the minimum and maximum of the eigenvector.\n* **Uniqueness of the Perron value.** Consequently the Perron eigenvalue is unique \u2014 the uniqueness half\n  of Perron\u2013Frobenius, obtained by a purely dynamical route. For the two templates this gives outright\n  rigidity: the value is exactly $2$ for the Lorenz template and exactly $\\varphi$ for the pruned one.\n* **Bounds.** $1 \\le \\lambda \\le \\#V$.\n* **Arithmeticity.** The Perron value is a root of the *monic integral* characteristic polynomial of the\n  transfer matrix, so $\\exp(\\text{entropy})$ is an **algebraic integer** \u2014 an arithmetic constraint on a\n  dynamical invariant.\n* **Periodic-orbit growth.** For a primitive graph, $\\log \\#\\mathrm{ClosedWalk}(n)/n \\to$ entropy;\n  periodic orbits grow *exactly* at the entropy rate. Upper bound: the eigenvector inequality\n  $(A^n)_{ii} \\le \\lambda^n$. Lower bound: primitivity lets one prefix and suffix an arbitrary path of\n  length $q$ by walks of length $m$, so $\\#V^2 \\cdot \\operatorname{tr}(A^{q+2m}) \\ge \\#P_q$.\n* **Existence of Perron data.** The Collatz\u2013Wielandt variational construction supplies a strictly\n  positive eigenvector for every primitive finite digraph, making all spectral statements\n  hypothesis-free: geometric simplicity of the eigenspace, spectral dominance (so the Perron value is\n  the spectral radius), and strict positivity of the entropy for at least two branches.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n* **Zeta functions in full.** Prove the determinant formula\n  $\\zeta(t) = \\exp\\bigl(\\sum_n \\#\\mathrm{Per}_n\\, t^n/n\\bigr) = 1/\\det(I - tA)$ directly, then the\n  functional equation for reversible templates. For the two templates this yields $1/(1-2t)$ and\n  $1/(1-t-t^2)$.\n* **Non-primitive and reducible graphs.** Develop irreducible components, the Frobenius period and\n  cyclic decompositions, giving the entropy as a maximum over components' Perron values \u2014 the symbolic\n  analogue of spectral decomposition of an Axiom A basic set.\n* **The realisation problem.** Which algebraic integers occur as Perron values? Constructing, for each\n  Perron number, a graph realising it would turn the arithmetic constraint into an exact\n  classification.\n* **Beyond one dimension.** Higher-dimensional shifts of finite type lose most of the theory: entropy is\n  no longer the logarithm of an algebraic number. Identifying which parts of the inverse-limit\n  machinery survive is a natural frontier.\n* **Templates for other attractors.** Instantiate the theory at H\u00e9non and R\u00f6ssler return graphs in\n  hyperbolic parameter regimes, producing certified entropies and algebraic separations for those\n  families, and allowing attractors to be compared across families by matching characteristic\n  polynomials.\n* **Quantitative Perron bounds.** Make the eigenvector spread explicit in terms of the graph (via\n  Hilbert projective-metric contraction rates) to convert the qualitative estimate\n  $\\#P_n \\asymp \\lambda^n$ into effective two-sided bounds with computable constants, giving rigorous\n  error bars on truncated entropy computations.\n",
