    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nThis cycle deepened the tropical scheme/Gr\u00f6bner thread by adding the *matroidal*\nlayer that the previous cycle's purely submodule-theoretic notion of tropical\nideal was missing: the vector elimination axiom, its proof for tropical\nhyperplanes, its failure for intersections, its behaviour under minors, the\nresulting circuit theory, and a first genuinely nontrivial tropical ideal (the\nvanishing ideal of a point) on which the Buchberger machinery of the previous\ncycle actually runs.\n\nThe conjectures below are the next testable steps, ranked by expected impact.\n\n## C1. Stable intersection repairs elimination\n\n**Conjecture.**  For coefficient vectors `c\u2081, c\u2082` on a finite ground set `E`, the\n*stable* intersection `H(c\u2081) \u2229_st H(c\u2082)`, defined as the set of limits\n`lim_{t \u2192 0} (H(c\u2081) \u2229 H(c\u2082 + t\u00b7v))` along a generic perturbation direction `v`,\nsatisfies the vector elimination axiom, even though the set-theoretic\nintersection does not (`intersection_not_satisfiesElimination`).\n\n*The key insight is* that the failure exhibited in this cycle is caused by a\nnon-transverse coincidence of two tropical minima, and perturbing one hyperplane\ndestroys precisely that coincidence while leaving the elimination witnesses\nconstructed in `tropVanishing_elimination` intact.\n\n*Why now?*  The formal framework (`IsTropicalLinearSpace`, the explicit witness\nconstruction, and a verified counterexample delimiting the truth) is in place, so\nthe conjecture is a statement about one extra limit operation rather than about\nan untested definition.\n\n## C2. Every tropical linear space is a hyperplane intersection minor\n\n**Conjecture.**  Every tropical linear space `V \u2286 T^E` on a finite ground set is\nobtained from a finite family of tropical hyperplanes by *stable* intersection\nfollowed by deletion; equivalently, the operations proved to preserve tropical\nlinear spaces in this cycle (`isTropicalLinearSpace_scaleSet`,\n`isTropicalLinearSpace_deletion`) together with stable intersection generate the\nwhole class from hyperplanes.\n\n*The key insight is* that the circuits of `V` (now available as a formal object,\nwith circuit elimination proved) each cut out a hyperplane containing `V`, and\nthe circuit elimination axiom is exactly the compatibility needed to reassemble\n`V` from those hyperplanes.\n\n*Why now?*  `circuit_elimination` and `exists_isCircuit_subset` supply the\ncombinatorial skeleton; what remains is a purely constructive reassembly step.\n\n## C3. Degreewise matroids of tropical ideals are non-uniform in general\n\n**Conjecture.**  There is a point-free tropical ideal \u2014 for instance the\nvanishing ideal of two distinct points, or the tropicalization of a curve \u2014 whose\ndegree-`d` truncation has a matroid that is *not* uniform for `d` large enough,\nin contrast with `truncation_isCircuit_iff` for a single point.  Concretely, the\nnumber of circuits of the degree-`d` truncation grows strictly slower than\n`binom(N_d, 2)`, where `N_d` is the number of monomials of degree at most `d`.\n\n*The key insight is* that a single point imposes exactly one linear condition per\ndegree, giving corank one and hence a uniform matroid, whereas several conditions\nforce dependencies among monomials and thus non-trivial circuits.\n\n*Why now?*  The uniform case is now a theorem, so any deviation is measurable\nagainst a proved baseline, and the truncation formalism\n(`truncation_eq_tropVanishing`) transfers the question to explicit finite\nmatroids.\n\n## C4. Buchberger completion computes circuits\n\n**Conjecture.**  For the vanishing ideal of a point and a test set `U` consisting\nof all polynomials supported on a finite monomial set `E`, the Buchberger\ncompletion of `\u2205` returns, up to tropical scaling, exactly a set of polynomials\nwhose supports are the circuits of the degree-`E` matroid \u2014 i.e. Buchberger\ncompletion is a circuit enumeration algorithm in the tropical world.\n\n*The key insight is* that leading-monomial reducibility, the driving relation of\nthe completion step, is monotone in the support order, so unresolved obstructions\nmust have minimal support.\n\n*Why now?*  Both halves exist formally and are already connected:\n`exists_buchberger_completion_vanishing` runs the algorithm on this ideal, and\n`truncation_isCircuit_iff` identifies the target set.\n\n## C5. A Nullstellensatz for tropical ideals with elimination\n\n**Conjecture.**  A subsemimodule `I` of the tropical polynomial semiring that\nsatisfies the degreewise elimination axiom and whose degree-`d` matroid is\nuniform of corank one for all `d` is precisely the vanishing ideal of a single\npoint of `\u211a^\u03c3`.\n\n*The key insight is* that corank one in each degree pins down one linear\nfunctional per degree, and the multiplicativity proved in\n`vanishesAt_mul_left` forces those functionals to be compatible across degrees,\nhence to come from a single evaluation.\n\n*Why now?*  The forward direction is fully proved in this cycle\n(`truncation_isCircuit_iff` plus `vanishingIdeal`), so only the converse \u2014 a\nrigidity statement about compatible corank-one systems \u2014 remains.\n",
