    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nThe five conjectures posed for this cycle are now theorems (see\n`Catalog/Logic/ImmortalityHierarchy.lean`, `Catalog/Logic/ImmortalityITTMClock.lean`,\n`Catalog/Logic/ImmortalityStructure.lean`, `Catalog/Logic/ImmortalitySigmaLimit.lean`). Every statement below is a *new*, concrete,\nfalsifiable conjecture generated by what those proofs revealed, with a definite Lean shape.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture A \u2014 The transfinite refinement tower reaches `\u03b5\u2080` exactly at level `\u03b5\u2080`\n\nExtend `nondetExtIter : \u2115 \u2192 SurvivalGame \u2192 SurvivalGame` to a tower indexed by ordinals: at\nsuccessors refine by `\u03c9`, at limits take the dependent lexicographic sum used to build\n`limitGame`. Conjecturally the value at level `\u03b4` is `\u03c9 ^ \u03b4` for every `\u03b4` below the first\nfixed point of `\u03b4 \u21a6 \u03c9 ^ \u03b4`, and the tower's own limit is exactly `\u03b5\u2080`:\n\n```lean\ntheorem towerValue (\u03b4 : Ordinal) : (tower \u03b4 finiteGame).value = \u03c9 ^ (\u03b4 + 1)\ntheorem tower_limit_value : (towerLimit finiteGame).value = \u03b5\u2080\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that the level-`k` bound already proved here comes entirely from the key\nfunction `limitKey \u27e8k, a\u27e9 = \u03c9 ^ k + typein a`, and that key is definable uniformly in the level:\nreplacing `k : \u2115` by `\u03b4 : Ordinal` turns the finite Cantor-normal-form argument into the general\none, so no new combinatorics is needed \u2014 only a type-level transfinite recursion.\n\n**Why now?** The `\u03a3\u2097`-based limit construction is verified, including its well-foundedness\n(the actual obstacle: Mathlib has no `IsWellOrder` instance for lexicographic sigma types, and\nthe key function supplies one). Moreover `ImmortalitySigmaLimit.sigmaGame_value_eq` already\nproves the *general* limit step \u2014 any chain of lives cofinal in an additively principal ordinal\nattains it \u2014 so only the transfinite type-level recursion remains to be built.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture B \u2014 Refinement stability is exactly Cantor-normal-form tail condition\n\n`ImmortalityStructure.refinementStable_opow_iff` settles stability inside the scale `\u03c9 ^ a`.\nThe general statement should be:\n\n```lean\ntheorem refinementStable_iff_cnf (o : Ordinal) :\n    RefinementStable o \u2194 o = 0 \u2228 \u2200 e \u2208 (CNF \u03c9 o).map Prod.fst, \u03c9 \u2264 e\n```\n\ni.e. `\u03c9 \u00b7 o = o` exactly when every Cantor exponent of `o` is infinite.\n\n**The key insight is** that `\u03c9 \u00b7 o = o` is a *purely local* condition on the leading behaviour of\n`o`: multiplication by `\u03c9` shifts each Cantor exponent `e` to `1 + e`, and `1 + e = e` is\nequivalent to `\u03c9 \u2264 e`, so stability is a tail condition, not a size condition.\n\n**Why now?** Mathlib carries `Ordinal.CNF` with its induction principles, and the `\u03c9 ^ a` case is\nalready proved here, which fixes the exact form of the general criterion and its proof skeleton.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture C \u2014 Clockability gap: not every ordinal is a closure ordinal of a monotone system with a *finitely presented* step\n\nThe system of `ImmortalityITTMClock` has closure ordinal exactly `\u03c9\u00b2`, but its step operator was\nallowed to consult the whole earlier configuration. Restrict to step operators that are\n*continuous of finite character* (a cell's activation depends on a finite subset of the earlier\nconfiguration). Conjecture: the closure ordinals of such systems on a countable cell set are\nexactly the ordinals `\u2264 \u03c9\u00b2` closed under `+ 1`, and in particular `\u03c9^\u03c9` is *not* one of them:\n\n```lean\ntheorem no_finite_character_system_of_closure_omega_pow_omega :\n    \u00ac \u2203 (S : FiniteCharacterSystem), IsLeast {\u03b1 | S.Terminal \u03b1} (\u03c9 ^ \u03c9)\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that `stage_succ` shows the closure ordinal of a monotone system equals the\norder type of its arrival well-order; finite character caps the \"branching depth\" of the arrival\norder, so it should cap the achievable order type \u2014 a genuine gap theorem separating machine\nresources from ordinal size.\n\n**Why now?** The faithful realization (`arrivalIso`) makes \"closure ordinal\" and \"order type of\nthe reachable-time well-order\" literally the same object, so any resource restriction on the\nmachine translates directly into an order-theoretic restriction on clocks.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D \u2014 Concatenation asymmetry is the only obstruction to a commutative theory\n\n`concatGame_not_comm` shows lives do not commute. Conjecture: they commute exactly on the\n*additively principal* scale, i.e.\n\n```lean\ntheorem concat_comm_iff (G H : SurvivalGame) :\n    (concatGame G H).value = (concatGame H G).value \u2194\n      \u2203 a : Ordinal, \u2203 m n : \u2115, G.value = \u03c9 ^ a * m \u2227 H.value = \u03c9 ^ a * n\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that `\u03b1 + \u03b2 = \u03b2 + \u03b1` for ordinals holds precisely when `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` are\nnatural multiples of a common additively principal ordinal, so the survival-theoretic question\n\"does the order of two lives matter?\" has a complete Cantor-normal-form answer.\n\n**Why now?** `concatGame_value` reduces the question to ordinal arithmetic, and Mathlib's\n`Principal (\u00b7 + \u00b7)` API (already used here for `\u03c9\u00b2`) supplies exactly the needed absorption\nlemmas.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture E \u2014 Value is a complete invariant for *strategy* equivalence, not just clocks\n\n`value_eq_iff_orderIso` shows the survival value is a complete invariant of the clock. The\nstrategic refinement conjecture: two games have the same value iff Mortal's forcing powers agree\n*uniformly under refinement*, i.e.\n\n```lean\ntheorem value_eq_iff_forall_forces (G H : SurvivalGame) :\n    G.value = H.value \u2194 \u2200 K : SurvivalGame, \u2200 \u03b2 : Ordinal,\n      (extBy G K).MortalForces \u03b2 \u2194 (extBy H K).MortalForces \u03b2\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that `extBy_value` turns refinement into left multiplication, so the claim\nbecomes: ordinals are separated by left multiplication together with `\u2264`. This is false for a\nsingle test multiplier (`2 \u00b7 \u03c9 = 1 \u00b7 \u03c9`), so the quantifier over all `K` is essential \u2014 the\nconjecture is a statement about which distinctions survive a non-cancellative operation.\n\n**Why now?** The negative example `not_forall_lt_extBy_value` isolates precisely the failure of\ncancellation, so this conjecture is the sharp repair of the naive claim, and every ingredient is\nalready formalized.\n",
