    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 the magma monoid as a transformation semigroup\n\nAll conjectures below are stated for `Bin(X)`, the monoid of all binary\noperations on a set `X` under `(f * g) a b = g (f a b) (f b a)`, formalized in\n`Catalog/Tropical/MagmaMonoid/`.  Each is falsifiable, and each is a direct\ndescendant of a theorem proved in this cycle.\n\nRecall the results now available:\n\n* `Bin X \u2243* (C_{T(X\u00d7X)}(swap))\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56` (`Structure.lean`);\n* `IsRegular f \u2194 commutativeImage f = diagonalImage f` (`Regularity.lean`);\n* `L`, `R`, `D` criteria (`Green.lean`, `GreenD.lean`);\n* `Z(Bin X) = {leftZero, rightZero}` for `|X| \u2265 2` (`Center.lean`);\n* `|(Bin (Fin n))\u02e3| = n! \u00b7 2^m \u00b7 m!`, `m = n(n-1)/2` (`UnitGroup.lean`);\n* the general form of the criterion for an arbitrary group action: a\n  `G`-equivariant map is regular in the equivariant transformation monoid iff\n  every image point has a preimage with at least the same stabilizer\n  (`EquivariantRegularity.lean`).\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (structure of the unit group)\n\n`(Bin (Fin n))\u02e3 \u2243* (Sym(n) \u00d7 (\u2124/2 \u2240 Sym(m)))\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56`, `m = n(n-1)/2`, the\nisomorphism being induced by restricting a swap-equivariant permutation of\n`X \u00d7 X` to the diagonal and to the set of reversal-orbits of off-diagonal\npairs.\n\n*The key insight is* that a unit of the magma monoid is nothing but a\npermutation of `X \u00d7 X` commuting with reversal, and reversal is an involution\nwith exactly two kinds of orbits, so the unit group must split as the direct\nproduct of \"what happens on the diagonal\" and \"what happens on the pairs\" \u2014 the\norder formula `n! \u00b7 2^m \u00b7 m!` already proved is precisely the shadow of this\nsplitting.\n\n*Why now?* The cardinality identity `card_units_bin_fin` is proved and the\nunit-to-permutation dictionary `unitsMulEquiv` is in place; only the wreath\ndecomposition of the off-diagonal factor remains, and Mathlib now has\n`Equiv.Perm.Basis`/`kerParam` machinery designed for exactly this.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (classification of `L`-classes over a finite set)\n\nFor `X = Fin n`, the `L`-classes of `Bin(X)` are in bijection with the pairs\n`(P, D)` where `P \u2286 X \u00d7 X` is reversal-invariant and non-empty, `D \u2286 P \u2229 \u0394`\nwith `|D| \u2264 n`, and `|(P \u2229 \u0394) \\ D| + k(P) \u2264 n(n-1)/2`, where `k(P)` is the\nnumber of two-element reversal-orbits of `P`.  In particular the number of\n`L`-classes of `Bin(Fin 2)` is computable in closed form.\n\n*The key insight is* that `greenL_iff` reduces an `L`-class to the pair\n`(pairImage f, diagonalImage f)`, so classification becomes the purely\ncombinatorial question of which such pairs are realizable by an equivariant\nsurjection `X \u00d7 X \u21a0 P` sending the diagonal onto `D`.\n\n*Why now?* Both invariants are proved to be complete `L`-invariants; the\nremaining step is a finite counting argument about equivariant surjections that\n`decide` can corroborate for `n = 2, 3` before a general proof is attempted.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (`D = J` and the ideal lattice)\n\nFor finite `X` the relations `D` and `J` coincide on `Bin(X)`, and the\nprincipal-ideal order is the \"shape order\" induced by\n`greenD_iff`: `f \u2264_J g` iff some swap-equivariant map sends\n`pairImage g` onto a superset of `pairImage f` matching diagonal images.\n\n*The key insight is* that `greenD_iff` already exhibits the `D`-class as the\nisomorphism type of the pair `(pairImage f, diagonalImage f)` as a\n\"reversal-set with marked diagonal\", and such shapes are well-founded for\nfinite `X`, which is the standard mechanism forcing `D = J`.\n\n*Why now?* `GreenD` is characterized and regularity is proved to be a\n`D`-class invariant (`IsRegular.of_greenD`), so the ideal-theoretic layer is the\nfirst genuinely new object in the hierarchy.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (stabilizer obstruction beyond \u2124/2)\n\nFor a finite group `G` acting on a finite set `Y`, the proportion of regular\nelements in the monoid `C_G(Y)` of equivariant transformations tends to `0` as\nthe number of points with non-trivial stabilizer grows, and equals `1` exactly\nwhen the action is free.\n\n*The key insight is* that `regular_equivariant_iff` localizes the obstruction at\npoints with non-trivial stabilizer, and `regular_equivariant_of_free` shows the\nfree case is unobstructed; the failure rate should therefore be governed purely\nby the stabilizer lattice, the magma monoid (`G = \u2124/2`, `n` fixed points out of\n`n\u00b2`) being the first example.\n\n*Why now?* The general criterion is proved, so this is a counting question about\nstabilizers, testable by `#eval` for `G = \u2124/3` on `X \u00d7 X \u00d7 X`, i.e. for the\nternary analogue of the magma monoid.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (regularity as an arithmetic detector)\n\nFor a commutative value monoid `R`, tropical multiplication `x \u2297 y` is a regular\nelement of `Bin(Tropical R)` iff `R` is 2-divisible \u2014 proved\n(`isRegular_tropicalMul_iff`).  The bold extension: for the `k`-fold tropical\npower operation `(x, y) \u21a6 x^i \u2297 y^j`, regularity in `Bin(Tropical R)` holds iff\n`i + j` divides ... more precisely iff every element of `R` is of the form\n`(i + j) \u00b7 s`, so that the whole divisibility lattice of `R` is recoverable from\nsemigroup-theoretic invariants of a single monoid `Bin(Tropical R)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the diagonal obstruction `commutativeImage =\ndiagonalImage` translates a *commutativity* phenomenon of the operation into a\n*solvability* statement in the value monoid, so any commutative operation built\nfrom the semiring structure exports its arithmetic into semigroup theory.\n\n*Why now?* The `i = j = 1` case is a theorem; the general case only needs the\ncomputation of `commutativeImage` for the weighted operations, which the same\ncriterion handles verbatim.\n\n## Conjecture 6 (idempotent census)\n\nThe number `e(n)` of idempotents of `Bin(Fin n)` satisfies `e(1) = 1`,\n`e(2) = 7` (verified by `decide` in `Regularity.lean`), and in general `e(n)`\ncounts the swap-equivariant transformations of `X \u00d7 X` that fix their image\npointwise; consequently `e(n)` grows like `n^{n(n-1)/2} \u00b7 (number of retractive\ndiagonal patterns)`, and in particular `log e(n) / log(n^{n\u00b2}) \u2192 1/2`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `product_self_eq_iff` identifies idempotents with\nretractions of `X \u00d7 X` commuting with reversal, and retractions onto a fixed\nimage set are counted orbit by orbit, which is exactly the computation already\nperformed for the unit group.\n\n*Why now?* Both the fixed-image description of idempotents and the orbit\ncounting technique for the reversal involution are now formalized; combining\nthem is a finite bookkeeping exercise that `#eval` can test for `n = 2, 3`.\n",
