    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Certified Adversarial Robustness via Sheaf Cohomology\n\nDerived from the analysis and adversarial review of this cycle.  All five\nconjectures are falsifiable and each has a concrete first Lean target.\n\nContext: this cycle proved (0 sorries) the discrete Poincar\u00e9 lemma for arbitrary\nnerve graphs with arbitrary abelian coefficients, `H\u00b9(loop, M) \u2243+ M`, the\nquantitative defect theorem `min uniform mismatch = |holonomy|/(n+1)`,\n`H\u00b9(torus) \u2245 \u211d\u00b2`, `H\u00b9 = 0` for tree nerves, the Betti-number law\n`dim H\u00b9 = |E| \u2212 |V| + 1`, the nonabelian discrete Poincar\u00e9 lemma with its\nmulti-class permutation-monodromy obstruction, and the equivalence between\ngluing of local `L\u221e` sign certificates and the absence of decision-sheaf\nholonomy.\n\n---\n\n## C1. Plaquette rank conjecture (dimension of `H\u00b9` for `2`-dimensional nerves)\n\n**Statement.** For a finite `2`-dimensional nerve (regions, overlaps, triple\noverlaps), `dim H\u00b9 = |E| \u2212 |V| + 1 \u2212 rank(\u03b4\u00b9)`, where `\u03b4\u00b9` is the plaquette\ncoboundary; equivalently `dim H\u00b9 = dim ker \u03b4\u00b9 \u2212 (|V| \u2212 1)`.\n\n**Evidence.** Verified in the two extreme cases proved here: the loop\n(`rank \u03b4\u00b9 = 0`, `dim H\u00b9 = 1`) and the discrete torus, where the `(m+1)(n+1)`\nplaquette relations have rank `|V| \u2212 1` and cut `dim H\u00b9` from `|V| + 1` down to\nexactly `2` (`finrank_torusH1`).\n\n**The key insight is** that the drop from the graph Betti number to the true\n\u010cech `H\u00b9` is precisely the rank of the plaquette relation matrix, so counting\nadversarial obstructions of a real cover is a rank computation, not a homotopy\ncomputation.\n\n**Why now?** `NerveBetti.finrank_nerveH1` already gives the `1`-dimensional case\nby rank\u2013nullity, and the same rank\u2013nullity argument applies verbatim once `\u03b4\u00b9`\nis defined; the torus file supplies the nontrivial test case.\n\n**First Lean target.** Define `delta1 : (Edge \u2192 \u211d) \u2192\u2097[\u211d] (Plaq \u2192 \u211d)` and prove\n`finrank (ker \u03b4\u00b9 \u29f8 range \u03b4\u2070) + (|V| \u2212 1) = finrank (ker \u03b4\u00b9)`.\n\n---\n\n## C2. Betti number = maximal certified radius deficit\n\n**Statement.** For a connected cover of a continuous score with overlap scale\n`\u03b4`, if the nerve has first Betti number `b`, then the set of local certificate\nfamilies that fail to glue is a codimension-`b` condition, and there exist\nscores realising exactly `b` independent obstruction scales\n`|H\u2081|/L\u2081, \u2026, |H_b|/L_b`, each an upper bound on the certified `L\u221e` radius of some\nregion.\n\n**The key insight is** that the *number* of independent loops of the cover\ncontrols the *number* of independent adversarial directions, so cover design\n(making the nerve a tree) is a certified-robustness intervention.\n\n**Why now?** `holonomy_obstructs_uniform_certificate` gives the `b = 1` case, and\n`cyclic_defect_isLeast` gives the quantitative scale; the general case needs a\nbasis of the cycle space, which `finrank_nerveH1` now supplies.\n\n**Falsifier.** A connected cover with `b = 2` in which every pair of independent\nholonomies is realised by the *same* boundary point would refute independence.\n\n---\n\n## C3. Lipschitz refinement: holonomy lower-bounds the adversarial distance\n\n**Statement.** If the score is `L`-Lipschitz in `L\u221e` and the sign holonomy around\na loop of anchors is nonzero, then there is an adversarial example within\ndistance `min_i |s(x_i)| / L` of some anchor, and this bound is attained by a\npiecewise-linear score.\n\n**The key insight is** that the qualitative obstruction of\n`not_certified_of_holonomy` (an *existence* statement produced by the\nintermediate value theorem) upgrades to a *metric* statement as soon as a\nLipschitz constant is available, turning cohomology into a numeric certificate\nbound.\n\n**Why now?** The IVT witness `exists_boundary_point_near` already localises the\nboundary point on a specific segment; only a Lipschitz estimate along that\nsegment is missing.\n\n---\n\n## C4. Metric multi-class monodromy (the nonabelian certificate theorem)\n\n**Status of the base case.** The *qualitative* nonabelian statement was proved in\nthis cycle: `Nonabelian.nonabelian_discrete_poincare` shows that for any group\n`G`, a transition cochain on a connected nerve comes from a global relabelling\niff its monodromy around every closed walk is trivial, and\n`perm_monodromy_obstructs_global_labelling` (with the realised example\n`exTransition_no_global_labelling`) gives the `k`-class instance.\n\n**Statement (open).** For a `k`-class classifier with continuous score vector\n`s : E \u2192 \u211d^k` and a cover with overlap scale `\u03b4`, the following are equivalent:\n(i) every region admits a local `L\u221e` certificate of radius `\u03b4` for its argmax\nclass; (ii) the permutation monodromy of the label-transport cochain is trivial\non every loop of the nerve.  Moreover, when the monodromy is nontrivial, the\ncertified radius of some region is at most `\u03b4`, witnessed by a point where two\nclass scores tie.\n\n**The key insight is** that the binary equivalence proved here\n(`local_certificates_glue_iff_global` together with\n`holonomy_obstructs_uniform_certificate`) is the `k = 2` case of a statement in\nwhich \"sign change\" is replaced by \"argmax change\", and the intermediate value\ntheorem is replaced by a tie-point argument on the difference of two coordinates.\n\n**Why now?** Both halves already exist separately: the nonabelian gluing theorem\nis proved, and the analytic tie-point argument is a two-coordinate instance of\n`exists_boundary_point_near`.\n\n**Falsifier.** A `3`-class cover with trivial monodromy but no global certificate\nat radius `\u03b4` \u2014 e.g. produced by an argmax that changes without any pairwise tie\ninside a region \u2014 would refute the equivalence.\n\n## C5. Spectral bound: holonomy versus the nerve Laplacian\n\n**Statement.** For a connected nerve with graph Laplacian `\u0394` and Fiedler value\n`\u03bb\u2082 > 0`, the least uniform defect of a discrepancy `g` obeys\n`\u2016g\u2016\u2082\u00b2 \u2212 \u2016proj_B g\u2016\u2082\u00b2 \u2265 \u03bb\u2082\u207b\u00b9 \u2016holonomy(g)\u2016\u00b2`, i.e. spectral expansion of the\nnerve quantitatively suppresses adversarial obstructions.\n\n**The key insight is** that `cyclic_defect_isLeast` is the `L\u221e` instance of an\n`L\u00b2` Hodge decomposition, where the harmonic representative is the projection\nonto `ker \u0394` restricted to the cycle space, so the defect is controlled by the\nspectral gap of the cover.\n\n**Why now?** The exact `L\u221e` answer for the loop is now proved, giving a\ncalibration point (`\u03bb\u2082 = 2(1 \u2212 cos(2\u03c0/(n+1)))` for the cycle graph) against\nwhich any `L\u00b2` bound can be tested numerically before formalisation.\n",
