    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nFive bold, falsifiable conjectures distilled from what survived and what failed\nin this cycle. Each is stated so that a single Lean file could settle it, and\neach is accompanied by the structural insight that suggests it and by the\nreason the present cycle makes it attackable now.\n\nNotation throughout: `x = log N`; `Superpoly`, `Subexp`, `PolyBounded`, `Lfun`,\n`ClassicalResource`, `barrierCost`, `LimitedBy`, `PolyTime` are the definitions\nin `Catalog/Cryptography/FactoringBarriers/`.\n\n---\n\n## C1. The trade-off arity conjecture (`AritySeparation`)\n\n> **Conjecture.** Define the *arity* of a cost profile `f` as the least `k` such\n> that `f x \u2264 k \u00b7 exp(c \u00b7 x^{1/k})` for some `c > 0` and all large `x`. Then the\n> arity classes are strictly nested: for every `k` there is a cost profile of\n> arity `k+1` that is not `O` of any arity-`k` profile, and a profile is\n> polynomially bounded iff it has no finite arity.\n\n*The key insight is* that the AM\u2013GM proof of `tradeoff_lower_bound` shows the\nexponent `1/k` is not a design parameter of any algorithm but the balance point\nof a multiplicative constraint \u2014 so \"improving the exponent\" is literally the\nsame problem as \"adding a stage\", and polynomial time is the `k \u2192 \u221e` limit.\n\n*Why now?* `tradeoff_lower_bound`, `tradeoff_attained` and\n`tradeoff_unbounded_arity_is_poly` are proved, so both endpoints of the ladder\nare formalised; only the strictness of the intermediate steps is open, and it\nreduces to a `Superpoly`-style limit computation of the kind already automated\nin `AsymptoticLadder.lean`.\n\n**Falsifier.** Exhibit `c, c'` and `k` with\n`(k+1)\u00b7exp(c\u00b7x^{1/(k+1)}) = O(k\u00b7exp(c'\u00b7x^{1/k}))`; this would collapse the\nladder and invalidate the classification's non-degeneracy.\n\n---\n\n## C2. Barrier closure under algorithmic composition (`BarrierClosure`)\n\n> **Conjecture.** The class of algorithms limited by a classified barrier is\n> closed under composition, sequential repetition and polynomial-time\n> preprocessing: if `A` is `LimitedBy rho` and `B` is `PolyTime`, then the\n> composite `x \u21a6 A.cost x + B.cost (p x)` is still `LimitedBy rho` for every\n> polynomial reparametrisation `p`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `Superpoly` is stable under `x \u21a6 x/b`\n(`Superpoly.comp_div`, already proved for ECM's `log p \u2248 x/2`), and the same\nargument should give stability under arbitrary polynomial reparametrisation \u2014\nwhich would upgrade the capstone from a statement about single algorithms to a\nstatement about an entire closed complexity class.\n\n*Why now?* The capstone currently quantifies over an *unstructured* set of cost\nprofiles. Closure is exactly what makes `ClassifiedResourceHypothesis` a\nstatement about a robust class rather than about a list, and the technical core\n(`Superpoly.comp_div`) is done.\n\n**Falsifier.** A polynomial reparametrisation `p` and a superpolynomial `f` with\n`f \u2218 p` polynomially bounded \u2014 e.g. any sub-logarithmic `p`; the conjecture must\ntherefore be stated with `p` bounded below by a positive power, and the sharp\nthreshold is itself the open question.\n\n---\n\n## C3. Sample/query duality for period finding (`SampleQueryDuality`)\n\n> **Conjecture.** The information-theoretic bound `K \u2265 r` for *linear* Fourier\n> sampling degrades to `K = \u0398(\u221ar)` \u2014 and no further \u2014 for *adaptive*\n> measurements that may depend on earlier outcomes; i.e. adaptivity buys exactly\n> a birthday-square-root and never more.\n\n*The key insight is* that `dft_sample_count_ge_period` is proved by a pure\ndimension count, which is insensitive to adaptivity, whereas Pollard rho's\n`\u0398(N^{1/4})` is exactly a birthday bound; the conjecture says these two numbers\nare the two ends of a single interpolation and that the randomness barrier\n(barrier 8) *is* the adaptive shadow of the DFT bound.\n\n*Why now?* Both endpoints are formalised in this cycle \u2014 the dimension bound in\n`DFTSampleBound.lean`, the exponential barrier `exp(x/4)` in\n`ResourceClassification.lean`, and the worst-case blindness statement\n`arithmetic_trajectory_blind` in `RandomnessBarrier.lean`, which already\nseparates the provable worst case (`\u221aN`) from the birthday heuristic\n(`N^{1/4}`) \u2014 so the missing piece is a single interpolation theorem rather than\ntwo independent lower bounds.\n\n**Falsifier.** An adaptive sampling scheme determining the period of every\nperiod-`r` signal with `o(\u221ar)` measurements.\n\n---\n\n## C4. Structural orthogonality as a rank condition (`OrthogonalityRank`)\n\n> **Conjecture.** For a semiprime `N = pq`, every function `g : \u2124/N\u2124 \u2192 \u2102`\n> computable from `N` alone by a bounded-depth arithmetic circuit has\n> exponentially small correlation with the indicator of the coset structure\n> `{x : gcd(x, N) > 1}`; consequently no bounded-depth arithmetic algorithm can\n> produce a congruence of squares.\n\n*The key insight is* that `congruence_of_squares` makes the *exploitation* step\nfree, so any lower bound must attack the *production* step; and the production\nstep is precisely a correlation-with-a-hidden-subgroup question, which is a\nrank/Fourier-analytic statement rather than a number-theoretic one.\n\n*Why now?* This cycle isolated the exploitation step as a proved, unconditional\nreduction, so the remaining difficulty is now localised to one clean statement.\nThe correlation formulation also matches the DFT machinery (`ZMod.dft`) already\nimported here.\n\n**Falsifier.** A bounded-depth arithmetic circuit family over `\u2124/N\u2124` with\ninverse-polynomial correlation with `gcd(x,N) > 1`.\n\n---\n\n## C5. Classification completeness is independent (`CRHIndependence`)\n\n> **Conjecture.** `ClassifiedResourceHypothesis` is not provable in the abstract\n> cost-profile model: there is a model of the `ClassicalAlgorithm` signature in\n> which some algorithm is polynomially bounded and limited by no classified\n> barrier, and another in which every algorithm is limited by one. Hence the\n> capstone conditional is optimal \u2014 no strengthening to an unconditional lower\n> bound is possible at this level of abstraction.\n\n*The key insight is* that `schema_nonvacuous` already exhibits both a\nclassified-and-slow algorithm and a fast-and-unclassified one within the *same*\nmodel; upgrading this to a genuine independence statement would prove that the\nhonest scope of the framework is forced, not a matter of taste.\n\n*Why now?* The model is small (a cost function plus a lower bound on it), so\nindependence is a finite-combinatorial matter about `Filter.atTop` statements,\nand both witnesses are already constructed and verified.\n\n**Falsifier.** A proof of `ClassifiedResourceHypothesis` from the axioms of the\nmodel \u2014 which, by `no_poly_under_CRH`, would immediately yield an unconditional\nsuper-polynomial lower bound for classical factoring in this model and would\ntherefore be a major result in its own right.\n",
