    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Free-Witness Thresholds after the Separation Principle\n\nEverything below is derived from the verified results in\n`Catalog/Algebra/FreeWitnessTraceThreshold.lean`.  The organising fact is the\n**separation principle** (`witness_modEq_iff_dvd_powsum_diff`): two\nfactorisations `N = ab = cd` are indistinguishable modulo `m` exactly when\n`m \u2223 (a^k + b^k) \u2212 (c^k + d^k)`.  For a semiprime this collapses to a single\nnumber, the **witness gap** `G_k(p,q) = (p^k \u2212 1)(q^k \u2212 1)`, and the whole\n\"threshold\" question becomes a divisibility question \u2014 not a size question.\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Logarithmic threshold, unconditional)\n\nFor every semiprime `N = pq` with `p, q > 3` the least determining modulus\nsatisfies `m*(p,q) = O(log N / log log N)`, and this is attained infinitely\noften.\n\n*The key insight is* that the failure set is `{m : m \u2223 G_2}`, so `m*` is the\nleast non-divisor of a number `< N\u00b2`; the least non-divisor of `G` is bounded by\nthe first prime not dividing `G`, and `G` has at most `log\u2082 G` distinct prime\nfactors.  `exists_prime_determines` and its pigeonhole sharpening\n`exists_prime_determines_of_card_gt` already prove the counting half (any family\nof more than `\u03c9(G)` primes contains a working modulus), and\n`card_primeFactors_gap_le_log` bounds `\u03c9(G) \u2264 log\u2082 G`; what is missing is an\neffective lower bound for `#{primes < x}` \u2014 a Chebyshev bound \u2014 to convert this\ninto an explicit `x = O(log N loglog N)`.\n\n*Why now?* Mathlib has `Nat.primesBelow`, Bertrand's postulate and the\nPrime Number Theorem infrastructure, so the remaining step is a bookkeeping\nexercise on top of `exists_small_prime_determines`, which is already proved.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Exact density of the `m* = 5` locus)\n\nThe set of semiprimes with `m*(p,q) = 5` has natural density `9/25` among\nsemiprimes ordered by the residues of `(p, q)` mod 5, and more generally\n`Prob[m* = r]` for the `n`-th prime `r` is `\u220f_{s < r} (1 \u2212 2/(s\u22121)) \u00b7 (\u2026)`\ncomputed from the independent equidistribution of `p, q` in `(\u2124/s)^\u00d7`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `five_determines_iff` turns `m* = 5` into the purely\nlocal condition `p, q \u2262 \u00b11 (mod 5)`, so the density question is a Dirichlet\nequidistribution question, one prime at a time; the observed histogram\n(`5 : 28 %`, `7 : 34 %`, `11 : 27 %`, \u2026) should match the product formula.\n\n*Why now?* Dirichlet's theorem with densities is in Mathlib\n(`Nat.infinite_setOf_prime_and_eq_mod`, `DirichletCharacter` L-series), and the\nfinite local conditions are already isolated in `five_dvd_sq_sub_one_iff`.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (Order-`k` collapse to the totient tower)\n\nFor each `k \u2265 1` the order-`k` gap satisfies\n`G_k(p,q) = (p^k \u2212 1)(q^k \u2212 1) = |GL\u2081(\ud835\udd3d_{p^k})| \u00b7 |GL\u2081(\ud835\udd3d_{q^k})|`,\nso the modular threshold for the order-`k` witness is governed by the orders of\nthe unit groups of the degree-`k` extensions, and `m*` for order `k` divides no\n`m` with `m \u2223 lcm` of those group orders.  Consequently `m*_k` is\n*non-increasing in the multiplicative structure*: `m*_k \u2264 m*_{kk'}` never holds\nin general, and the sequence `k \u21a6 m*_k` is eventually periodic modulo any fixed\nprime.\n\n*The key insight is* that `gap_one_eq_totient` (`G_1 = \u03c6(N)`) is the `k = 1`\nshadow of a group-theoretic identity: the free witness of order `k` hides the\nfactorisation behind the order of `(\ud835\udd3d_{p^k})^\u00d7 \u00d7 (\ud835\udd3d_{q^k})^\u00d7`, which is the\nRSA trapdoor for `k = 1` and a Frobenius-twisted trapdoor for `k > 1`.\n\n*Why now?* `determines_iff_not_dvd_gap` is proved for all `k` simultaneously,\nso the conjecture is a statement about one explicit arithmetic function; Mathlib\nhas `ZMod.card_units_eq_totient` and finite field cardinalities to close it.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Beyond semiprimes: the pair-separation spectrum)\n\nFor arbitrary `N` define the *separation spectrum*\n`S_k(N) = { (a^k + b^k) \u2212 (c^k + d^k) : ab = cd = N }`.  Then `\u03c3_k(N) mod m`\ndetermines the full divisor-pair structure of `N` iff `m` divides no nonzero\nelement of `S_k(N)`, and the least such `m` is `O(\u03c9(N) \u00b7 log N)` where `\u03c9` is the\nnumber of distinct prime factors.\n\n*The key insight is* that `witness_modEq_iff_dvd_powsum_diff` already holds for\narbitrary `N` \u2014 the semiprime case is special only because the spectrum has a\nsingle nonzero element, `(p^k\u22121)(q^k\u22121)`.\n\n*Why now?* The general lemma is proved; what remains is a counting argument over\nthe `\u03c4(N)/2` divisor pairs, which needs only `Nat.divisors` API.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Aggregation irreducibility \u2014 the real barrier)\n\nThere is no algorithm that, given `N` and a modulus `m \u2264 polylog(N)`, computes\n`\u03c3\u2082(N) mod m` in time `N^{o(1)}` unless factoring is easy.  Equivalently,\npartial witness values are as hard to *aggregate* as full ones.\n\n*The key insight is* the contrast established here: the *information* needed to\nfactor is only `O(log N)` bits of `\u03c3\u2082` (Conjecture 1, and already\n`exists_small_prime_determines`), so any hardness must come entirely from the\ndivisor-sum aggregation, never from information scarcity.  This isolates\n\"barrier 4\" as a pure complexity statement, with the information-theoretic side\nnow closed.\n\n*Why now?* The formal results here remove the information-theoretic escape\nroute: a barrier proof can no longer appeal to \"not enough of the witness is\nknown\", since `\u03c3\u2082 mod 7` suffices infinitely often.\n",
