    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 bold, testable conjectures from the interval-hint round\n\nThis round formalised the negative verdict for the trace-set (TRACEPROFILE)\nfilter: it is *exact* (`TraceSetFilter.add_mem_traceSet`), *exactly half-sized*\nper prime (`TraceSetFilter.two_mul_card_traceSet_legendre`: `2|T| = m + \u03c7(N)`),\n*minimal among all exact filters* (`TraceSetFilter.card_ge_of_exact_filter`),\n*multiplicative and positionally blind* across primes\n(`TraceSetNoAmplification.card_survivors_interval`), and therefore *incapable of\nisolating a hinted trace* unless the primorial of the moduli exceeds the width of\nthe hint (`TraceSetNoAmplification.isolation_requires_primorial`,\n`TraceSetUniversalBarrier.coupled_isolation_requires_primorial`).  The following\nconjectures push past that boundary.\n\n## C1. The half-bit barrier is a *character* barrier, not a trace barrier\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `f \u2208 \u2124[X, Y]` be any absolutely irreducible symmetric\npolynomial and define the `f`-profile filter\n`T_m^f(N) = { f(a, b) mod m : a\u00b7b \u2261 N mod m, a \u2208 (\u2124/m)\u02e3 }`.  Then\n`|T_m^f(N)| / m \u2192 1/deg_Y(f)` as `m \u2192 \u221e`, with error `O(m^{-1/2})` given by a\nWeil bound; in particular no symmetric invariant of the factor pair prunes more\nthan a `1/deg` fraction, and the pruning rate is *never* `m^{-c}` for `c > 0`.\n\n*The key insight is* that our `2|T| = m + \u03c7(N)` identity is the degree-2 case of\ncounting points on the curve `f(a, N/a) = t`, so the density is governed by the\nnumber of `t`-fibres, i.e. by the degree of the fibre map, not by any arithmetic\nsecret of `N`.\n\n*Why now?* The exact degree-2 count is now a theorem with an elementary\nfibre-counting proof (`two_mul_card_traceSet`); the same fibre argument plus\nMathlib's growing algebraic-geometry API makes the general degree case reachable.\n\n## C2. Free filters cannot beat the primorial, even with an oracle for `\u03c9(N)`\n\n**Conjecture.** For every family of exact filters `S_m \u2286 \u2124/m` computable from\n`N mod m` in `poly(log m)` time, and every hint window of width `W`, the number\nof surviving candidates is at least `W / \u220f_{m \u2208 \ud835\udd44} m` whenever `\u220f_{m \u2208 \ud835\udd44} m \u2264 W`.\nConsequently, isolating a `k`-bit hint requires moduli whose product is `2^{\u03a9(k)}`,\nhence total sieve work `2^{\u03a9(k / log k)}`.\n\n*The key insight is* that exactness forces `S_m \u2287 T_m` (proved:\n`traceSet_subset_of_exact`), and CRT makes the survivor count exactly\nmultiplicative (proved: `card_survivors_zmod`), so the only remaining freedom is\nhow large the modulus product is allowed to be.\n\n*Why now?* The coupled case \u2014 filters that correlate the primes arbitrarily \u2014 is\nalready closed here (`coupled_exact_filter_card_ge`); what remains is the\ncomplexity-theoretic bookkeeping of the sieve cost, which is a finite, formalisable\naccounting exercise rather than a new mathematical idea.\n\n## C3. Hint amplification is equivalent to a square-root oracle\n\n**Conjecture.** An algorithm that, given `N` and a hint interval of width `W`,\nreturns the true trace using `poly(log N)` filter evaluations exists **iff**\nthere is a `poly(log N)` algorithm that, given `N` and `t`, decides whether\n`t\u00b2 \u2212 4N` is a perfect square for `\u03a9(W)` many `t` simultaneously (a \"batch\nsquare-detection oracle\").\n\n*The key insight is* the exact Fermat equivalence\n`exists_factorisation_iff_isSquare_int`: the trace scan and the difference-of-\nsquares scan are the *same* predicate, so amplification of the hint is\nequivalent to batching square detection over the window.\n\n*Why now?* The equivalence is now a theorem over `\u2124` with no side conditions,\nso the reduction can be stated as a clean formal statement about predicate\nfamilies rather than an informal analogy.\n\n## C4. A `2/3`-density invariant would break the barrier \u2014 and none exists\n\n**Conjecture (sharp dichotomy).** For every `N`, every prime `m \u2224 N` and every\n*exact* filter `S \u2286 \u2124/m` obtained from a polynomial invariant of the factor pair,\n`|S| \u2265 (m \u2212 1)/2`; moreover the bound `(m \u2212 1)/2` is attained only by the trace\nset and its affine images.  Hence there is no \"denser-than-half\" free invariant,\nand a hypothetical invariant with density `1/3` would immediately give a\n`3^{-\u03c9}` sieve and a sub-exponential factoring algorithm.\n\n*The key insight is* that any exact filter must absorb the entire orbit of the\nconjugation involution `x \u21a6 N/x`, whose orbit count is `(m \u00b1 1)/2` \u2014 the fixed\npoints being precisely the square roots of `N`.\n\n*Why now?* The orbit computation is formalised (`two_mul_card_traceSet`), and the\nuniqueness half (\"only the trace set attains it\") is a finite combinatorial\nstatement about involutions that current tactics can attack directly.\n\n## C5. Positional blindness is the real obstruction (a general no-go)\n\n**Conjecture.** Any candidate filter that is `M`-periodic accepts exactly `|S|`\nof every `M` consecutive candidates (proved here as `card_window_filter`);\ntherefore *no* periodic test can convert an interval hint into positional\ninformation.  The conjecture is that the converse holds too: a filter that\namplifies an interval hint must be aperiodic, hence must depend on the *size* of\nthe candidate \u2014 and every size-dependent exact test known (Fermat, Lehman,\nHart) already costs `\u03a9(N^{1/3})`.\n\n*The key insight is* that \"residue information\" and \"positional information\" are\northogonal: CRT independence makes the survivor set a union of full residue\nclasses, which is translation-invariant by construction.\n\n*Why now?* `card_window_filter` isolates the periodicity hypothesis in one line\nof Lean, so the converse can be attacked as a statement about the aperiodicity\nof the accepted set rather than about factoring itself.\n",
