    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 the Walsh/GF(2) face of the factoring barrier\n\nDerived from the Stage-3 analysis and Stage-4 critique of the results now proved in\n`Catalog/Novelty/WalshSpectralFlatness.lean` and `Catalog/Novelty/SpectralFlatnessFactoring.lean`.\nEach conjecture is falsifiable by a finite computation at small parameters and is stated so that a\nLean formalisation can begin immediately.\n\n---\n\n## C1. The ordering defect is exactly a top-bit statistic\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `t \u2265 2`, `1 \u2264 j < t`. Over the support of *ordered* odd pairs `p < q` mod `2^t`\nthe correlation of `p_j` with any statistic of the low block `N mod 2^t` is nonzero, but every such\ncorrelation is bounded by `C \u00b7 2^{-t/2}`, and the entire defect is carried by the event\n`p < q` \u2014 formally, the correlation equals `Cov(1[p<q], g(N)) \u00b7 (something depending only on j)`\nup to `O(2^{-t})`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `lowblock_corr_zero` fails on the ordered support for exactly one reason:\nthe sign-reversing involution `p \u21a6 p XOR 2^j` used in `sum_bitSign_oddRes` does not preserve\n`p < q`, so the defect must be a functional of the order statistic alone, i.e. a magnitude\nstatistic \u2014 the same family as the top-bit law.\n\n*Why now?* The proof of the exact theorem isolates the involution as the single point of failure,\nand the computations in `ComputationalEvidence.md` already show the defect shrinking as\n`2/15, 2/31, 1/21` while the maximising parity migrates from `\u2205` to a *high* bit `{6}`.\n\n---\n\n## C2. A degree hierarchy over the prime-restricted support\n\n**Conjecture.** For the exact `k`-bit prime semiprime support, for every fixed degree `d` there is\n`k_0(d)` such that for all `k \u2265 k_0(d)` and all `1 \u2264 j \u2264 k - 7`, every parity of degree `\u2264 d` of\nthe bits of `N` correlates with `p_j` by at most `C_d \u00b7 \u03c0(2^k)^{-1/2} \u00b7 polylog`, while for\n`j > k - 7` the maximum degree-1 correlation stays bounded away from `0`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the two proved theorems bracket the truth from both sides \u2014 exactly zero\nbelow (`lowblock_corr_zero`, on the odd support) and strictly positive above (`covTop_pos`) \u2014 so the\nonly open content is the *transition window*, whose width the experiment measures as ~6 bits.\n\n*Why now?* `highDegree_mass_ge` converts any such correlation bound into a statement about where\nthe Fourier mass lives, so a single quantitative equidistribution input (primes in short\narithmetic progressions mod `2^t`) would upgrade the empirical claim to a theorem.\n\n---\n\n## C3. Sharp constant for the top-bit covariance\n\n**Conjecture.** `covTop e \u2192 (2 log 2 \u2212 1)/4 \u2248 0.09657` as `e \u2192 \u221e`.  Rescaling `p = 2^{k-1}x`,\n`q = 2^{k-1}y` sends the balanced support to `S = {(x,y) \u2208 [1,2]\u00b2 : x \u2264 y}` (area `1/2`), the event\n`p_{k-2} = 1` to `A = S \u2229 {x \u2265 3/2}` (area `1/8`, so `P(A) = 1/4`) and the carry-out event to\n`B = S \u2229 {xy \u2265 2}` (area `1 \u2212 log 2`, so `P(B) = 2(1 \u2212 log 2)`); since `A \u2286 B`, the limiting\ncovariance is `P(A)(1 \u2212 P(B)) = (2 log 2 \u2212 1)/4`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `hiSet \u2286 topSet` is a *scale-invariant* condition once `p = 2^{k-1}x`,\n`q = 2^{k-1}y`, so the covariance is a Riemann sum for a fixed planar area functional and must\nconverge, with the observed decreasing sequence `0.222, 0.180, 0.138, 0.118, 0.107` as its\napproximants.\n\n*Why now?* `covTop_pos` already proves positivity for all `e` by a purely combinatorial argument;\nthe limit statement needs only a lattice-point-counting error term, well inside reach.\n\n---\n\n## C4. No low-degree GF(2) approximator, unconditionally, via the low block\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `d` and every GF(2) polynomial `P` of degree `\u2264 d` in the bits of `N`,\nthe fraction of *odd* pairs `(p,q)` mod `2^t` on which `P(N) = p_j` is exactly `1/2` for all\n`1 \u2264 j < t` \u2014 and this remains true when `P` is allowed to read, in addition, any `o(t)` of the\nhigh bits of the true integer product `pq` (not merely `pq mod 2^t`).\n\n*The key insight is* that the proved theorem already handles *arbitrary* functions of the low\nblock, so the only extension needed is to let the predictor see a bounded amount of magnitude\ninformation; the group-theoretic proof (`group_zero_block`) should survive as long as the extra\ninformation is a function of a coset of a subgroup of bounded index.\n\n*Why now?* `group_zero_block` isolates the mechanism as the regular representation, which makes the\n\"low block + bounded magnitude\" hybrid a statement about a subgroup filtration rather than about\nprimes.\n\n---\n\n## C5. Spectral spreading is achieved by the factoring function\n\n**Conjecture.** For the `k`-bit prime semiprime support, the number of parities with nonzero\nWalsh coefficient for `f_j` is at least `2^{\u03a9(k)}` for every information-bearing `j` \u2014 i.e. the\nlower bound in `card_support_ge_of_flat` is essentially attained, so the factoring bit is not just\nflat but *maximally spread*, behaving like a random function of its input.\n\n*The key insight is* that flatness alone (`|corr| \u2264 \u03b5` everywhere) forces support size `\u2265 \u03b5^{-2}`,\nand the measured `\u03b5 \u2248 10^{-2}` at `k = 14` already forces `\u2265 10^4` nonzero coefficients; pushing\n`\u03b5` down to the noise floor `2^{-n/2}` would force exponentially many.\n\n*Why now?* Both halves of the needed inequality are now formal: `exists_corr_sq_ge` gives the floor\nand `card_support_ge_of_flat` converts a flatness bound into a support lower bound. Only the\nsupport-restricted analogue of Parseval remains.\n",
