    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 tropical reading of the round-4 closures\n\nDerived from the analysis and adversarial review of the four Lean files in\n`Catalog/Tropical/` (`TropicalTorsionCensus`, `TropicalCensusMulti`,\n`TorsionProfileAmbiguity`, `FactorLocationBarriers`).\n\nWhat the formalisation established, in one sentence: *for a squarefree modulus the\nwhole `KROOT` free-witness family (`d \u21a6 #{x : x^d = 1 mod N}`) is a tropical\npolynomial whose data is the per-prime multiset of tropical roots, and that data is\ninvariant under shuffling roots between the prime factors \u2014 which is exactly why it\ncannot locate a factor.* The conjectures below push on the three places where the\nproofs were tight.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Tropical Sealing Conjecture). Every multiplicative \"spectral\" statistic of `(\u2124/N\u2124)\u02e3` is root-shuffling invariant, hence cannot locate.\n\n**Statement.** Let `F` be any function of `N` computable from the isomorphism class of\nthe abelian group `(\u2124/N\u2124)\u02e3` alone. Then for squarefree `N = \u220f p`, `F(N)` is invariant\nunder any permutation of the local valuation multisets `{v_\u2113(p-1)}_{p | N}` that\npreserves the multiset at each prime `\u2113`. Consequently there exist, for every\n`B`, two `B`-bit semiprimes with equal `F` and coprime factorisations.\n\n*The key insight is* that `TropicalTorsionCensus.gcd_profile_eq_of_valuations` already\nproves the invariance for the torsion profile, and the torsion profile is a complete\nisomorphism invariant of a finite abelian group; so the conjecture reduces to a purely\ntropical statement about shuffling roots of one-variable min-plus polynomials, with the\nopen part being the *density* claim (existence of shuffled pairs at every bit size,\nwhich needs a Linnik/Bombieri\u2013Vinogradov type input on primes in progressions).\n\n*Why now?* The invariance half is formalised and machine-checked; only the\nprime-density half remains, and it is a statement about primes `p \u2261 1 + 2^a\u00b7u`, an area\nwhere effective results exist.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Tropical Corner Barrier). No `poly(log N)`-time algorithm can evaluate the census exponent `k \u21a6 min(k, v\u2082(p-1)) + min(k, v\u2082(q-1))` at any `k \u2265 1` for adversarial semiprimes.\n\n**Statement.** Evaluating `T(k)` for one `k \u2208 [1, log N]` is as hard as factoring, in the\nsense that an oracle returning `T(2)` for arbitrary semiprimes yields a probabilistic\npolynomial-time factoring algorithm.\n\n*The key insight is* that `T(2) = 2^{min(2,a)+min(2,b)}` distinguishes `a = 1` from\n`a \u2265 2`, i.e. it reveals `p mod 4`-type information about the *unordered* pair, and\ncombining this with the classical square-root-of-1 factoring reduction (which our\n`energyGroundSet_eq` four-point ground space makes explicit) should upgrade the oracle\ninto a factorisation, showing that the *sealing* result of\n`census_cannot_locate` is tight rather than an artefact.\n\n*Why now?* The exact formula `torsionCensus_eq` is now a theorem, so the reduction can\nbe attempted symbolically rather than experimentally.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3 (Level-0 invisibility is the only obstruction). The census exponent function determines the multiset `{v\u2082(p-1) : p | N}` completely once the number of prime factors is known.\n\n**Statement.** For squarefree `s, t` with `s.card = t.card`,\n`(\u2200 k, censusExponentMulti s k = censusExponentMulti t k)` implies the multisets\n`{v\u2082(p-1) : p \u2208 s}` and `{v\u2082(p-1) : p \u2208 t}` are equal, *including* the level-0 part.\n\n*The key insight is* that `census_determines_root_multiplicities` already gives equality\nat every level `\u2265 1`; the level-0 multiplicity is then forced by the cardinality\nconstraint, so the conjecture should follow from a counting argument \u2014 but our current\nproof does not carry `s.card = t.card`, and making it do so requires a\ntropical-degree statement (`deg` of a min-plus polynomial = number of roots with\nmultiplicity, including roots at `0`).\n\n*Why now?* It is a two-lemma addition on top of the existing multiplicity theorem and\nwould complete the classification of exactly what the free-witness family sees.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Tropical hypersurface form of the factorisation problem). The set of factorisations of `N` is the corner locus of the two-variable tropical polynomial `P(X,Y) = (X \u2299 Y) \u2295 N`, and locating a corner point requires `\u03a9(\u221aN)` queries in the min-plus query model.\n\n**Statement.** In the min-plus model where a query at `(x, y)` returns\n`min(x + y, log N)` plus the indicator of equality, any deterministic algorithm needs\n`\u03a9(\u221aN)` queries to output a nontrivial corner point of `P`.\n\n*The key insight is* the straddle theorem `divisor_pair_straddles_corner`: every divisor\npair meets the corner `\u221aN` from both sides, so a query at `(x,y)` only reveals which\nside of the tropical line the point lies on \u2014 one bit \u2014 while the number of candidate\ncorner points below the corner is `\u221aN` and exactly one is nontrivial\n(`nontrivial_witness_below_corner`).\n\n*Why now?* Both ingredients are proved; what remains is to formalise the query model\nand run an adversary argument, which is a finite combinatorial statement well suited to\nLean.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Zero-entanglement is generic for algebraic witness Hamiltonians). Any energy of the form `E(a,b) = g(ab)` with `g(N) = 0` has a ground space that is a union of `\u03c4(N)` product states, hence entanglement entropy exactly `0` and no descent gradient.\n\n**Statement.** Formalise \"product state\" and \"zero entanglement\" for the divisor-pair\nground space and prove that the ground space of every `ab`-mediated energy is a disjoint\nunion of `\u03c4(N)` rank-one tensors, so no tensor-network ansatz with bond dimension `> 1`\ncan help.\n\n*The key insight is* that `energyGroundSet_eq` shows the ground space is a finite set of\n*points* (not a correlated subspace), and `energy_gap` shows the landscape is a delta:\nthe search cost is therefore the counting `4/N\u00b2`, independent of the representation.\n\n*Why now?* The ground-space computation is done; the remaining step is a definition of\nthe Schmidt rank of a finitely supported state, which Mathlib's tensor-product API can\nsupport directly.\n",
