    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 next-cycle conjectures from the round-3 closures\n\nThe five round-3 hypotheses (RS-MIND, MODPAR-CERT, BURAU-ORD, DENS-SUB,\nCONG-DIV) are now closed with machine-checked Lean proofs in\n`Catalog/Computation/Factoring/`.  What the formalization exposed, beyond the\npaper's numerics, is that all five closures factor through **two** structural\nfacts: (i) a CRT splitting `\u2124/N \u2245 \ud835\udd3d_p \u00d7 \ud835\udd3d_q` that makes every invariant a pair\nof prime-level invariants, and (ii) Dirichlet's theorem, which says residue data\ncannot separate those pairs.  The conjectures below are the sharpest testable\nstatements that this pair of facts suggests.\n\n---\n\n## C1. The CRT-splitting dichotomy for evaluation codes\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `k \u2264 min(p,q)` and every `N = pq`, the *weight\nenumerator* of the evaluation code `C_k(N)` \u2014 not merely its minimum distance \u2014\nis the Hadamard product of the two Reed\u2013Solomon weight enumerators, and every\none of its \"gaps\" (weights not attained) determines `max(p,q)`.  Consequently a\n`poly(log N)`-time algorithm for *any* single nonzero coefficient of the weight\nenumerator yields a factoring algorithm.\n\n*The key insight is* that `RSMind.card_zeroSet_le` is really a statement about\nthe product structure of the zero set (`RSMind.card_zeroSet_le_mul`): the whole\nweight spectrum, not just its minimum, is a product of prime-level spectra, so\nevery spectral feature is a free witness.\n\n*Why now?* The minimum-distance case is fully proved (`min_distance_general`,\nexact for all `k \u2264 p`); the general spectrum needs only a counting refinement of\nthe same CRT injection, with no new machinery.\n\n## C2. Beyond congruences: polynomial-time-determined invariants\n\n**Conjecture.** `FreeWitness.not_revealsFactor_of_congruenceDetermined`\ngeneralises from \"determined by `N mod m`\" to \"determined by the value of a\nfixed polynomial-size arithmetic circuit on the digits of `N`\": no such\ninvariant names a nontrivial factor of every large semiprime, unless factoring\nis in P.\n\n*The key insight is* that the Dirichlet argument only used one property of the\nclass `{N : N \u2261 a mod m}` \u2014 that it contains two *coprime* semiprimes.  Any\ninvariant whose level sets contain two coprime semiprimes is blind in exactly\nthe same way, and level sets of small circuits are large.\n\n*Why now?* The meta-theorem is already stated abstractly over an arbitrary\n`I : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115`; only the \"level sets are rich\" input has to be replaced, turning a\nnumber-theoretic lemma into a combinatorial one.\n\n*Round-4 progress.* The *list* version of C2 is now proved:\n`CandidateLists.no_bounded_candidate_list` shows that a congruence-determined\nfunction returning a whole set of candidate divisors, of size bounded by any\nfixed `k`, still fails on some large semiprime \u2014 the witness being a family of\n`k+1` pairwise-coprime semiprimes in one residue class\n(`CandidateLists.exists_coprime_semiprime_family`).  What remains open in C2 is\nreplacing \"congruence-determined\" by \"circuit-determined\".\n\n## C3. Braid words versus order-finding: an exact equivalence\n\n**Conjecture.** For every braid word `w \u2208 B\u2083` whose exponent sum is `e \u2260 0`, the\norder of the reduced Burau image `r_a(w)` over `\u2124/N` equals\n`c(w) \u00b7 ord_N(a^{e/ gcd}) ` for a word-dependent constant `c(w) \u2264 6`; hence\n*every* braid word (not just `\u03c3\u2081\u03c3\u2082`) is an order-finding instance, and no choice\nof word escapes barrier 6/8.\n\n*The key insight is* that `Burau.bm_cube` identifies the centre `\u0394\u00b2 = (\u03c3\u2081\u03c3\u2082)\u00b3`\nwith the scalar `a\u00b3`, so the order of any word is controlled modulo the centre \u2014\nthe quotient `B\u2083/Z(B\u2083) \u2245 PSL(2,\u2124)` is virtually free, and the scalar part is the\nonly place where `a` can hide.\n\n*Why now?* `Burau.orderOf_bm` proves the case `w = \u03c3\u2081\u03c3\u2082` exactly\n(`orderOf = lcm(3, ord a)`), and `Burau.lcm_dvd_card_burauSubgroup` shows the\ngroup order inherits it; the general word case is the same computation done\nalong the Zassenhaus filtration.\n\n## C4. A decision-tree lower bound with a matching upper bound\n\n**Conjecture.** The MODPAR primitive has query complexity exactly\n`\u0398(m / log m)` for recovering `{p mod m, q mod m}`: the adversary bound of\n`ModPar.transcript_eq_of_avoids` (a query set avoiding \u2264 6 marked classes is\nuninformative) is tight up to the `log` factor achieved by a randomized\ngroup-testing strategy over the residues.\n\n*The key insight is* that the parity pattern is a *sparse* Boolean vector (3 ones\nout of `m`, `ModPar.card_support`), and sparse recovery from parity queries is\nexactly group testing \u2014 so the true complexity should be sub-linear, contradicting\nthe folklore `\u03a9(m)` reading of the closure.\n\n*Why now?* The support and its size are both proved exactly, so the two sides of\nthe bound can be compared without any further structure theory.\n\n## C5. Games whose equilibria are *not* the answer\n\n**Conjecture.** Every finite game whose unique pure Nash equilibrium is a\nnontrivial divisor of `N` and whose payoff function is evaluable in\n`poly(log N)` time yields a `poly(log N)` factoring algorithm; equivalently, in\nany such game the payoff oracle must itself decide divisibility\n(`CongDiv.payoff_nonneg_iff_dvd`).  Hence no game-theoretic reformulation can be\na genuine algorithm.\n\n*The key insight is* that `CongDiv.best_response_unique_general` shows the\nequilibrium is `minFac N` for *every* composite `N`, and\n`CongDiv.payoff_constant_off_divisors` shows the payoff landscape has no\ngradient: the only information channel is the divisibility test itself.\n\n*Why now?* Both halves are proved for the concrete congestion game; the\nconjecture asks only to abstract \"payoff evaluable in poly time\" into the\nhypothesis, which is a definitional step.\n\n## C6. Quantitative candidate-list growth\n\n**Conjecture.** The bound of `CandidateLists.no_bounded_candidate_list` is\nquantitatively sharp in the following sense: for a modulus `m` and a threshold\n`B = N`, any congruence-determined candidate list that succeeds on all\nsemiprimes below `N` must have size `\u03a9(\u03c0(\u221aN) / m)`, i.e. essentially the trial\ndivision list, and the residue class contributes only a constant-factor saving.\n\n*The key insight is* that the coprime family constructed above can be taken\ninside a single class of *any* modulus, so the class structure removes no\ncandidates at all beyond those excluded by congruence obstructions on the\nfactors themselves.\n\n*Why now?* The qualitative statement is proved unconditionally; the quantitative\nversion needs only a counting version of the same Dirichlet input (prime\ncounting in progressions), which is available in effective form.\n",
