    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 after Round-6: the Noise-Floor Principle and the Trace-Lemma Frontier\n\nRound 6 closed the scalar and matrix theory of the noise floor: the minimum\nexcess risk of *every* spectral filter equals `b \u00b7 tr(A(A+b\u00b71)\u207b\u00b9)`\n(`isLeast_filterRisk`, `trace_resolvent_eq_effDim`, `isLeast_filterRisk_matrix`),\nthe minimiser is unique (`filterRisk_eq_noiseFloor_iff`), ridge attains it only\nunder an exact isotropy relation (`ridge_optimal_iff_self_similar`) and misses it\nby at least `4/3` otherwise (`ridge_strict_gap_two_modes`), the floor is squeezed\nby a head/tail count and by a log-determinant capacity\n(`half_minSum_le_noiseFloor`, `noiseFloor_le_capacity`), maximised at the\nisotropic spectrum (`isGreatest_noiseFloor_of_energy`), early stopping\ndominates matched ridge one-sidedly (`gradFlow_le_four_mul_ridge`,\n`ridge_can_be_arbitrarily_worse`), and \u2014 for a geometric spectrum \u2014 the\nlog-corrected `1/N` scaling law is now derived rather than assumed\n(`geometric_scaling_law`).\n\nThe following conjectures are the natural next cycle.  Each is falsifiable, and\neach is stated so that a single Lean theorem would settle it.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D1 (Loewner monotonicity of the noise floor)\n\nFor real positive semidefinite `A \u227c B` (Loewner order) and `b > 0`,\n\n```\ntr (A (A + b\u20221)\u207b\u00b9)  \u2264  tr (B (B + b\u20221)\u207b\u00b9).\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that `x \u21a6 x/(x+b) = 1 - b/(x+b)` is operator monotone, so\nthe statement reduces to the antitonicity of the matrix inverse on the positive\ncone, `A \u227c B \u27f9 (B+b\u00b71)\u207b\u00b9 \u227c (A+b\u00b71)\u207b\u00b9`, followed by taking traces \u2014 turning an\nanalytic inequality into a congruence-transformation argument with\n`PosSemidef.sqrt`.\n\n**Why now?** Round 6 already provides the exact resolvent diagonalisation\n(`resolvent_eq`) and the identification of the trace with `effDim`, so the only\nmissing ingredient is the operator-inverse antitonicity, which is a\nself-contained matrix lemma; with D1 the entire Part I\u2013VI theory upgrades from\n\"fixed spectrum\" to \"monotone in the data covariance\", i.e. *more data in the\nLoewner sense never lowers the effective dimension.*\n\n**Falsifier:** a pair `A \u227c B` of `2\u00d72` PSD matrices with\n`tr(A(A+b)\u207b\u00b9) > tr(B(B+b)\u207b\u00b9)`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D2 (Sharp early-stopping constant)\n\nThe constant `4` in `gradFlow_le_four_mul_ridge` can be replaced by\n\n```\n\u03ba* = sup_{u \u2265 0}  [ e^{-2u} + (1-e^{-u})\u00b2 ] / [ (1+u)^{-2} + u\u00b2(1+u)^{-2} ]  <  2,\n```\n\nand `\u03ba*` is attained at a unique interior `u* \u2248 2.2`; moreover no constant below\n`\u03ba*` works for any signal-to-noise ratio.\n\n**The key insight is** that the ratio of the two per-mode risks depends on the\nspectrum only through `u = \u03bc\u03c4`, so the whole comparison collapses to a\none-dimensional extremal problem in `u` \u2014 a genuine calculus-of-one-variable\nstatement hidden inside a statistical folklore claim.\n\n**Why now?** The per-mode reduction (`mode_gradFlow_le_four_ridge`) is already\nformal; only the sharp scalar optimisation over `u` remains, and the numerics of\n\u00a76 of `ComputationalEvidence.md` localise the maximiser to `1.68 \u2264 \u03ba* < 2`.\n\n**Falsifier:** a mode `u` where the ratio exceeds `2`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D3 (Power-law scaling law)\n\n*(The exponential case is now closed: `geometric_scaling_law` proves\n`b(m+1)/2 \u2264 noiseFloor \u2264 b(m+1) + b/(1-r)` at the natural cut\n`r^{m+1} \u2264 b \u2264 r^m`, i.e. `noiseFloor \u224d b\u00b7log(1/b)/log(1/r)`.  What remains is\nthe power-law case, where the counting function is polynomial rather than\nlogarithmic.)*\n\nFor the power-law spectrum `a\u1d62 = (i+1)^{-\u03b2}` with `\u03b2 > 1`, truncated at any\n`n \u2265 b^{-1/\u03b2}`, there are constants `0 < c_\u03b2 \u2264 C_\u03b2` with\n\n```\nc_\u03b2 \u00b7 b^{1 - 1/\u03b2}  \u2264  noiseFloor(a, b)  \u2264  C_\u03b2 \u00b7 b^{1 - 1/\u03b2}.\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that the head/tail sandwich of Part IV converts the\nanalytic scaling law into pure counting: `#{i : a\u1d62 \u2265 b}` plus a tail energy, so\nthe exponent `1 - 1/\u03b2` is a statement about the *counting function of the\nspectrum*, not about any optimisation.\n\n**Why now?** `half_minSum_le_noiseFloor` and `minSum_head_tail` reduce the\nproblem to two elementary sums, both of which have closed forms (geometric) or\nstandard integral comparisons (power law); this would give the first fully\nformal derivation of a neural-scaling-law exponent from a variational principle.\n\n**Falsifier:** a `\u03b2` for which the two-sided bound fails for arbitrarily small\n`b`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D4 (Capacity is the exact price of adaptivity)\n\nLet `A_S = {a : \u03a3 a\u1d62 \u2264 S}`.  Then the *regret of the best single ridge parameter*\nagainst the Wiener optimum, in the worst case over `A_S`, is governed by the\ncapacity gap:\n\n```\nsup_{a \u2208 A_S}  [ inf_\u03bb filterRisk(a,b,ridge \u03bb) - noiseFloor(a,b) ]\n      \u224d  b \u00b7 ( logDet(a*, b) - effDim(a*, b) )   for the maximising a*.\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that both the ridge deficiency and the capacity gap\nmeasure the same thing \u2014 the spread of the numbers `a\u1d62/(a\u1d62+b)` around their mean\n\u2014 so an inequality between two symmetric concave functionals should be provable\nby the same tangent-line technique used in `noiseFloor_le_flat`.\n\n**Why now?** Round 6 supplies all three quantities in formal form (`filterRisk`\nfor ridge, `noiseFloor`, `logDet`) plus the tangent-line machinery\n(`tangent_identity`); the conjecture is the first quantitative link between\ninformation-theoretic capacity and the *suboptimality of a hyperparameter\nfamily*, rather than of an estimator.\n\n**Falsifier:** a spectrum with a large capacity gap but ridge-optimal risk (or\nvice versa), e.g. found by optimising over `Fin 3` spectra numerically.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture D5 (Noise floor of a random covariance: Marchenko\u2013Pastur regime)\n\nFor `A = (1/N) X\u1d40X` with `X` an `N \u00d7 n` matrix of i.i.d. standard Gaussians and\n`n/N \u2192 \u03b3 \u2208 (0,1)`,\n\n```\n(1/n) \u00b7 tr (A (A + b\u20221)\u207b\u00b9)  \u2192  1 - b \u00b7 m_\u03b3(-b),\n```\n\nwhere `m_\u03b3` is the Stieltjes transform of the Marchenko\u2013Pastur law; consequently\nthe noise floor per parameter converges to `b (1 - b m_\u03b3(-b))`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the trace lemma turns the noise floor into a linear\nspectral statistic of the sample covariance, precisely the class of quantities\ncontrolled by the Stieltjes transform \u2014 so a random-matrix limit theorem\ntransfers verbatim to a learning-theoretic constant.\n\n**Why now?** The catalog already contains a Wigner semicircle development\n(`Catalog/MachineLearning/WignerSemicircle/`), and Round 6 supplies the exact\nidentity `tr(A(A+b)\u207b\u00b9) = \u03a3 \u03bc\u1d62/(\u03bc\u1d62+b)` needed to phrase the limit as convergence\nof an empirical spectral integral; joining the two files is the shortest path to\na formally verified \"double descent\"-style curve.\n\n**Falsifier:** a mismatch between the finite-`n` numerics of\n`tr(A(A+b)\u207b\u00b9)/n` and `1 - b m_\u03b3(-b)` beyond `O(n^{-1/2})`.\n\n---\n\n### Three concrete next-cycle sub-conjectures (recursive abduction)\n\n1. **D1a (scalar shadow of D1):** if `a \u2264 a'` pointwise *after sorting*, then\n   `effDim a b \u2264 effDim a' b` \u2014 already true for the unsorted case\n   (`effDim_mono_spectrum`); the sorted version is what Weyl's inequalities\n   deliver from `A \u227c B`.\n2. **D2a:** `(1-e^{-u})(1+u) \u2264 3u/2` for all `u \u2265 0` (would immediately improve\n   the early-stopping constant from `4` to `9/4`).\n3. **D3a:** for antitone spectra the head set is an initial segment:\n   `{i : b \u2264 a\u1d62} = Iio k` with `k = #head` \u2014 the missing combinatorial step in\n   front of every scaling-law computation.\n",
