    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 after Experiment 391 (Berggren\u2013Price interlock)\n\nThe Pythagorean-tree line for factoring is now closed with exact statements\n(`Catalog/Algebra/BerggrenPriceInterlock/`, five files, all `sorry`-free):\nboth trees enumerate the same vertex set exactly once, every odd coprime factorisation\n`N = pq` is the node `((p+q)/2,(q\u2212p)/2)` with odd leg exactly `N`, the two descents are\ninequivalent (determinants `\u00b11` vs `\u00b12`, leg-swap symmetric vs not, exactly two shared\nedges), and the traversal cost is inversely coupled to Fermat's cost\n(`m \u2264 2\u00b7s\u00b7(2d+3)\u00b2`). The following conjectures are the natural next cycle.\n\n## C1. Exact Berggren depth = odd-continued-fraction length\n\n**Conjecture.** For every node `(m,n)` the Berggren depth equals\n`d_B(m,n) = \u03a3_i a_i \u2212 (number of \"reflect\" steps)`, where `a_i` are the partial\nquotients of the *subtractive* expansion of `m/n` generated by the three parent rules\n(`m < 2n`: reflect; `2n < m < 3n`: reflect-and-shift; `m > 3n`: subtract `2n`).\nIn particular `d_B(m,1) = (m\u22122)/2` exactly, and `d_B` is a function of the ratio `m/n`\nalone in the sense that `d_B(m,n) = d_B(m',n')` whenever `m/n = m'/n'` (which for\ncoprime pairs is a tautology, so the content is the closed formula, not the invariance).\n\n*The key insight is* that `berg_ratio_bound` (`m \u2264 (2d+3)n`) is the crude shadow of an\nexact identity: the Berggren descent **is** the slow (subtractive) Euclidean algorithm on\n`(m,n)`, so its depth must be the sum of partial quotients, not their number.\n*Why now?* The parent rule is already formalised (`berg_parent`) and the ratio bound is\nproved; only the accounting of the three branch types against the continued-fraction\ndigits remains, and it is a finite induction over the same three cases.\n\n## C2. Price depth is `\u0398(log m)` \u2014 a matching upper bound\n\n**Conjecture.** Every node satisfies `d_P(m,n) \u2264 2\u00b7log\u2082(m) + O(1)`; combined with the\nproved lower bound `m \u2264 2^(d+1)` this makes Price depth `\u0398(log m)`, and hence\n`d_P = \u0398(log N)` at the `N`-node, matching the observed `\u2248 1.4\u00b7log\u2082(p+q)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the Price parent halves `m` when `m` is even and halves `n`\nwhen `m` is odd, and the parity of `m` cannot stay odd twice in a row along a descent\nonce one tracks `(m mod 2, n mod 4)`: a two-step potential `\u03a6 = log\u2082(m\u00b7n)` decreases by\nat least `1` per two steps. *Why now?* `price_parent` is formalised with all four cases\nexplicit, so the potential argument is a direct induction; this would upgrade\n`price_level_gt_fermat` from \"level width beats Fermat\" to a *complete* cost model of the\nPrice tree.\n\n## C3. No polynomial-size branch predicate for the `N`-node\n\n**Conjecture (barrier).** There is no predicate `P(N, w)` computable in time\n`poly(log N, |w|)` such that, for every odd semiprime `N`, `P(N, w)` holds exactly for\nthe prefixes `w` of the Berggren (or Price) address of the `N`-node. Equivalently, the\naddress string is incompressible relative to `N`.\n\n*The key insight is* that a prefix predicate would let one binary-search the address and\nso factor in polynomial time, while the trade-off theorem\n`m \u2264 2\u00b7s\u00b7(2d+3)\u00b2` shows the address *length itself* already encodes the size of Fermat's\nscan \u2014 so the predicate would have to decide Fermat's cost from `N` alone.\n*Why now?* The trade-off inequality gives the first formal handle: it converts \"no\npruning rule\" from folklore into a statement about a proved numerical coupling.\n\n## C4. The `\u00b12` obstruction is the only obstruction: classify the ternary Pythagorean trees\n\n**Conjecture.** Up to relabelling, the only triples of integer linear maps that act as a\nternary tree on the node set `{1 \u2264 n < m, gcd = 1, m+n odd}` with root `(2,1)` are the\nBerggren triple (determinants `\u00b11`) and the Price triple (determinants `\u00b12`), and no\ntriple of determinant `|det| \u2265 3` exists.\n\n*The key insight is* that determinant `d` forces the gcd of an image pair to divide `d`,\nwhich together with the parity condition (`a+c` and `b+d` odd, proved to be necessary)\nleaves only finitely many candidate matrices with entries bounded by the growth of `m`.\n*Why now?* An exhaustive search over `|entries| \u2264 3` already returns exactly the six\nknown generators, so the classification is within reach of a finite case analysis in\nLean.\n\n## C5. A hypotenuse-side tree over the Gaussian integers\n\n**Conjecture.** The obstruction `not_dvd_hypot` (no node has `N \u2223 m\u00b2+n\u00b2` when\n`N` has a prime factor `\u2261 3 mod 4`) is sharp: for `N` all of whose prime factors are\n`\u2261 1 (mod 4)`, the nodes with `N \u2223 m\u00b2+n\u00b2` form exactly `2^{\u03c9(N)}` congruence classes of\n`(m : n) mod N`, one for each factorisation of `N` into Gaussian primes up to units and\nconjugation, and each class contains a node at Price depth `O(log N)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `m\u00b2 + n\u00b2 = |m + in|\u00b2`, so hypotenuse divisibility is a\nstatement about Gaussian ideals, and the tree structure should intertwine with the\nGaussian class group action rather than with the rational ratio `m/n`.\n*Why now?* The mod-4 obstruction is proved, so the complementary case is precisely\ndelimited; Mathlib's `ZMod`, `legendreSym` and Gaussian-integer API make the counting\nstatement formalisable.\n",
