    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Spectral Free-Witness, next cycle\n\nDerived from the analysis and adversarial review of this cycle's verified results\n(`Catalog/Algebra/SpectralFreeWitness.lean`, `\u2026Walk.lean`, `\u2026Sharp.lean`, `\u2026Lower.lean`).\n\nEstablished this cycle (all machine-checked, 0 sorries):\n\n* **Doubling lemma** `exists_dyadic_quarter`, spectral gap `\u03bc_k \u2264 1 \u2212 1/(2(M+1))`.\n* **Recovery** `heat_kernel_order_recovery`: `round(1/p_n(e)) = r` at `n = 8(M+1)\u00b2`\n  whenever `r \u2264 N \u2264 2^M`; corollary for `r = ord_N(b)`.\n* **Operational grounding** `walk_return_eq_heatReturn`: the spectral expression *is*\n  the `n`-step return probability of the diffusion (characters are eigenvectors +\n  discrete Fourier inversion).\n* **Sharpness** `dyadic_gap_isTheta` and **necessity** `heat_kernel_needs_quadratic_time`:\n  the diffusion time is `\u0398((log N)\u00b2)`, not `O(log N)`.\n* **Correction** `heatReturn_approx_multiplicative`: the witness *value* is\n  multiplicative in the order up to `1/N\u00b2`; only the mechanism is spectral.\n* **Payload** `factor_from_even_order`: a recovered even order splits `N` by one gcd.\n\n---\n\n## C1. The sharp dyadic deficiency constant is 2\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `r \u2265 2`, every `k \u2262 0 (mod r)` and every `M` with `r \u2264 2^M`,\n\n  `\u03a3_{t=0}^{M} (1 \u2212 cos(2\u03c0 k 2^t / r)) \u2265 2`,\n\nwith equality only for `(r, k) = (2, 1)`.  Consequently `\u03bb_k \u2264 1 \u2212 2/(M+1)` and\n`\u03bc_k \u2264 1 \u2212 1/(M+1)`, halving the mixing constant to `n = 4(M+1)\u00b2`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the doubling lemma produces not just one far-arc visit but a\n**budget**: the orbit `x, 2x, 4x, \u2026` must cross the far arc, and the crossing costs at\nleast `1` in deficiency, while the *approach* to the crossing (the last doubling before\nit, whose circle distance is exactly half) costs a further amount that never vanishes.\n\n*Why now?* Our proof only extracts the single crossing term (giving `\u2265 1`); the\nexhaustive search in `ComputationalEvidence.md` \u00a75 shows the truth is exactly `2` for all\n`M \u2264 9`, so the missing factor is a bookkeeping argument over consecutive doublings, not\na new idea.  Falsifiable by a single `(r, k, M)` with deficiency `< 2`.\n\n## C2. The Mersenne family is the extremal gap family, with constant `c* \u2248 3.3946`\n\n**Conjecture.** `lim_{M\u2192\u221e} (1 \u2212 \u03bb\u2081(2^M \u2212 1, M)) (M+1) = c*` exists, `c* = 2\u03c0\u00b2/3 \u00b7 \u03ba` for an\nexplicit `\u03ba`, and for all `r \u2264 2^M` and `k \u2262 0`, `(1 \u2212 \u03bb_k)(M+1) \u2265 2` with the supremum of\nthe *minimal-gap-over-large-r* profile attained along Mersenne cycles.\n\n*The key insight is* that for `r = 2^M \u2212 1` the dyadic orbit `{2^t}` is exactly the set of\nbinary place values, so the deficiency is a geometric series `\u03a3 4^t/r\u00b2` truncated at the\ntop \u2014 a self-similar quantity, which is why the numerics converge to five digits by\n`M = 12`.\n\n*Why now?* `dyadicEigen_mersenne_ge` already gives the `\u0398(1/M)` shape with constant 106;\nclosing the gap between 106 and 3.3946 needs only sharper cosine control at the few top\nfrequencies (`t \u2265 M \u2212 3`), where the quadratic bound is wasteful.\n\n## C3. Lacunary base-`a` walks: gap `\u0398(1/(M_a + 1))` with `M_a = \u2308log_a r\u2309`\n\n**Conjecture.** For any integer `a \u2265 2`, the walk with generators `{\u00b1 a^t : t \u2264 M_a}` on\n`Z/rZ` (with `gcd(a, r)` arbitrary) has all nontrivial eigenvalues `\u2264 1 \u2212 1/(M_a + 1)`,\nand the corresponding recovery `round(1/p_n(e)) = r` holds at `n = 8 (M_a+1)\u00b2`; moreover\nthe constant degrades exactly like `log a`, so `a = 2` is optimal among all lacunary bases.\n\n*The key insight is* that the doubling lemma is really an *`a`-adic drift* lemma: the\ncircle distance is multiplied by `a` at every step until it exceeds `r/(2a)`, so the arc\none lands in is `[r/(2a), 1 \u2212 r/(2a)]` and the negative-cosine window shrinks with `a`.\n\n*Why now?* Our `cdist_double` / `cdist_pow_two` proofs are entirely arithmetic and\ngeneralise verbatim with `2 \u2192 a`; the only new ingredient is the cosine sign on the\nnarrower arc.  Falsifiable computationally for `a = 3, 5, 10` in minutes.\n\n## C4. Aggregation necessity in the straight-line model (barrier-4, formalisable form)\n\n**Conjecture.** Any straight-line arithmetic program that, given `r` and `M`, outputs a\nnumber `p` with `|p \u2212 p_n(e)| < 1/(4r\u00b2)` while reading only `o(r)` of the eigenvalues\n`\u03bc_0, \u2026, \u03bc_{r\u22121}` as inputs, must fail on some instance.  Equivalently: the heat-kernel\nwitness is *sealed by aggregation* in the read-limited model.\n\n*The key insight is* that the recovery map is rigid \u2014 `heatReturn_injective` shows the\nvalue determines `r` exactly \u2014 so any program reading few coordinates can be fooled by\nswitching an unread eigenvalue, since each `\u03bc_k^n` can be made to carry `\u0398(1/r)` of the\ntotal mass by choosing `k` in the slow band.\n\n*Why now?* Rigidity is already proved; what remains is a purely combinatorial adversary\nargument in a model simple enough to formalise (finite index sets, no real-RAM\nsubtleties).  This is the first version of \"barrier 4\" that is actually a theorem\nstatement rather than a slogan.\n\n## C5. Witness-value multiplicativity is not an accident: a classification dichotomy\n\n**Conjecture.** Every free witness that recovers a residue/order coordinate `r \u2264 N` to\naccuracy `o(1/r\u00b2)` is, as a function of `r`, within `O(1/N\u00b2)` of a *multiplicative*\nfunction of `r`; the \"spectral vs. CRT-multiplicative\" distinction therefore lives in the\n*computation*, never in the witness value.\n\n*The key insight is* that exact rounding forces the witness to agree with `1/r` (or an\ninvertible reparametrisation of it) to accuracy `1/(2r\u00b2)`, and `1/r` is multiplicative \u2014\nthis is exactly what `heatReturn_approx_multiplicative` proves for the heat kernel.\n\n*Why now?* This cycle turned a qualitative classification label into a quantitative,\ncheckable statement; the general version needs only an abstract \"recovery \u21d2 proximity to\n`1/r`\" lemma, which our rounding lemma `round_one_div_of_close` already contains in germ.\n",
