    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 after CFPERIOD-NULL (Experiment 398)\n\nWhat is now formally settled in `Catalog/Shared/CFPeriodNull.lean`:\n\n1. the PQa state machine of `\u221aN` and its four integral invariants\n   (`step_inv`), hence `h\u00b2 \u2212 N q\u00b2 = \u00b1 d` at every convergent (`pell_value`) and\n   a unit of `\u2124[\u221aN]` at every `d = 1` (`cfRun_pell`);\n2. the reduced-regime theory (`red_step`, `red_run`), hence the\n   de-confounding statement `a_k \u2264 2\u230a\u221aN\u230b` with equality at period end;\n3. the single factor-adjacent exit \u2014 a split square root of `1 mod N`\n   (`split_root_factors`, `pell_unit_splits`) \u2014 and its three obstructions:\n   odd prime powers are immune (`cf_channel_null_on_prime_powers`), the cheap\n   `m\u00b2+1`, `m\u00b2+2` windows give only trivial gcds\n   (`cheap_window_null_m2_add_one/two`), and small denominators form a\n   density-zero set (`cheap_unit_window_sparse`);\n4. the negative-Pell congruence bit and its no-pinning character\n   (`negPell_prime_factor_ne_three_mod_four`, `congruence_no_pinning`).\n\nFive falsifiable follow-ups, ranked by expected impact.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture A (grand challenge): a *split-root density* law for period-end units\n\nFor odd `N` with `r \u2265 2` distinct prime factors, let `u(N)` be the period-end\nunit of `\u221aN` when the period is even.  Conjecture: the probability (over\nsemiprimes `N \u2264 X`) that `u(N)` is a **split** root of `1 mod N` tends to\n`1 \u2212 2^{1\u2212r} \u2212 \u03b4` with `\u03b4 \u2192 0`, i.e. `3/4` for semiprimes; the observed rate in\nthis project is `41/53 \u2248 0.77` (and `206/269 \u2248 0.77` in the original sweep).\n\n*The key insight is* that a norm-`+1` unit is a square root of `1` in\n`(\u2124/N)^\u00d7  \u2245 (\u2124/p)^\u00d7 \u00d7 (\u2124/q)^\u00d7`, and the split roots are exactly the two\n\"mixed-sign\" points of the `2`-torsion `{\u00b11} \u00d7 {\u00b11}`; the conjecture says the\nperiod-end unit equidistributes over that `2`-torsion subgroup modulo the\n`(\u00b11, \u00b11)` diagonal forced by the genus theory of `Q(\u221aN)`.\n\n*Why now?* Sections 4 and 11 of the file already reduce the question to the\n`2`-torsion of `(\u2124/N)^\u00d7`, and the R\u00e9dei\u2013Reichardt `4`-rank machinery is the\nmissing bridge \u2014 a well-posed, computable target.\n\n## Conjecture B: the period-end coordinates are a complete invariant\n\nConjecture: the map `N \u21a6 (h_l, q_l)` (period-end convergent) is injective on\nnon-squares, and `N = (h_l\u00b2 \u2213 1)/q_l\u00b2` recovers `N`.  The second half is\nimmediate from `pell_value`; the first half is the falsifiable part.\n\n*The key insight is* that `cheap_unit_window_sparse` already shows the fibres\nof `q_l` are of size `O(\u221aX)`; injectivity would upgrade a counting bound to a\nstructural bijection, turning \"period statistics\" into a coordinate system on\nnon-squares rather than a channel.\n\n*Why now?* Both directions are decidable on any finite range with the `cfRun`\ndefinition in this file, so a counterexample search costs nothing.\n\n## Conjecture C: an unconditional `\u03a9(log N)` lower bound for the period\n\nConjecture: for every non-square `N`, the period `l(N)` satisfies\n`l(N) \u2265 c\u00b7log N / log log N`, with the convergent-denominator growth\n`q_{k+1} \u2265 q_k + q_{k-1}` as the engine and the regulator `R(N) \u226b log N` as the\ntarget.\n\n*The key insight is* that the invariants proved here give\n`h_k\u00b2 \u2212 N q_k\u00b2 = \u00b1 d_{k+1}` with `1 \u2264 |d_k| \u2264 2\u221aN`, so a *short* period forces a\n*small* fundamental unit, which forces `N` into the `m\u00b2 + c` family with\n`|c| \u2264 2\u221aN` \u2014 exactly the sparse family of `cheap_unit_window_sparse`.\n\n*Why now?* The sparsity theorem is proved; only the Fibonacci-type growth\nlemma for `q_k` (a two-term induction on the same state machine) is missing.\n\n## Conjecture D: the channel is *equivalent* to SQUFOF, not weaker\n\nConjecture: for every odd composite non-prime-power `N`, some state of the PQa\nrun with `d_k` a perfect square yields a proper factor in `O(N^{1/4})` steps,\nand no CF-based extractor beats that exponent; formally, any factor produced by\na function of `(m_k, d_k, h_k, q_k)_{k \u2264 K}` with `K = poly(log N)` implies `N`\nlies in a density-zero family.\n\n*The key insight is* that `pell_value` shows `d_k` is precisely the norm form\nvalue `h\u00b2 \u2212 N q\u00b2`; a square `d_k = t\u00b2` gives the ambiguous form / SQUFOF\nfactorisation, so the whole channel is the norm-form value set, nothing more.\n\n*Why now?* `pell_value` plus `cheap_unit_window_sparse` already give the\n\"density-zero\" half for `d_k = 1`; extending from `d = 1` to `d = t\u00b2` is the\nsame argument with a different constant.\n\n## Conjecture E: no-pinning is a theorem schema, not a fact about `mod 4`\n\nConjecture: for *every* modulus `M` and every function `F` of the residue data\n`(N mod M, l mod M, unit mod M)`, `F` fails to determine any proper divisor of\ninfinitely many semiprimes.  `congruence_no_pinning` proves the case where `F`\nsees only the factor residues; the general case should follow from Dirichlet\nplus a pigeonhole on the finitely many residue patterns.\n\n*The key insight is* that all the structure the channel exposes is invariant\nunder replacing `(p, q)` by any other pair in the same residue classes, and\nDirichlet says those classes are infinite.\n\n*Why now?* The Dirichlet input is already in Mathlib and already used here;\nonly the packaging of \"residue data\" as a finite quotient is missing.\n\n---\n\n### Three concrete next-cycle sub-conjectures\n\n1. **Period-end normal form.**  At the first `k \u2265 1` with `d_k = 1` one always\n   has `m_k = \u230a\u221aN\u230b` (observed on 283/283 non-squares `N \u2264 300`).  This is the\n   only unproved hypothesis of `cf_period_end_quotient`, and it is the\n   integral shadow of the classical purely-periodic expansion of\n   `\u230a\u221aN\u230b + \u221aN`.\n2. **Converse of the negative-Pell dichotomy.**  If the period is odd then\n   `x\u00b2 \u2212 N y\u00b2 = \u22121` is soluble, i.e. `N` is a sum of two coprime squares.  This\n   project proves the obstruction direction only\n   (`negPell_prime_factor_ne_three_mod_four`).\n3. **Square-`d` states and the SQUFOF exponent.**  Some `k = O(N^{1/4})` has\n   `d_k` a perfect square, and the associated ambiguous form factors `N`; by\n   `pell_value` this is exactly the statement that the norm form `h\u00b2 \u2212 N q\u00b2`\n   takes a square value early.  Falsifiable on any finite range with the\n   `cfRun` definition of Section 3.\n\nTwo results promised as sub-conjectures in an earlier draft of this list were\nclosed inside this cycle and are now theorems in the file: the Fibonacci growth\n`q_k \u2265 fib k` (`cf_q_ge_fib`) and the box bound `0 < d_k \u2264 2\u230a\u221aN\u230b`\n(`cf_d_le_two_a0`).\n",
