    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions\n\nThe results of this cycle turn four informal verdicts into theorems: the trace lemma\n`R_k(N) = gcd(k, p-1)\u00b7gcd(k, q-1)`, the joint-closure / no-aggregator barrier, the sterility of the\nsmooth-step walk, the constructive sufficiency of the residue/order coordinate, and the closed-form\namplification of the additive hint. Three further cycles added the *quantitative* form of the\naggregation barrier (the least complete exponent is exactly `lcm(p-1, q-1)`), the squarefree trace\nlemma (`2^\u03c9(N)` square roots of unity), and the identification of the aggregation depth of an\narbitrary odd modulus with its Carmichael exponent. The following are the sharpest open questions\nthose proofs expose.\n\n## C1 (resolved, now generalised). The Carmichael threshold\n\nThe original conjecture \u2014 that the completeness threshold is `\u0398(N)` rather than `\u0398(\u221aN)` \u2014 is now a\ntheorem: the least positive exponent with `R_k(N) = \u03c6(N)` is exactly\n`lcm(p-1, q-1) = \u03c6(N)/gcd(p-1, q-1)`, and this extends to every squarefree modulus, where the\nthreshold is the Carmichael exponent `lcm_{r | N}(r-1)`.\n\nA later cycle went further: `R_k(N) = \u220f_{p | N} gcd(\u03c6(p^{v_p(N)}), k)` for *every* odd `N`, which\nidentifies the aggregation depth of an arbitrary odd modulus as its Carmichael exponent\n`\u03bb(N) = lcm_{p | N} \u03c6(p^{v_p(N)})`.\n\n**Successor conjecture (the 2-adic case).** For `N = 2^e \u00b7 M` with `M` odd and `e \u2265 3`,\n`R_k(N) = gcd(2, k) \u00b7 gcd(2^{e-2}, k) \u00b7 \u220f_{p | M} gcd(\u03c6(p^{v_p}), k)`, and the least complete\nexponent is `lcm(2^{e-2}, \u03bb(M))` \u2014 strictly smaller than `\u03c6(N)`, so the even part *shortens* the\naggregation depth by a factor of two without ever making it polynomial.\n\n*The key insight is* that the proof for odd moduli needed only multiplicativity of the witness over\ncoprime factors plus cyclicity of each local unit group, and cyclicity fails exactly at `2^e`,\n`e \u2265 3`, where the local group is `Z/2 \u00d7 Z/2^{e-2}`; the missing ingredient is a root count in a\nproduct of two cyclic groups, which the multiplicativity of root counts over direct products already\nsupplies.\n\n*Why now?* Multiplicativity of the witness over coprime factors and the product root count are both\nestablished, and the `Z/2 \u00d7 Z/2^{e-2}` structure of the unit group modulo `2^e` is standard, so only\nthe transport step is missing.\n\n## C2. Joint closure survives unbounded but sparse exponent sets\n\n**Conjecture.** For every infinite set `S \u2286 \u2115` of exponents with `#(S \u2229 [1, X]) = O(X^{1-\u03b5})` and\nevery prime `q`, there are still two distinct primes `p \u2260 p'` with `R_k(pq) = R_k(p'q)` for all\n`k \u2208 S \u2229 [1, N]`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the Dirichlet argument used for finite `S` only needs the primes to be\n`\u2261 1` modulo `lcm(S \u2229 [1, X])`, and for sparse `S` that lcm is still small enough for a prime of the\nright size to exist under standard prime-gap heuristics \u2014 so sparsity, not finiteness, is the\noperative hypothesis.\n\n## C3. An unconditional lower bound\n\nCan the exponent-depth lower bound be promoted from \"algorithms restricted to free witnesses\" to\n\"algorithms with black-box access to arithmetic modulo `N`\"? A generic-group-model result of this\nshape would be the strongest available formal evidence that factoring is classically hard, and it is\nthe precise open problem the framework isolates.\n\n## C4. Beyond gcd channels for random walks\n\nThe sterility theorem closes the greatest-common-divisor channel for multiplicative walks. Whether\n*any* efficiently samplable classical stochastic process on `Z/N` can have provably useful randomness\nbeyond the birthday floor of its orbit remains open; all known candidates reduce either to smoothness\n(subexponential, governed by the Dickman\u2013de Bruijn density) or to order finding (currently quantum).\n\n## C5. Pricing hints properly\n\nHint amplification in the Coppersmith tradition converts roughly half the bits of `p` into the full\nfactorisation in polynomial time. The framework's scope must therefore be stated as \"extraction from\n`N` alone\" rather than \"factoring\", and a companion theory that prices *partial* hints \u2014 how many\nbits of `p+q`, or of `\u03c6(N)`, suffice \u2014 is entirely open.\n",
