    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 after closing the higher-power reciprocity channel\n\nWhat this cycle established, formally and sorry-free\n(`Catalog/Combinatorics/PowerResidueCriterion.lean`,\n`PowerResidueCircularity.lean`, `PowerResidueNoAmplification.lean`,\n`PowerResidueSharpness.lean`):\n\n1. the `k`-th power criterion in any finite cyclic group, and its transfer to\n   `(ZMod p)\u02e3` \u2014 the algebraic definition of the cubic/quartic symbol;\n2. the cubic and quartic residuacity bits of `2` are **not** computable from any\n   congruence datum `p % M` with `M \u2223 lcm(1,\u2026,16)`, for any value type and any\n   decision rule, while the quadratic bit *is* computable from `p % 8`;\n3. every `K`-symbol residuacity fingerprint has capacity exactly `2^K`,\n   independently of the exponent `k`, and that ceiling is attained at `K = 2`;\n4. `k`-th powers are `1/k` of the units, so the cubic bit is strictly *sparser*\n   (hence individually less informative) than the quadratic bit;\n5. a hybrid bound: hint + `L` dials + `K` higher-power bits still leave a class\n   of at least `|\u03a9| / ((M*/gcd(M*,m))\u00b72^K)` indistinguishable candidates.\n\nThe five conjectures below are the sharpest open questions this leaves.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture A (unbounded escape). *For every modulus `M`, cubic residuacity of `2` is not `M`-periodic on primes.*\n\nFormally: for every `M \u2265 1` there are primes `p \u2261 q (mod M)`, both `\u2261 1 (mod 3)`,\nwith `2` a cube mod `p` and not mod `q`.  We proved this for every `M \u2223 720720`;\nthe general case is stated but not settled.\n\n**The key insight is** that `2` is a cube mod `p` exactly when `p = x\u00b2 + 27y\u00b2`,\na *splitting* condition in the non-abelian field `\u211a(\u221b2, \u03c9)`, and a congruence\ncondition would force the splitting set of a degree-3 non-normal field to be a\nunion of ray classes \u2014 contradicting the non-abelian Galois group `S\u2083`.\n\n**Why now?** Mathlib now has Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic\nprogressions and a usable class-field-theoretic vocabulary; the remaining gap is\na Chebotarev-free argument: it suffices to exhibit, for each `M`, a *pair* of\nprimes in one class with different splitting behaviour, and the `x\u00b2 + 27y\u00b2`\nparametrisation makes such pairs constructible rather than merely dense.\n\n## Conjecture B (exact channel capacity, not just the ceiling). *For bases `a\u2081,\u2026,a_K` multiplicatively independent modulo cubes, the cubic residuacity fingerprint attains all `2^K` patterns on primes below `exp(O(K))`.*\n\nWe proved attainment for `K = 2` with the explicit set `{7, 31, 61, 307}`; the\nconjecture asserts attainment in general with a singly-exponential search bound.\n\n**The key insight is** that the joint distribution of cubic bits should be\ngoverned by the Galois group of the compositum `\u211a(\u03c9, \u221ba\u2081, \u2026, \u221ba_K)`, which is\n`(\u2124/3)^K \u22ca \u2124/2` when the bases are independent modulo cubes; equidistribution\nthen forces every pattern to appear with density `3^{-K}`.\n\n**Why now?** The `2^K` upper bound is already formal, so a matching constructive\nlower bound would turn the capacity statement into an exact theorem \u2014 the first\n*sharp* information-theoretic statement about a residue channel in this catalog.\n\n## Conjecture C (bias beats count). *A `K`-bit cubic fingerprint separates strictly fewer candidates on average than a `K`-bit quadratic one, by a factor `(3/4)^{K/2+o(K)}`.*\n\nMotivated by the proved sparsity `(p\u22121)/3` versus `(p\u22121)/2` and by the measured\n`23` versus `31` distinct patterns on the 68 primes of `[1000,2000]`.\n\n**The key insight is** that capacity is not the number of readings but the\nentropy of their distribution: a bit that is `1` with probability `1/3` carries\n`H(1/3) \u2248 0.918` bits, so `K` cubic bits carry `0.918\u00b7K` bits against the\nquadratic `K` \u2014 the higher reciprocity law is *strictly worse per symbol*.\n\n**Why now?** The counting lemma `card_powerResidue_units` is the only analytic\ninput needed; the remaining step is a Chernoff/entropy estimate over a candidate\nset, entirely inside Mathlib's `Probability` library.\n\n## Conjecture D (transversality is generic). *For every pair of exponents `k \u2260 l` with `k, l \u2265 2` and every base `a` that is neither a `k`-th nor an `l`-th power in `\u2124`, all four combinations of `k`-th and `l`-th power residuacity of `a` occur among primes.*\n\nProved here for `(k, l) = (2, 3)`, `a = 2`, with the witnesses `7, 13, 31, 43`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the two residuacity conditions are splitting\nconditions in linearly disjoint Kummer extensions, so their bits are\nindependent; failure would exhibit an unexpected containment of number fields.\n\n**Why now?** Transversality is exactly the property that makes a channel\n*combinable*; a general theorem would let the hybrid bound\n`hybrid_no_amplification` be proved *tight*, closing the loop from upper bound to\nexact rate.\n\n## Conjecture E (circularity is intrinsic). *Any algorithm that outputs the cubic bit of `a` at a secret prime `p`, given only `N = p\u00b7q` and `poly(log N)` congruence data, can be converted into a factoring algorithm with polynomial overhead.*\n\nThis is the barrier-6 statement the experiment asserts informally.  Our CRT\ntheorem `isPowerResidue_mul_moduli` is its algebraic half: the `N`-computable\nresiduacity datum is the *conjunction* of the two local data, hence symmetric in\n`p` and `q`.\n\n**The key insight is** that a symmetric datum plus one asymmetric bit yields the\nfactorisation: knowing the cubic bit at `p` but not at `q` breaks the symmetry\nthat `ZMod N` arithmetic imposes, and the CRT isomorphism turns that asymmetry\ninto an explicit idempotent, i.e. into a factor.\n\n**Why now?** With the CRT half formalised, the missing step is a reduction, and\nthe catalog already contains a reduction calculus in the `Round11` and\n`DialThreshold` files; formalising Conjecture E would upgrade \"we could not find\na use\" into \"a use would be a factoring algorithm\".\n",
