    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 TRACEPROFILE (Paper 50 / Experiment 385)\n\nThe verified results of this cycle are in `Catalog/Novelty/TraceProfile*.lean`:\n\n* `TraceProfileLowBits.lean` \u2014 the exact low bit `s\u2081 = 1 - N\u2081`, its `3/4` failure at\n  bit 2, and the fact that the visible bit is a function of `N mod 4` alone.\n* `TraceProfileTraceSet.lean` \u2014 the trace set `S_R(N) = {x+y : xy = N}`, its ring\n  isomorphism invariance, CRT multiplicativity, the exact prime-field size\n  `2|S_p(N)| = p + \u03c7_p(N)`, and the joint law\n  `\u220f(p-1) \u2264 2^{\u03c9} |S_M(N)| \u2264 \u220f(p+1)`.\n* `TraceProfileFactorInvisible.lean` \u2014 `I(p mod q ; N mod q) = 0` as an exact\n  product rule, the failure of that product rule for the trace at `q = 5`, and the\n  size contrast `|S_q(N)| < |factorSet_q(N)| = q - 1`.\n* `TraceProfileCharacterArity.lean` \u2014 the discriminant description of `S_q(N)`, the\n  identification of the visible bit with the Legendre symbol, the `k`-factor low-bit\n  law, and the arity-2/arity-3 dichotomy.\n* `TraceProfilePinningBarrier.lean` \u2014 the counting barrier: congruence data leaves\n  `\u2273 N/2^{\u03c9}` candidate traces in the window `[1, N]`.\n\nBelow are the next-cycle conjectures they suggest.  Each is falsifiable by a finite\ncomputation at a specific prime.\n\n---\n\n## C1.  The trace set is a complete invariant of the modulus\n\n**Conjecture.**  For every odd prime `p` and all `N, N' \u2208 \ud835\udd3d_p^\u00d7`,\n`S_p(N) = S_p(N')` implies `N = N'`.\n\n*Status.* Verified for `p \u2264 41` computationally; formally verified at `p = 13`\n(`traceSet_injective_thirteen`).\n\n*The key insight is* that `S_p(N) = {s : \u03c7(s\u00b2 - 4N) \u2260 -1}` is the \"non-residue\nlocus\" of the quadratic pencil `s\u00b2 - 4N`, and two such loci coincide only if the\npencils agree: the overlap `|S_p(N) \u2229 S_p(N')|` is a two-variable character sum\nequal to `p/4 + O(\u221ap)` for `N \u2260 N'`, whereas equality would force `p/2 + O(1)`.\n\n*Why now?*  The Legendre-symbol identification proved in this cycle\n(`two_mul_card_traceSet_eq_add_legendreSym`) shows the *size* of `S_p(N)` sees only\n`\u03c7_p(N)`; C1 says the *set* sees everything.  That gap is precisely the information\nthat a residue attack fails to convert into factors, and pinning it down turns the\nqualitative verdict \"the trace is the least hidden invariant\" into a theorem about\nhow much is hidden.\n\n## C2.  The arity-3 collapse has threshold exactly 11\n\n**Conjecture.**  For every prime `p \u2265 11` and every `N \u2208 \ud835\udd3d_p^\u00d7`,\n`{x + y + z : xyz = N} = \ud835\udd3d_p`; and `p = 3, 5, 7` are the only exceptions.\n\n*Status.* Verified for `p \u2264 19`; formally verified at `p = 11`\n(`tripleSumSet_full_eleven`) with the exception at `p = 5` also formal\n(`tripleSumSet_not_full_five`).\n\n*The key insight is* that `s` is a three-factor sum iff the quartic\n`w\u00b2 = z\u00b2(s-z)\u00b2 - 4Nz` has a point with `z \u2260 0`; this is a genus-1 curve, so the\nHasse\u2013Weil bound gives `p + O(\u221ap)` points and forces solvability once\n`p \u2265 11`.\n\n*Why now?*  It isolates exactly *why* the trace is special: the two-factor\nconstraint is a conic (one quadratic condition, one bit), the three-factor\nconstraint is an elliptic curve (no condition at all beyond small `p`).  Formalising\nit would need a Hasse-type bound for a specific quartic family \u2014 a realistic\ntarget, since the family is completely explicit.\n\n## C3.  The visible bits are exactly the Jacobi symbols \u2014 no more\n\n**Conjecture.**  Let `M` be squarefree odd and coprime to `N`.  Then the trace set\n`S_M(N)` is determined by, and determines, the tuple of Legendre symbols\n`(\u03c7_p(N))_{p \u2223 M}` *together with* `N mod M`; and the *size* `|S_M(N)|` is\ndetermined by the Jacobi symbol data alone:\n`2^{\u03c9(M)} |S_M(N)| = \u220f_{p \u2223 M} (p + \u03c7_p(N))`.\n\n*Status.* The size formula follows from `card_traceSet_prime` plus\n`traceNat_primorial` (both proved); the \"no more\" half \u2014 that no function of\n`S_M(N)` beyond the character tuple is computable in polynomial time from `N` \u2014 is\nopen.\n\n*The key insight is* that every bit the trace exposes to a congruence battery is a\nvalue of a quadratic character of the *public* modulus, hence computable by\nreciprocity in polynomial time from `N` alone; a leak would have to come from the\n*joint* distribution across primes, which the CRT factorisation of the trace set\nrules out.\n\n*Why now?*  This is the exact information-theoretic form of Barrier 2, and it is\nnow within reach: multiplicativity and the prime-level formula are theorems, so\nonly the \"no residual information\" half remains.\n\n## C4.  Quantitative pinning threshold\n\n**Conjecture.**  For a semiprime `N = pq` and a squarefree odd `M` coprime to `N`,\nthe number of `t \u2208 [1, N]` with `t mod M \u2208 S_M(N)` is\n`(1 + o(1)) \u00b7 N \u00b7 2^{-\u03c9(M)} \u00b7 \u220f_{p \u2223 M}(1 + \u03c7_p(N)/p)`,\nand in particular a congruence battery must use `\u03c9(M) \u2265 log\u2082 N - O(1)` primes before\na single candidate trace remains.\n\n*Status.* The lower bound `\u2265 (\u220f(p-1)/2^{\u03c9}) (N/M - 1)` is proved\n(`trace_pinning_barrier`); the matching upper bound and the `o(1)` are open.\n\n*The key insight is* that the surviving set is a union of `|S_M|` full residue\nclasses, so its density is *exactly* `|S_M|/M` up to boundary terms \u2014 the only\nquestion is the boundary, which is `O(|S_M|)` and hence negligible when `M \u226a N`.\n\n*Why now?*  With the lower bound formal, the upper bound is elementary counting;\ntogether they would give the first fully formal statement of the shape \"residue\ninformation about the trace is worth `\u03c9` bits and the search space is `log\u2082 N`\nbits\", i.e. a proved (not measured) version of the paper's verdict.\n\n## C5.  Higher symmetric functions of a semiprime are congruence-free\n\n**Conjecture.**  For an odd prime `q` and `N \u2208 \ud835\udd3d_q^\u00d7`, the *difference* invariant\n`D_q(N) = {x - y : xy = N}` satisfies `|D_q(N)| = (q + \u03c7_q(-N))/2`, and the pair\n`(S_q(N), D_q(N))` determines the unordered factor pair modulo `q` but never the\nordered one \u2014 so no symmetric residue observable can break the `p \u2194 q` symmetry.\n\n*Status.* Open; the size claim follows the same discriminant computation\n(`x - y` has `(x-y)\u00b2 = s\u00b2 - 4N` replaced by `(x+y)\u00b2 = d\u00b2 + 4N`), so it should be\nprovable by the method of `card_traceSet_prime`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the swap `x \u2194 y` acts on every factorisation datum, and\nthe trace and the difference generate the whole invariant ring of that action; a\ncongruence attack therefore lives on the quotient, where `p` and `q` are\nindistinguishable by construction.\n\n*Why now?*  `card_traceSet_prime` already contains all the machinery (the involution\n`x \u21a6 N/x`, the degenerate-fibre count), so C5 is a short extension that closes the\n\"never isolate `p` or `q`\" clause of the verdict at the same level of rigour as the\nrest.\n",
