    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 the cyclic splitting-type channel\n\nDerived from what survived and what failed in this cycle.  Each conjecture is falsifiable by a\nsingle computation on a cyclic order `n`; each is currently supported by exhaustive enumeration\nin `ComputationalEvidence.md` but *not* proved.\n\nNotation: for a cyclic Galois order `n`, `T(x) = n / gcd(n, x)` is the splitting type,\n`H(T)(n)` its entropy, `nr` the binary \"splits completely or not\" readout, and\n`I_pair(n) = H(\u03a0) \u2212 (1/n)\u03a3_c H(\u03a0_c)` the unordered type-pair / product-residue channel of the\ncatalog model.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (parity dichotomy of the cap)\n\n**For every `n \u2265 2`: `I_pair(n) > 1` if and only if `n` is even and `n \u2265 4`; `I_pair(2) = 1`;\nand `I_pair(n) < 1` for every odd `n \u2265 3`.**\n\n*Status.* Proved for prime orders in both directions (`Ipair_prime_lt_one`,\n`Ipair_prime_eq_one_iff_two`); verified by exhaustive enumeration for every `2 \u2264 n \u2264 24`;\nopen for general composite `n` (in particular for every odd composite `n \u2265 9`).\n\n*The key insight is* that the cap is a statement about the **2-torsion of the Galois group**,\nnot about the number of splitting types: the product residue `N \u2261 pq` determines the pair type\nonly through the quotient by squares, so exactly one bit of pair information \u2014 the parity bit \u2014\nis unconditionally recoverable, and everything above one bit must come from a further even\nlevel of the divisor lattice.\n\n*Why now?* The prime case is now closed in Lean with an exact closed form, so the remaining\ncontent is precisely the composite case; and the coprime additivity of `H(T)`\n(`HT_mul_coprime`) plus the Sylow decomposition (`HT_eq_sum_primePow`) give the algebraic\nscaffolding that was missing before.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2 (CRT additivity of the pair channel)\n\n**For coprime `m, n > 0`: `I_pair(mn) = I_pair(m) + I_pair(n)`.**\n\n*Status.* The analogous statement for the single-prime channel is a theorem\n(`HT_mul_coprime`), and the type map itself is CRT-multiplicative (`typNat_mul_coprime`).\nThe pair statement is confirmed exactly on all nine tested coprime pairs, including\n`I_pair(12) = I_pair(3) + I_pair(4)` and `I_pair(45) = I_pair(9) + I_pair(5)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the pair channel of `C_{mn}` is the *product channel* of the pair\nchannels of `C_m` and `C_n`: CRT splits both the type variable and the conditioning residue\ninto independent coordinates, and mutual information is additive over independent products.\n\n*Why now?* All the ingredients are formalized \u2014 the CRT multiplicativity of the type map, the\nadditivity of `H(T)`, and a general evaluation scheme for `I_pair`.  What is missing is a\nproduct decomposition of the conditional tables `\u03a0_c`, a purely combinatorial step.\n\nConjecture 1 follows from Conjecture 2 together with the 2-adic law of Conjecture 3 and the\nproved bound `I_pair(p) < 1` for odd primes, so this is the highest-leverage target.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3 (the 2-adic pair law)\n\n**For every `k \u2265 1`: `I_pair(2^k) = (4/3)(1 \u2212 4^{\u2212k})`, hence the 2-primary pair channel\nsaturates at exactly `4/3` bits.**\n\n*Status.* Verified exactly for `k = 1,\u2026,6`.  The corresponding *type* statement is now a\ntheorem: `H(T)(2^k) = 2 \u2212 2^{1\u2212k}` with `H(T)(2^k) \u2191 2` (`HT_two_pow`,\n`HT_two_pow_tendsto`).\n\n*The key insight is* that along the 2-adic tower the conditional pair tables are self-similar:\nthe table at level `k+1` is the table at level `k` refined by one extra binary square-class\nbit, which produces a geometric series with ratio `1/4` \u2014 the same self-similarity that gives\nthe exact type law `2 \u2212 2^{1\u2212k}`.\n\n*Why now?* The type-side geometric law is proved, and its proof (an induction over the divisor\ntower `1, 2, \u2026, 2^k` with `\u03c6(2^j) = 2^{j\u22121}`) is exactly the recursion the pair side needs.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4 (sharp second-order asymptotics of the prime channel)\n\n**`p\u00b2 \u00b7 I_pair(p) \u2212 log\u2082 p \u2192 2\u00b7log\u2082 e = 2/ln 2 = 2.885390\u2026` as `p \u2192 \u221e` along the primes;\nin particular `I_pair(p) = (log\u2082 p + 2 log\u2082 e + o(1))/p\u00b2`.**\n\n*Status.* The two-sided bound `1/(p\u00b2 log 2) \u2264 I_pair(p) \u2264 (log\u2082 p + 5)/p\u00b2` is proved\n(`Ipair_prime_lower`, `Ipair_prime_upper_sq`); the sharp constant is not.  Evaluating the\nproved closed form: `p\u00b2 I_pair(p) \u2212 log\u2082 p` = 2.7458 (p=5), 2.7838 (p=7), 2.8301 (p=13),\n2.8541 (p=23), 2.8782 (p=101), 2.8847 (p=1009) \u2014 monotonically approaching `2/ln 2`.\n\n*The key insight is* that in the closed form the `log\u2082 p` coefficients\n`1 \u2212 (p\u22121)(2p\u22121)/p\u00b2 + (p\u22121)(p\u22122)/p\u00b2` cancel to exactly `1/p\u00b2`, so the entire remaining channel\nis the second-order part of the `log(1 \u2212 1/p)` and `log(1 \u2212 2/p)` expansions, whose `1/p` terms\ncancel as well and whose `1/p\u00b2` terms combine to `2/ln 2`.\n\n*Why now?* An exact closed form for `I_pair(p)` now exists in Lean, so the asymptotic question\nis a pure real-analysis exercise with no combinatorics left in it \u2014 a good target for a\nTaylor-expansion formalization.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5 (divisor-lattice extremality)\n\n**Among all cyclic orders with exactly `D` divisors, `H(T)(n)` is maximized by the orders whose\ntype law is closest to uniform on the divisor lattice, and `sup_n (log\u2082 d(n) \u2212 H(T)(n)) = \u221e`\nwhile `inf_n (log\u2082 d(n) \u2212 H(T)(n)) = 0` is attained only at `n \u2264 2`.**\n\n*Status.* The upper bound `H(T)(n) \u2264 log\u2082 d(n)` and its strictness for `n \u2265 3` are proved\n(`HT_le_logb_card_divisors`, `HT_lt_logb_card_divisors`); the extremal analysis is open.\nNumerically the gap is 0.085 at `n = 4`, 0.081 at `n = 6`, 0.36 at `n = 9`, 0.45 at `n = 16`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the deficit `log\u2082 d(n) \u2212 H(T)(n)` is exactly a Kullback\u2013Leibler\ndivergence between the Euler-\u03c6 law `\u03c6(d)/n` and the uniform law on divisors, so the extremal\nproblem is a KL-minimization over the divisor lattice \u2014 a convex problem whose solution should\nbe dictated by the multiplicities in the prime factorization.\n\n*Why now?* The Gibbs estimate used to prove the bound already exhibits the deficit as a sum of\nper-divisor relative-entropy terms, so the KL reformulation is one refactoring away, and the\nSylow decomposition reduces the extremal problem to prime powers.\n",
