    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 Singular Moduli Factoring after the \u221aN Theorem\n\nThe formalisation in `Catalog/Cryptography/SingularModuli/` settles the basic\nquestion: the gcd-of-class-polynomial-values method is exactly a `\u221aN` method,\nits success set has density `\u2264 4h/\u221aN`, the class-number speed-up is cancelled by\nthe evaluation cost, and no precomputed table of evaluation points works for\nmore than logarithmically many primes. The following conjectures are the natural\nnext targets. Each is falsifiable, and each is stated so that a counterexample\nor a proof would be recognisable in Lean.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Degree\u2013density duality: the barrier is a conservation law)\n\n*For every family of integer polynomials `H_k` with `deg H_k = h_k` and every\nfamily of evaluation strategies, the product (density of useful points) \u00d7 (cost\nof one evaluation) is `O(1/\u221aN)` \u2014 the two factors trade off exactly.*\n\nFormally: if `W(H)` denotes any cost measure with `W(H) \u2265 deg H` (Horner is\noptimal up to constants for a generic polynomial) then\n`W(H) \u00b7 S(H, N) / N \u2264 4\u00b7W(H)\u00b7h/\u221aN` cannot be improved to `o(1/\u221aN)` by any choice\nof `H`, because `S \u2264 h(p+q)` is sharp (`successCount_add_two_sq_eq`).\n\n**The key insight is** that `h` appears in the numerator of the density bound\nand in the denominator of the per-evaluation cost with the same exponent, so the\n`\u221aN` rate is a *conservation law of the construction*, not a limitation of the\ncounting technique. **Why now?** `total_work_ge` already proves the `\u221aN/4` lower\nbound for a single monic `H`; the missing step is a cost model in Lean\n(`WorkModel` structure with `W(H) \u2265 natDegree H`) and the corresponding\n`\u2200 H, W(H) \u00b7 N / S(H,N) \u2265 \u221aN / 4`, which is a routine generalisation of the\nproof already in `Sharpness.lean`.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Elliptic escape: CM torsion evades the counting bound)\n\n*Replacing \"evaluate `H_D` at `j\u2080`\" by \"compute the `\u2113`-division polynomial of a\nrandom curve `E/\u2124_N` and gcd its value\" gives a success density bounded by\n`c\u00b7\u2113\u00b2/\u221aN`, i.e. the same `\u221aN` rate but with `h` replaced by the degree\n`(\u2113\u00b2\u22121)/2` \u2014 hence still no gain; but the density is achieved for a positive\nproportion of curves rather than a proportion `\u2248 h/p`.*\n\n**The key insight is** that both methods are instances of one scheme: a fixed\ninteger polynomial whose roots mod `p` encode an arithmetic property of `p`, and\nthe counting theorem `successCount_eq` applies verbatim, since it never uses\nanything about `H_D` beyond its degree. **Why now?** `successCount_eq` and\n`successDensity_le` in this repository are already stated for an arbitrary monic\n`H \u2208 \u2124[X]`, so the whole of ECM-style analysis can be plugged in by instantiating\n`H` with division polynomials; only the sharpness direction (existence of roots)\nneeds new input, namely Hasse's bound.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 3 (Adaptive search does not beat uniform search)\n\n*For every adaptive deterministic algorithm that may choose its next evaluation\npoint as a function of all previously observed gcds, the number of steps needed\nto factor a balanced semiprime `N = pq` is at least `\u221aN/(4h)` in the worst case\nover `p, q`.*\n\nThe results here prove this for *oblivious* (precomputed) strategies\n(`finite_family_table_fails`) and bound the *average-case* rate for uniform\nsampling (`successDensity_le_balanced`). The adaptive case needs the observation\nthat a failed trial returns `gcd = 1`, i.e. one bit, so after `k` failures the\nadversary's set of consistent `(p,q)` shrinks by at most `k\u00b7h(p+q)` residues.\n\n**The key insight is** that a failing gcd query is information-theoretically\nalmost free for the adversary: it excludes only the `h(p+q)` residues counted by\n`successCount_le`, so an adversary argument in the style of\n`RandomnessBarrier.arithmetic_trajectory_blind` should give the matching\nworst-case bound. **Why now?** The exact criterion (`evalGcd_nontrivialDivisor_iff`)\nturns the transcript of an adaptive attack into a purely combinatorial object \u2014\na sequence of residues avoiding two unknown root sets \u2014 which is exactly the\nsetting where a Lean adversary argument is feasible.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Class number cannot be pushed to `\u221aN`)\n\n*For discriminants `|D| \u2264 B` the class number satisfies `h(D) = O(\u221aB log B)`,\nwhile the bit size of `H_D` grows like `h(D)^{2+o(1)}`; consequently the\n\"use a huge class number\" strategy has total cost at least `N^{1/2\u2212o(1)}` even\nunder the most optimistic assumption that a single evaluation costs\n`\u00d5(deg H_D)`.*\n\n**The key insight is** that the barrier proved here is `\u221aN/(4h)` *evaluations*,\nso the only way to reach polynomial time is `h \u2248 \u221aN`, which forces\n`|D| \u2273 N/ log\u00b2 N` and a class polynomial with more than `N^{1/2}` coefficients \u2014\nthe polynomial itself becomes the exponential object. **Why now?** The Lean\nstatement needs only the Brauer\u2013Siegel-type upper bound `h(D) = O(\u221a|D| log|D|)`\nas an explicit hypothesis; the rest is arithmetic on the bounds already proved,\nso the conjecture can be formalised as a conditional theorem today and made\nunconditional when Mathlib gains class-number asymptotics.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 5 (Universality of the `\u221aN` rung for \"algebraic-set\" methods)\n\n*Let `f \u2208 \u2124[X\u2081,\u2026,X_m]` be any fixed polynomial and consider the method\n\"pick `x \u2208 (\u2124/N)^m`, output `gcd(f(x), N)`\". Then the density of successful `x`\nis at most `deg f \u00b7 (1/p + 1/q)`, so every such method is a `\u221aN` method.*\n\nThis is the multivariate generalisation of `successDensity_le`; the substitute\nfor \"at most `deg` roots\" is the Schwartz\u2013Zippel bound `#Z(f) \u2264 deg f \u00b7 p^{m\u22121}`\nover `F_p`.\n\n**The key insight is** that the entire barrier rests on one inequality \u2014 a\nnonzero polynomial has few zeros modulo `p` \u2014 so any method whose useful set is\ncut out by fixed algebraic equations lands on the same rung, regardless of the\ngeometry (elliptic curves, abelian surfaces, Drinfeld modules) used to motivate\nit. **Why now?** Mathlib has the machinery for Schwartz\u2013Zippel-style counting,\nand the CRT transfer lemma proved here (`card_range_filter_crt`) is already\nstated for an arbitrary predicate on `ZMod p \u00d7 ZMod q`, so only the root-count\nstep needs replacing.\n\n---\n\n### Three concrete next-cycle sub-conjectures\n\n1. **(3a)** Every adaptive strategy making `k` queries factors a uniformly\n   random balanced semiprime with probability at most `4kh/\u221aN` (union bound over\n   the transcript) \u2014 provable from `successCount_le` alone.\n2. **(1a)** `successCount H (p*q) = h(p+q) \u2212 2h\u00b2` occurs for infinitely many\n   primes `p` for each fixed `D` (Chebotarev density for the ring class field),\n   making the upper bound attained infinitely often.\n3. **(5a)** For `f(X,Y) = Y\u00b2 \u2212 X\u00b3 \u2212 aX \u2212 b` the density of `(x,y)` with\n   `gcd(f(x,y), N)` nontrivial is `\u0398(1/p + 1/q)`, exhibiting a two-variable\n   instance of Conjecture 5 with matching upper and lower bounds.\n",
