    "description": "# FUTURE DIRECTIONS \u2014 GENERIC-RECOVERY, next cycle\n\nThree cycles of the loop produced: (I) the master bound and the exact fibre\ncount of generic hints, (II) sharpness, an average-case strengthening and the\nexact `t\u22123` bit value of the trace hint, (III) the structural explanation \u2014\nevery deficit observed is the order of an invariance group of candidate\nsymmetries.  The conjectures below are the open ends of that thread.  Each is\nfalsifiable by a finite computation and each has a formalisable statement.\n\n## C1. Deficit = order of the invariance group, exactly (not merely `\u2265`)\n\n**Conjecture.**  Let `S` be a finite candidate set and `h` a hint.  Define the\ninvariance group `\u0393_h = { \u03c3 \u2208 Sym(S) : h \u2218 \u03c3 = h }`.  Then the number of\nreadings satisfies `#readings = |S| / |\u0393_h|` **iff** `\u0393_h` acts freely and\ntransitively on every class; and in general `#readings \u00b7 min_orbit \u2264 |S|`, with\nequality exactly when all orbits are of equal size.  Cycle III proves the\ninequality (`card_image_mul_le`); the conjecture is the equality case and its\ncharacterisation.\n\n*The key insight is* that a hint is not a function but a partition, and a\npartition with a group of symmetries can only be as fine as the coarsest orbit\ndecomposition \u2014 so the \"wasted bits\" of every hint family in the taxonomy are a\nsingle group-theoretic invariant, not four separate accidents.\n\n*Why now?*  Cycles I\u2013III already give the two extreme cases in Lean (trivial\ngroup: exact `2^t`; Klein four-group: exact `2^{t-3}`), so the general statement\ncan be attacked with the existing `cost_ge_of_family` / `card_image_mul_le`\nmachinery plus Mathlib's `MulAction` orbit-counting API.\n\n## C2. The 2-adic root explosion is governed by `v\u2082(p \u2212 q)`\n\n**Conjecture.**  For odd `p \u2260 q`, `N = pq`, `s = p + q`, the number of solutions\nof `x\u00b2 \u2212 s x + N \u2261 0 (mod 2^t)` equals `2^{min(v\u2082(p\u2212q)+1, \u230at/2\u230b)+1}` for\n`t` large, in particular it saturates at `2^{v\u2082(p\u2212q)+2}` and never grows with\n`t` beyond that.  The computed table (`ComputationalEvidence.md` \u00a76) shows\nsaturation at `8` for `v\u2082(p\u2212q)=2` and `16` for `v\u2082(p\u2212q)=3`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the trace hint's sub-bit behaviour is not a constant\n`3`-bit tax but a function of a single 2-adic invariant of the secret pair, so\nthe \"hint deficit\" of a family can depend on the secret and not only on the\nhint \u2014 a genuinely new axis in the taxonomy.\n\n*Why now?*  `sq_congr_iff` (cycle I) already reduces the whole question to a\n2-adic valuation bookkeeping over `\u2124`, which `omega` plus induction on `t` can\ncarry.\n\n## C3. No hint family is position-sensitive at the counting level\n\n**Conjecture.**  For every `k`, `t` and every pair of position sets\n`A, B \u2286 {0,\u2026,k\u22121}` with `|A| = |B|`, and every candidate set `S` that is a\nunion of residue classes mod `2^k`, the two bit-leak hints have identical class\nsize *multisets*, not merely identical sizes on the full cube.  Consequently a\nCoppersmith-style advantage of contiguous top-half leakage is invisible to any\ncounting argument and must be charged entirely to lattice reduction.\n\n*The key insight is* that \"position\" is an algorithmic resource, not an\ninformational one; the Coppersmith condition `t \u2265 k/2` cannot be recovered from\nany cardinality bound, which is why every attempt to beat it by re-arranging\nthe leaked bits has failed.\n\n*Why now?*  `card_fiber_coordRestrict_congr` proves the full-cube case in three\nlines; the general case is the statement that the two coordinate subgroups are\nconjugate under a permutation of the cube, which is within reach of Mathlib's\n`Equiv.Perm` API.\n\n## C4. Composite hints: is the bit budget exactly additive on symmetric sets?\n\n**Conjecture.**  If two hints `h\u2081, h\u2082` have invariance groups `\u0393\u2081, \u0393\u2082` then the\njoint hint `(h\u2081,h\u2082)` has invariance group `\u0393\u2081 \u2229 \u0393\u2082` and usable bits\n`log\u2082(|S|/|\u0393\u2081 \u2229 \u0393\u2082|)`; in particular two `t`-bit hints with the same invariance\ngroup are worth `t` bits together, not `2t`.  Cycle I proves only the upper\nbound `t\u2081 + t\u2082` (`worstCost_pair_ge`).\n\n*The key insight is* that hint composition is intersection of symmetry groups,\nso redundancy between hints is measurable *a priori* \u2014 a designer can tell\nwhether a second leak is free before measuring anything.\n\n*Why now?*  It is the direct group-theoretic dual of `worstCost_pair_ge` and\nneeds only the orbit machinery of C1.\n\n## C5. A trace-set floor for `N`-checkable hints\n\n**Conjecture.**  Let `h(p)` be any hint computable from `(N, p mod m)` alone.\nThen on the set `S_N` of candidates consistent with `N`, `h` cannot cut the\nsearch below the \"trace-set floor\" `|S_N| / m`; in particular no `N`-checkable\nhint of unbounded length beats a `log\u2082 m`-bit external hint.  Cycle I proves the\ndegenerate case (hint depending on `N` only: zero information,\n`worstCost_of_public`).\n\n*The key insight is* that checkability against public data is exactly\nfactorisation through a public quotient, and factorisation through a quotient is\nthe same phenomenon that makes public hints worthless \u2014 the floor is a fibre\ncount, not a cryptographic assumption.\n\n*Why now?*  `worstCost_of_public` gives the mechanism; the general version needs\nonly the product-of-quotients bound already used in `worstCost_pair_ge`.\n",
