    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 Mordell denominators, cycle 6\n\nCycles 1\u20135 refuted the \"only bad primes\" conjecture for `E_N : y\u00b2 = x\u00b3 + N` and identified the\nmechanism (a prime divides `den x(nP)` exactly when `nP` reduces to the identity mod that\nprime).  Cycle 6 attacked the converse question \u2014 *can a factor of `N` ever appear?* \u2014 and\nproved a singular-locus law plus an anti-factoring theorem for doubling orbits.  The following\nconjectures are the concrete next steps that these results suggest.\n\n## Conjecture C1 (the `n`-coprime anti-factoring law)\n\nLet `N` be odd and squarefree, `P` an integral point of `E_N`, and `n \u2265 1` with\n`gcd(n, N) = 1`.  Then `gcd(den x(nP), N) = 1`; conversely, for each prime `p \u2223 N` there are\npoints `P` for which `p \u2223 den x(pP)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that at a bad prime `p \u2223 N` the reduced curve `y\u00b2 = x\u00b3` has cuspidal\nnon-singular locus `E_ns(\ud835\udd3d_p) \u2245 (\ud835\udd3d_p, +)`, on which multiplication by `n` is injective exactly\nwhen `p \u2224 n`; the case `n = 2^k` proved this cycle\n(`integral_point_orbit_no_factor`) is the special case where injectivity is automatic for odd `p`.\n\n*Why now?*  The whole proof reduces to one formal statement \u2014 that `t = x/y` is additive modulo\n`p` on the cuspidal locus \u2014 and the coprime parametrisation `x = a/e\u00b2, y = b/e\u00b3` already in the\ncatalog turns that into a polynomial congruence identity, which is within reach of `linear_combination`.\nThe observed `n = 5` appearance of the bad prime `5` on `E_55` (see `ComputationalEvidence.md`)\nis exactly the predicted converse.\n\n## Conjecture C2 (the filtration is a group filtration)\n\nFor a prime `\u2113 \u2265 5` with `\u2113 \u2224 N` and `m \u2265 1`, the set\n`E_m(\u2113) = {O} \u222a {P : \u2113^{2m} \u2223 den x(P)}` is a subgroup of `E_N(\u211a)`, and\n`v(P + Q) \u2265 min(v(P), v(Q))` for `v(P) = \u00bd\u00b7v_\u2113(den x(P))`.  Consequently\n`d(P, Q) = \u2113^{-v(P\u2212Q)}` is an ultrametric on `E_N(\u211a)` making it a non-archimedean topological\ngroup.\n\n*The key insight is* that the intersection identity `x\u2081x\u2082x\u2083 = \u03bd\u00b2 \u2212 N` for the chord\n`y = \u03bbx + \u03bd` converts the subgroup property into a valuation computation on `\u03bd`, avoiding the\nfull formal-group machinery: the level-1 case is already proved (`kernel_stable_add`) and the\nlevel-`m` case is the same computation with `a\u2081 = a\u2082` cancellation tracked.\n\n*Why now?*  It would give the first genuinely topological statement in this thread \u2014 an\nultrametric group structure on `E_N(\u211a)` built purely from denominators \u2014 and it upgrades every\n\"divides\" statement in the catalog to a metric one.\n\n## Conjecture C3 (primitive divisors: a Zsygmondy law for Mordell denominators)\n\nFor every non-torsion integral point `P` of `E_N` with `N` squarefree there is `n\u2080` such that\nfor all `n \u2265 n\u2080` the denominator `den x(nP)` has a prime divisor `\u2113` with `\u2113 \u2224 den x(mP)` for\nall `1 \u2264 m < n`, and every such primitive `\u2113` is a prime of good reduction for `n` large.\n\n*The key insight is* that primitivity plus the singular-locus law forces the new prime to be\ngood: bad primes are confined to the finitely many indices divisible by a factor of `N`, so\nalmost all primitive divisors are good, which quantifies \"the conjecture fails infinitely\noften\" into \"it fails at almost every index\".\n\n*Why now?*  The catalog already has the apparition-index subgroup `denKernel` and the exact\n`2`-adic growth law; combining them gives a lower bound on `den x(nP)` and an upper bound on\nthe contribution of old primes, which is the standard shape of a Zsygmondy argument.\n\n## Conjecture C4 (a quantitative barrier: denominator oracles are factoring-useless)\n\nLet `Oracle(N, P, n) = den x(nP)`.  For odd squarefree `N`, every value returned for `n`\ncoprime to `N` is a `N`-unit, and the multiset of prime valuations of the oracle outputs is\ndetermined by `(N, P)` alone.  Hence no algorithm making `poly` oracle queries with\n`gcd(n, N) = 1` can output a nontrivial factor of `N` with non-negligible probability.\n\n*The key insight is* that C1 turns the \"no factor appears\" theorem into an information-theoretic\nindependence statement: the oracle factors through data that is invariant under all\nfactorisations of `N`, so it is simulable without knowing `p` and `q`.\n\n*Why now?*  The invariance half is already a theorem (`factorisation_barrier`), and the\n`N`-unit half is proved for `n = 2^k` this cycle; C1 supplies the missing indices, after which\nthe barrier statement is a formal consequence rather than a heuristic.\n\n## Conjecture C5 (density of good denominator primes)\n\nFor fixed `N` and non-torsion `P`, the number of distinct primes dividing\n`den x(P), \u2026, den x(nP)` grows like `c\u00b7n\u00b2/log n` (up to constants), and all but `O(1)` of them\nare primes of good reduction.\n\n*The key insight is* that `log den x(nP) ~ 2\u0125(P)n\u00b2` by the theory of canonical heights, so the\ntotal prime mass grows quadratically while the bad primes contribute `O(log n)`; the good\nprimes must therefore dominate asymptotically.\n\n*Why now?*  The exact `2`-adic growth theorem in the catalog already gives the quadratic lower\nbound along `2^k`-orbits, so a first, unconditional weak form (\"infinitely many distinct good\ndenominator primes for a fixed curve\") looks reachable without the full height machinery.\n",
