    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 tropical theory of boundary-token width\n\nDerived from the results in `Catalog/Tropical/NeuralNetworks/EOSWidth*.lean`\n(analysis stage: what survived, what failed, and why).\n\n## What survived\n\n* The tropical dichotomy is exact, not approximate: `eos_mem_digitSpan_iff`\n  (span membership \u21d4 no exclusive dimension), `margin_le_of_no_exclusive_dim`\n  together with its sharpness `margin_bound_sharp` (best margin `= max\u1d62 c\u1d62`),\n  and `exclusive_dim_unbounded_margin` / `exclusive_dim_margin_robust`.\n* The empirical \"threshold\" reading is provably wrong as a deterministic law:\n  `no_sharp_boundary`.\n* The correct law is a one-sided distribution shift: monotone in width\n  (`separable_mono`, `separable_eosVec_mono`), interior probability in the\n  fragile regime and probability one in the robust regime\n  (`one_sided_distribution_shift`).\n\n## What failed / needed a different definition\n\nA first attempt to state the law as \"the cure probability of a *random readout*\njumps at `E = D`\" is false: a random max-plus readout separates nothing with\nprobability one in either regime, because separation is a property of the\n*learned* readout.  The definition had to move the randomness into the learned\nembedding coefficients `c` and quantify existentially over readouts\n(`Separable`).  With that change the criterion became exact and the probability\nstatement provable.\n\n## Conjectures\n\n### C1 (E = 24, the shape of the shift). The fragile cure probability is strictly increasing in `E` on `0 < E \u2264 D` and is given by `P(max\u1d62\u2264E c\u1d62 > 0)`.\nFormally: for i.i.d. symmetric coefficients, `P(cure | E) = 1 \u2212 2^{-E}` for\n`E \u2264 D` and `1` for `E > D`, so no `E` in the fragile range has probability `0`\nor `1`.\n**The key insight is** that `separable_eosOf_iff` makes the cure event a\n*maximum-of-coefficients* event, and maxima of independent coordinates are\nmonotone in the number of coordinates \u2014 the shift is a max-stability phenomenon,\nnot a capacity phenomenon.\n**Why now?** `separable_eosOf_iff` and `margin_bound_sharp` already pin the\nevent exactly; only the product-measure bookkeeping remains, and the untested\n`E = 24` arm is a direct empirical test of the predicted strict monotonicity.\n\n### C2 (depth amplification, model half now proved). The depth-uniform statement is settled inside the max-plus model: `depth_uniform_ambiguity` shows the fragile boundary trajectory stays within `max\u1d62 c\u1d62` of the all-digit trajectory at *every* unroll depth, and `exclusive_dim_persists` shows the robust regime keeps its exclusive dimension at every depth.  The open conjecture is the quantitative transfer: in a trained GRU the accuracy at unroll depth `n` in the fragile regime should decay smoothly and monotonically with `n`, with no depth at which it recovers.\n**The key insight is** that a max-plus layer is monotone and commutes with\ntropical scalars (`mplusApply_shift`), so a gap bounded at depth 1 can never be\namplified \u2014 which is exactly the observed smooth progressive-unroll collapse\n(1.0000 \u2192 0.9556 \u2192 0.1445 \u2192 0.0166) rather than a cliff.\n**Why now?** The model half is proved with no extra hypotheses, so the empirical\nprediction (monotone, non-recovering degradation in depth for `E \u2264 D`, none for\n`E > D`) is immediately falsifiable on the existing unroll sweep.\n\n### C3 (representational distinctness is a matroid/rank invariant). Whether a boundary token is separable depends only on the *support pattern* of the token relative to the supports of the digit atoms, i.e. on the underlying (tropical) matroid, not on the numerical embedding.\n**The key insight is** that `eos_mem_digitSpan_iff` is stated purely in terms of\nsupports, so separability should factor through the lattice of supports and be\ninvariant under any coordinatewise strictly monotone reparametrisation.\n**Why now?** The catalog already contains tropical matroid material\n(`Tropical/TropicalPointMatroid.lean`); joining it to `InDigitSpan` would place\nthis phenomenon inside an existing invariant theory.\n\n### C4 (exclusive dimensions beat width at real scale). At fixed total boundary width, an embedding with `k \u2265 1` exclusive dimensions cures with probability `1` while a width-`W` embedding with `0` exclusive dimensions cures with probability bounded away from `1` for every `W`.\n**The key insight is** `width_is_not_the_control_variable`: two tokens of equal\ntropical support size can lie on opposite sides of the dichotomy, so any law\nphrased in terms of width alone must be a proxy law.\n**Why now?** The prediction is cheap to test (one exclusive dimension added to a\nwidth-20 EOS) and it separates the width hypothesis from the distinctness\nhypothesis in a single arm.\n\n### C5 (statistical sharpness of the two-regime split). The observed data reject *every* exchangeable one-parameter model of the cure event, not merely the i.i.d. Bernoulli one, with likelihood ratio below `10\u207b\u2075`.\n**The key insight is** that `binomial_kernel_max` bounds the pooled likelihood\nuniformly in the parameter via an AM\u2013GM argument, so the rejection needs no\nasymptotics and should survive replacing independence by exchangeability through\nde Finetti's representation.\n**Why now?** `homogeneous_null_rejected` already gives the i.i.d. case in closed\nform; the exchangeable case is the same bound integrated against a mixing\nmeasure.\n",
