    "description": "# Future directions (derived from the NET-27 formalisation)\n\nThe three files\n`Catalog/NumberTheory/EOSWidthMonotoneRamp.lean`,\n`Catalog/NumberTheory/EOSExclusiveDimGenericity.lean`,\n`Catalog/NumberTheory/EOSRampZetaBridge.lean`\nestablish, in a finite-field obstruction model of the \"exclusive dimension\" reliability curve:\nmonotonicity, strict positivity of failure at every finite width (no capacity cliff), a\ntwo-sided geometric envelope `p^{-k} \u2264 1 - P(cure) \u2264 m\u00b7p^{-k}`, an exact `q`-Pochhammer\ngenericity bound, and an Euler-product identity linking the ramps over all characteristics to\n`\u03b6(s)`.  The following conjectures are the natural next targets.\n\n## C1. Exact multi-obstruction curve via subspace M\u00f6bius inversion\n\n**Conjecture.**  For a family `W\u2081,\u2026,W_m` of proper subspaces of an `n`-dimensional `\ud835\udd3d_p`-space,\nthe failure probability at width `k` is exactly\n`\u2211_{\u2205 \u2260 S \u2286 [m]} (-1)^{|S|+1} p^{-k\u00b7codim(\u22c2_{j\u2208S} W_j)}`,\nand in particular the curve `k \u21a6 P(cure)` is *strictly* increasing whenever `m \u2265 1`, closing the\ngap between `failProb_antitone` (proved) and strict monotonicity (proved here only for `m = 1`).\n\n*The key insight is* that inclusion\u2013exclusion over the lattice generated by the obstructions\nturns the failure count into a signed sum of exact subspace counts, each of which is already a\n`p`-power \u2014 so the whole curve is a finite Dirichlet polynomial in `p^{-k}`, whose leading term\nforces strict decrease of the failure mass.\n\n*Why now?*  The exact single-subspace count (`card_tuples_mem`) and the union bound\n(`failCount_le_sum`) are already formalised; only the alternating-sum bookkeeping over\n`Finset (Fin m)` remains, and `Catalog/NumberTheory/SubspaceCounting.lean` supplies the\nGaussian-binomial machinery for the intersections' codimensions.\n\n## C2. The knee is at `k = \u2308log_p m\u2309` \u2014 a formal \"design rule\"\n\n**Conjecture.**  Define the knee `k*(m,p) := min {k : 1 - P(cure at k) \u2264 1/2}`.  Then in the\nmodel `\u2308log_p m\u2309 \u2264 k*(m,p) \u2264 \u2308log_p (2m)\u2309`, so the empirically observed \"reserve \u2265 4 exclusive\ndimensions\" rule is the statement `m \u2248 8\u201316` obstruction modes at `p = 2`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the two-sided envelope proved in `cureProb_deficiency_two_sided`\nalready pins the deficiency between `p^{-k}` and `m p^{-k}`, so the knee can only move by an\nadditive `log_p m`, never by a multiplicative factor \u2014 a threshold *location* result even\nthough there is no threshold *sharpness*.\n\n*Why now?*  Both envelope inequalities are theorems; the remaining work is a monotone-inversion\nlemma for `k \u21a6 m p^{-k}`.\n\n## C3. Ramp/anti-ramp trade-off has an interior optimum\n\n**Conjecture.**  The lower bound of `prob_indep_and_cure_ge`,\n`f(k) = 1 - m p^{-k} - (p^k-1)/((p-1)p^n)`, is maximised at\n`k* = \u230a(n + log_p(m(p-1)))/2\u230b + O(1)`, i.e. the best exclusive width is roughly `n/2`, and the\nmaximum value is `1 - \u0398(p^{-n/2})`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the reliability error decays like `p^{-k}` while the genericity error\ngrows like `p^{k-n}`, so the sum is a convex Laurent polynomial in `p^k` whose minimum is at the\ngeometric mean of the two scales \u2014 the \"boundary token\" problem is an AM\u2013GM balance, not a\ncapacity constraint.\n\n*Why now?*  Both error terms are already proved in Lean; only a one-variable convexity argument\nover `x = p^k` is missing.\n\n## C4. Zeta-side refinement: the ramp series detects primality\n\n**Conjecture.**  For every integer `q \u2265 2` (not necessarily prime) define the ramp series\n`R_q(s) = \u2211_k q^{-ks}`.  Then `\u220f_{q \u2265 2} R_q(s)` diverges for all `s`, while the product\nrestricted to the characteristics realisable by the model (`q` a prime power) equals\n`\u03b6(s)\u00b7\u220f_{p} \u220f_{r \u2265 2} (1 - p^{-rs})^{-1}`, i.e. the reliability model over all *finite fields*\ncomputes `\u220f_{r\u22651} \u03b6(rs)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that allowing `\ud835\udd3d_{p^r}` as the coefficient field replaces each Euler factor\n`(1-p^{-s})^{-1}` by the full `r`-tower, and the resulting product telescopes into a product of\nshifted zeta values \u2014 so \"which reliability curves are realisable\" is an arithmetic question.\n\n*Why now?*  `EOSRampZetaBridge.tprod_rampSeries_eq_riemannZeta` establishes the `r = 1` layer\nusing Mathlib's `riemannZeta_eulerProduct_tprod`; the model itself already works over any finite\nfield, only the `ZMod p` specialisation is hard-coded.\n\n## C5. Cliffs are impossible for any exchangeable draw model\n\n**Conjecture.**  Let the `k` exclusive directions be drawn from *any* exchangeable distribution\non `V^k` whose one-dimensional marginal has full support.  Then the reliability curve is still\nmonotone and still satisfies `P(cure at k) < 1` for every `k` \u2014 i.e. `IsMonotoneRamp` is\nrobust to the uniformity assumption, and no distributional assumption can create a cliff.\n\n*The key insight is* that the prefix-fibration argument of `failCount_succ_le` never uses\nuniformity, only that the failure event is closed under deleting a coordinate; positivity comes\nfrom the atom at `0`, which any full-support marginal charges.\n\n*Why now?*  The combinatorial core (`failCount_succ_le`) is proved; generalising it means\nreplacing counting measure with an arbitrary product/exchangeable measure, for which Mathlib's\n`MeasureTheory` product API suffices.\n",
