    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 bold, testable conjectures from the NET-31 formalization\n\nThe two Lean files\n(`Catalog/NumberTheory/BoundaryBlockInternalization.lean`,\n`Catalog/NumberTheory/BoundaryBlockCollectiveCRT.lean`) prove that the round's\nheadline law (\"internalization is a seed-fixed trait, width sets `P(cure)`\") is a\ntheorem about *gated readouts*, and that \"the block is used collectively\" is a\ntheorem about *pairwise coprime capacity*. The following conjectures are what\nsurvives as open after that analysis. Each is falsifiable by an experiment the\nlab already knows how to run, or by a Lean proof/counterexample.\n\n---\n\n## C1. Two-parameter rigidity: the trait is `(base, sep)`, and only `sep` is width-visible\n\n**Conjecture.** For every seed there exist width-independent reals\n`base, gain, demand` and an integer `sep` such that (i) the seed cures at width\n`k` iff `sep \u2264 k` **and** `demand \u2264 base + k\u00b7gain`, and (ii) boundary dependence\nat every curing width is exactly `base < demand`. Consequently the dependence\nset is width-invariant, and no function of the `k = 1` outcome alone can predict\nit.\n\n*The key insight is* that the round's two \"impossible\" observations \u2014 the same\ndependent set at `k = 2` and `k = 3`, and the absence of any `k = 1` predictor \u2014\nare not in tension: they are jointly forced as soon as curing needs a\n**resolution** condition in addition to a **capacity** condition, which is\nexactly what `k1_outcome_independent_of_trait` proves (all four combinations of\n`k = 1` outcome \u00d7 trait are realisable, so the two axes are logically\nindependent).\n\n*Why now?* The seed-fixed, width-swept design of this round is the first that\nholds the seed constant while varying `k`, so `sep` and `base` are separately\nidentifiable for the first time. **Test:** fit `sep` from the smallest curing\nwidth of each seed and `base/gain` from one retention read; the model then\npredicts every other width with no free parameters.\n\n---\n\n## C2. Sign-sensitivity is a boundary-case artefact of `k = 2`, not a mechanism\n\n**Conjecture.** For every seed and every width `k \u2265 3`, the single-coordinate\nflip is a no-op; at `k = 2` the flip read is *equivalent* to the whole-block read\n(`flip` hits \u27fa boundary-dependent), with no false positives or negatives.\n\n*The key insight is* that on a block read collectively the flip costs exactly\n`2\u00b7a` of aggregate drive while the whole-block ablation costs `k\u00b7a`, so the flip\nthreshold is `(k-2)\u00b7a`: it degenerates to the `zeroN` threshold precisely at\n`k = 2` (`flip_iff_selfSufficient_at_width_two`) and becomes strictly weaker for\n`k \u2265 3` (`flip1_noop_of_width_three`). At the arithmetic layer sign flips are\n*always* free (`resolves_neg`), so sign sensitivity can never be a capacity\neffect.\n\n*Why now?* The round observed `flip = 0 %` in all 12 arms at `k = 3` and\n`\u22127 \u2026 \u221225 %` in 4/4 dependent arms at `k = 2` \u2014 precisely the predicted\ndegeneracy. **Test:** run `k = 4` and `k = 5`. The conjecture forbids *any*\nflip effect at `k \u2265 3`, and predicts that a two-coordinate flip at `k = 4`\nbehaves like a one-coordinate flip at `k = 2`.\n\n---\n\n## C3. The harmonic law: `k \u00b7 retention(k) \u2192 base / gain`, with one known outlier\n\n**Conjecture.** For a boundary-dependent seed, the `zeroN` retention decays like\n`base/(gain\u00b7k)`; equivalently `k \u00b7 retention(k)` converges and the retention\nseries over widths diverges (`retention_asymptotic`, `not_summable_retention`).\nA single positive-width read therefore determines the whole profile\n(`retention_profile_determined`).\n\n*The key insight is* that if the answer path mixes a width-independent internal\ndrive with `k` equal boundary contributions, then retention is a M\u00f6bius\n(fractional-linear) function of `k`, and M\u00f6bius functions are rigid: one value\nplus the fixed point at `k = 0` pins the rest.\n\n*Why now?* The round's own numbers already put this under stress: fitting\n`gain/base` at `k = 2` predicts `k = 3` retentions of `0.672 / 0.877 / 0.732 /\n0.866` against measured `0.704 / 0.901 / 0.710 / 0.744` \u2014 good for seeds\n`13, 14, 15`, **badly off for seed `17`** (see `ComputationalEvidence.md`).\n**Test:** measure `k = 4` for seeds `15` and `17`. Harmonic decay predicts\nretention `\u2248 0.65` for `s = 15`; a second boundary channel switching on at\n`k = 3` (the natural repair for `s = 17`) predicts a visible kink instead.\n\n---\n\n## C4. Exclusivity is coprimality: the capacity criterion for redundancy\n\n**Conjecture.** \"Exclusive dimension\" is, mechanistically, \"coprime channel\": a\nboundary block behaves as `k` pairwise coprime moduli, so its answer capacity is\nthe *product* of per-dimension capacities, single-dimension ablation is a no-op\nexactly while the answer range stays under the single-drop capacity\n`\u220f_{i \u2260 j} m_i`, and whole-block ablation is always fatal\n(`collective_use`). In particular dropping one of `k` dimensions is safe up to\nrange `2^(k-1)` and unsafe above the single-drop capacity\n(`single_drop_can_break_without_margin`).\n\n*The key insight is* that collectivity (\"`zero1 = 0 %` in every arm, `zeroN`\n`9\u201329 %`\") is not evidence of a distributed code per se: it is the Chinese\nRemainder Theorem, and it comes with a *quantitative* margin that per-instance\nverification must respect.\n\n*Why now?* The design rule \"\u2265 3 exclusive dims \u21d2 reliable cure\" is currently\nstated width-wise; the CRT reading makes it range-wise and hence testable at\nfixed width by enlarging the answer alphabet. **Test:** hold `k = 3` and grow\nthe answer range; the conjecture predicts a sharp onset of `zero1` sensitivity\nonce the range exceeds the smallest single-drop capacity, with the *largest*\nmodulus being the one whose ablation bites first.\n\n---\n\n## C5. A trained-weight predictor of the trait exists and is a rank criterion\n\n**Conjecture.** Boundary dependence is decidable from the trained weights alone,\nwithout any eval draws: the seed is self-sufficient iff the answer-path readout\nrestricted to the non-boundary subspace already meets its decision margin, i.e.\niff the residual threshold `demand \u2212 base` is `\u2264 0`. Operationally: the\nprojection of the answer logit gap onto the complement of the boundary block has\nthe same sign as the full logit gap on every instance.\n\n*The key insight is* that in the gate model the whole trait is one scalar\n(`Dependent R \u2194 0 < R.thr`, `dependent_iff`), and that scalar is a *static*\nfunction of the weights \u2014 the eval battery only estimates it. The battery is in\nfact fully identifying at the block level (`block_identified_by_zero_battery`),\nand the flip arm is redundant given the zero arms (`drive_flip1_eq`), so a\nweight-space predictor cannot be information-limited.\n\n*Why now?* This closes the round's open item \"a trained-WEIGHT predictor of the\nseed trait\" with a concrete, cheap statistic. **Test:** compute the projected\nmargin for all 12 seeds and check it separates `{13, 14, 15, 17}` from the rest \u2014\nthe conjecture is refuted by a single seed on the wrong side, and it also\npredicts the *magnitude* ordering of the `zeroN` hits, which the round showed is\n**not** predicted by coordinate magnitude alone.\n",
