    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 after \"PRNGs cannot beat pigeonhole\"\n\nWhat is settled (formally, 0 sorries, in `Catalog/MachineLearning/PRNGCompression*.lean`):\n\n* a deterministic generator with an `s`-bit seed that reaches every `n`-bit\n  string forces `s \u2265 n` (`prng_seed_bits_lower_bound`);\n* the same holds when the decoder additionally reads an arbitrary side program\n  (`prng_assisted_no_gain`), when generators are chained\n  (`prng_composition_no_gain`), and when a library of `2^m` generators may be\n  searched (`prng_family_no_gain`, `exists_hard_for_whole_family`);\n* the failure is quantitative: at most a `2^(1-d)` fraction of files can be\n  shrunk by `d` bits (`compressible_fraction_le`), and the *average* rate is\n  `n - O(log n)` (`average_length_lower_real`);\n* the bound is tight, which isolates exactly what a PRNG can do: its `\u2264 2^s`\n  outputs admit an `s`-bit code and nothing else is helped\n  (`exists_code_of_small_set`, `prng_dichotomy`).\n\nThe following conjectures are the natural next cycle. Each is falsifiable and\nstated so that a formalization attempt is meaningful.\n\n## C1. Resource-bounded seeds do not restore the trick \u2014 with an *exponential* loss\n\n**Conjecture.** For every polynomial-time generator family `G_n : {0,1}^{s(n)} \u2192 {0,1}^n`\nand every polynomial-time decoder, the number of `n`-bit strings compressible\nbelow `n - d` bits is at most `2^{n-d+1}`, and moreover the *time-bounded*\ncomplexity `KC^t` satisfies `KC^t(x) \u2265 KC(x)` with the counting bound holding\nuniformly in `t`; hence no complexity assumption (P \u2260 NP, one-way functions)\ncan ever be used to beat pigeonhole.\n\n*The key insight is* that the counting argument never inspects the decoder, so\nit survives verbatim under any resource bound \u2014 bounding resources can only\n*remove* decodable strings, never add them.\n*Why now?* Our `KC` and `exists_shortest_code` are stated for an arbitrary\n`D : List Bool \u2192 Bits n`; specializing `D` to a step-indexed interpreter is a\npurely definitional extension of the existing files.\n\n## C2. Randomized compressors: a `\u03b4`-error scheme still needs `n - log(1/\u03b4)` bits\n\n**Conjecture.** If an encoder may toss true coins and is only required to\ndecode correctly with probability `1 - \u03b4` over its own randomness *and* a\nuniform input, then some input still needs `\u2265 n - log\u2082(1/\u03b4) - 1` bits.\n\n*The key insight is* that a randomized scheme is a distribution over\ndeterministic schemes, and an averaging (first-moment) argument selects one\ndeterministic scheme with the same error, which our counting bound already\nkills.\n*Why now?* `prng_assisted_no_gain` already handles \"best of `2^m` deterministic\nschemes\"; the missing step is a Markov-type averaging lemma over `Finset`\ndistributions, which Mathlib's `Finset.sum` API supports directly.\n\n## C3. Structured sources: a sharp \"PRNG dividend\" theorem\n\n**Conjecture.** Let a source be supported on a set `A \u2286 {0,1}^n` and let `G` be\na generator with `s`-bit seeds. The optimal expected code length using `G` is\n`H_0(A \u2229 range G) + Pr[A \u2216 range G]\u00b7n \u00b1 1`; in particular the PRNG's dividend\nequals exactly `Pr[x \u2208 range G]\u00b7(n - s)` bits and vanishes for any source whose\nsupport is not correlated with `range G`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `prng_dichotomy` splits every source into a\n\"generated\" part (compressible to `s` bits, by `exists_code_of_small_set`) and a\n\"free\" part (incompressible, by `exists_KC_ge`); the conjecture says these two\nregimes are the whole story with no interaction term.\n*Why now?* Both halves of the split are already theorems; what remains is the\nexpectation bookkeeping over a `Finset`-supported distribution.\n\n## C4. Descriptive-complexity form of the family bound: the `log`-loss is optimal\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `m`, there is a family `F` of `2^m` decompressors and\na string `x` such that `min_i KC(F_i, x) = n - m` exactly, i.e. the additive\nloss `m` in `exists_hard_for_whole_family` cannot be improved to `m - 1`, while\nsimultaneously *no* family achieves `min_i KC(F_i, x) \u2264 n - m - 1` for all `x`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the family decoder `familyDecoder F` is an optimal\nprefix-selection machine, so the `m` index bits are simultaneously sufficient\n(upper bound, already proved) and necessary (matching lower bound, open).\n*Why now?* `exists_code_of_small_set` gives the tightness technology on the\nconstructive side; the lower bound needs only a partition-counting argument\nover `2^m` decoders.\n\n## C5. Kolmogorov-structure view: \"seed hunting\" is a measure-zero strategy\n\n**Conjecture.** For a uniformly random `x \u2208 {0,1}^n`, the probability that\n*some* generator describable in `m` bits outputs `x` is at most `2^{m+1-n}`; so\nthe expected payoff of searching a library of generators grows only linearly in\nthe library's description size while the search cost grows exponentially.\n\n*The key insight is* that a library is itself data: describing which generator\nto use costs exactly the bits it saves, which is the content of\n`family_hard_strings_are_abundant`.\n*Why now?* The counting half is proved; turning it into a probability statement\nrequires only `PMF.uniformOfFintype` and the existing `Finset.card` bound.\n",
