    "description": "# Future directions\n\nDerived from the formalisation in `Catalog/Algebra/KleberComplementaryProducts.lean` and\n`Catalog/Algebra/KleberManyFoldProducts.lean`, and from the exploratory data in\n`ComputationalEvidence.md`.\n\nWhat is *proved* (sorry-free Lean, characteristic-zero domains, in particular `\u2124` and any\nfield of characteristic zero):\n\n* products `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2` of monomial symmetric functions indexed by pairs with **pairwise\n  distinct multiset unions** `\u03b1 \u228e \u03b2` are linearly independent\n  (`linearIndependent_msym_mul`), and likewise for products of arbitrarily many factors\n  (`linearIndependent_msym_prod`);\n* as a corollary of the many-fold theorem, products of power sums `p_{k_1} \u22ef p_{k_r}`\n  indexed by pairwise distinct multisets of positive exponents are linearly independent\n  (`linearIndependent_psum_monomials`, via `m_{(k)} = p_k`);\n* a product `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2` determines `\u03b1 \u228e \u03b2` (`parts_union_eq_of_msym_mul_eq`);\n* the one-row componentwise splitting case is unconditional\n  (`linearIndependent_row_splittings`);\n* the number-of-variables hypothesis is necessary\n  (`not_linearIndependent_of_too_few_variables`) and the distinct-union hypothesis is not\n  (`linearIndependent_collision_pair_two`, covering every two-element union class).\n\nThe engine behind all of this is the quadratic statistic `Q(d) = \u03a3_i d_i\u00b2`, which satisfies\n`Q(u+v) = Q(u) + Q(v) + 2\u27e8u,v\u27e9`: the monomials of `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2` of minimal `Q` are exactly those\nwhose part multiset is `\u03b1 \u228e \u03b2`. This is a filtration *transverse* to dominance order \u2014 the\ndominance-leading term `m_\u03b8` is the same for every splitting of `\u03b8`, which is why the naive\ntriangularity argument fails.\n\n## Conjecture 1 (union classes). For a fixed multiset `U`, the products `m_A m_B` over unordered multiset splittings `A \u228e B = U` are linearly independent over `\u211a` (and over `\u2124`).\n\nThe key insight is that the `Q`-filtration collapses on a single union class \u2014 all these\nproducts have proportional leading forms, a scalar multiple of `m_U` \u2014 so the class must be\nseparated one layer up, by the monomials obtained from `U` by merging exactly one pair of\nparts, where the coefficient is governed by how `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` distribute the merged pair.\n\nWhy now? Conjecture 1 is exactly the missing half of the argument: combined with the\nalready-formalised `Q`-grading it yields the full componentwise splitting theorem for\nmonomial symmetric functions. It is verified for every `U` with `|U| \u2264 8`, and its smallest\ninstance (`U = {v,v}`) is already a theorem in the Lean files.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (merge filtration). Order monomials by the number of \"merges\" needed to obtain them from `U`. For pairs in one union class the matrix of coefficients at the one-merge layer, indexed by pairs `{\u03b1,\u03b2}` and by merged pairs of parts `{a,b} \u2286 U`, has full row rank.\n\nThe key insight is that a one-merge monomial `w` with merged parts `a, b` receives a\ncontribution from `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2` in exactly two ways \u2014 `a+b` is a single part of one factor, or\n`a` and `b` lie in different factors \u2014 so the coefficient is a linear function of the\n\"crossing statistics\" of the splitting, which are distinct for distinct splittings.\n\nWhy now? The layer-by-layer coefficients are completely explicit finite counts (products of\nbinomial coefficients at the zeroth layer), and the Lean infrastructure for extracting the\ncoefficient at any prescribed monomial (`coeff_msym_mul`, `exists_split_of_coeff_ne_zero`)\nis already in place, so this conjecture is directly attackable by formalisation.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (Schur transfer). The `Q`-filtration argument lifts verbatim from `m` to `s`: for a family of pairs with pairwise distinct multiset unions, the products `s_\u03b1 s_\u03b2` are linearly independent over every commutative ring `R` (not just in characteristic zero).\n\nThe key insight is that `s_\u03bb = m_\u03bb + \u03a3_{\u03bc \u25c1 \u03bb} K_{\u03bb\u03bc} m_\u03bc` is unitriangular with **unit**\ndiagonal, so the `Q`-minimal layer of `s_\u03b1 s_\u03b2` is `m_{\u03b1\u228e\u03b2}` with coefficient a *product of\nbinomial coefficients times 1*, and the leading unit survives base change to an arbitrary\ncommutative ring \u2014 the same mechanism that makes the paper's statement ring-independent.\n\nWhy now? The obstruction is not the argument but the missing Mathlib infrastructure (there\nis no ring of symmetric functions and no Schur function in Mathlib); the present files build\n`m`-functions, orbits, and the coefficient calculus from scratch, so adding Kostka-triangular\n`s`-functions on top of them is a well-defined, self-contained project.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (rigidity: the product remembers the pair). If `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2 = m_{\u03b1'} m_{\u03b2'}` (with enough variables) then `{\u03b1,\u03b2} = {\u03b1',\u03b2'}`.\n\nThe key insight is that `parts_union_eq_of_msym_mul_eq` already forces `\u03b1 \u228e \u03b2 = \u03b1' \u228e \u03b2'`, so\nthe whole question reduces to Conjecture 1 restricted to a two-element subfamily \u2014 rigidity\nis the \"injectivity shadow\" of linear independence.\n\nWhy now? Half of the statement is a theorem in the current files, and the remaining half is\nthe smallest nontrivial case of Conjecture 1, making this the cheapest genuine test of the\none-merge technique of Conjecture 2.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (quantitative dependence threshold). Fix `n`. The family of *all* products `m_\u03b1 m_\u03b2` with `|\u03b1| + |\u03b2| = n` has rank exactly `p(n)` (the dimension of the degree-`n` component), and a maximal independent subfamily can always be chosen by picking one pair per multiset union.\n\nThe key insight is that the `Q`-grading gives a surjection \"pair \u21a6 union\" onto partitions of\n`n`, and the theorem proved here shows any section of that surjection yields an independent\nset; the conjecture asserts that this bound is tight, i.e. unions index a basis of the span.\n\nWhy now? The upper bound `rank \u2264 p(n)` is dimension counting and the lower bound is exactly\nthe formalised theorem, so the conjecture is a sharp, immediately testable statement that\nwould turn the criterion into a complete description of the span of all such products.\n",
