    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 bold, falsifiable conjectures from the NET-45 cycle\n\nEach conjecture is stated so that a single computation, run, or Lean development decides\nit. Formal counterparts of the definitions used here live in\n`Catalog/Logic/KneeMarginChain.lean`, `Catalog/Logic/AttentionSelectionDilution.lean` and\n`Catalog/Logic/AttentionSelectionExchange.lean`.\n\n## C1. The margin law: margins decay geometrically along an exact chain\n\n**Conjecture.** Along a doubling ladder on which the product law `k* = A\u00b7d\u00b7ctx/\u03b4` is exact\nat every rung, the margin at rung `i` satisfies `m_i \u2264 M\u00b7\u03c1^i` with `\u03c1 < 1`; consequently\nthe certified depth of an exact chain grows only like `log(1/\u03b7)`, and **no exact chain of\nmore than `O(log(1/\u03b7))` rungs can ever be certified at noise `\u03b7`**.\n\n*The key insight is* that exactness is a quantisation coincidence: rung `i` reports the\ngrid point immediately above a continuous knee whose fractional position in the last grid\ncell is essentially equidistributed, and the margin is the retained-accuracy increment\nacross that fraction of a cell \u2014 an increment which shrinks as the retained curve\nflattens with context. The NET-45 ladder `0.007, 0.010, 0.003, 0.006, 0.0013` is\nconsistent with `\u03c1 \u2248 0.6` on the odd subsequence and is already at the resolution limit\nof the harness.\n\n*Why now?* `KneeMarginChain.CertDepth` and `certDepth_antitone` give the exact\nbook-keeping, and five rungs of margins are recorded. A sixth rung (`ctx = 4096`) with a\nmargin below `0.0008` would confirm the geometric law and would also prove that the\nprogramme can never certify the chain it is measuring.\n\n## C2. The dilution exponent: the selection gap decays like `k/N_eff`\n\n**Conjecture.** At matched sparsity `k/L`, the selection gap of the measured profiles\nobeys `gap(ctx) \u224d c\u00b7(k/N_eff(ctx))^{1/2} \u2212 k/L`, so with `N_eff \u221d ctx^{0.86}` the gap\ndecays like `ctx^{-0.43}` and reaches zero \u2014 the uniform-attention rigidity point of\n`SelectionDilution.uniform_of_topMass_eq` \u2014 at a finite, computable context.\n\n*The key insight is* that `SelectionExchange.selection_gap_split_eq` makes the gap an\nexact invariant of self-similar refinement, so **any** decay is a measurement of the\nfailure of self-similarity, and the Cauchy\u2013Schwarz cap\n`SelectionDilution.topMass_sq_le_card_mul_sumSq` converts the measured effective support\ndirectly into the achievable gap.\n\n*Why now?* The measured gaps (`+5.3, +5.9, +1.7`) and the two measured\neffective supports (`291.16, 526.39`) already over-determine the two constants; a single\n`ctx = 4096` run tests the prediction, and a gap of zero would be the strongest possible\nnegative result about data-free attention pruning.\n\n## C3. Mass retention and accuracy retention diverge\n\n**Conjecture.** The budget needed to retain `98 %` of the attention *mass* grows like\n`N_eff(ctx) \u221d ctx^{0.86}`, while the budget needed to retain `98 %` of the *accuracy*\ngrows like `ctx` (the product law). The two curves therefore cross, and beyond the\ncrossing context the knee is strictly **above** the mass-retention budget: attention mass\nstops being the mechanism of the knee.\n\n*The key insight is* `SelectionDilution.budget_ge_of_retained_mass`: concentration alone\nforces `k \u2265 \u03b2\u00b2\u00b7N_eff`, a bound with a *different exponent* from the product law, so the\ntwo cannot both be exact laws for all context.\n\n*Why now?* At `ctx = 2048` the two budgets are `\u2248 505` (mass) and `256` (accuracy) \u2014 they\nhave already crossed, and `SelectionDilution.net45_mass_retention_at_knee_bound` proves\nthe knee budget cannot retain the mass. Measuring the retained *mass* at the knee in the\nnext run is a one-line addition to the harness and directly tests the conjecture.\n\n## C4. The certified-depth bound on amplitude identifiability\n\n**Conjecture.** The Zipf amplitude `A` of `Probability.AttentionCostLaw` is identifiable\nonly to width `s/2^{D(\u03b7)}`, where `D(\u03b7)` is the certified depth of the chain at the\nharness noise `\u03b7` \u2014 not to width `s/2^n` for the number `n` of rungs actually run. At\n`\u03b7 = 0.006` this gives width `8`, not the width `2` window `(14, 16]` claimed from four\nrungs.\n\n*The key insight is* that `Logic.KneeResolutionLimits` computes the resolution of a rung\n*assuming its reading is correct*, while `KneeMarginChain.net45_chain_collapse` shows only\nthe first two readings are certified at the measured spread; identifiability must be\ncomputed on the certified prefix.\n\n*Why now?* Both halves are already formal \u2014 the resolution theory in\n`KneeResolutionLimits` and the depth theory here \u2014 so the conjecture is a Lean\ndevelopment away, and it decides whether the programme's headline amplitude window is\nreal.\n\n## C5. Refinement invariance beyond the dyadic case\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `r \u2265 2`, the `r`-fold self-similar refinement (each position\nsplit into `r` positions of weight `p_i/r`) leaves the top-mass functional invariant:\n`T_{rk}(split_r p) = T_k(p)`; and the invariance characterises self-similarity, i.e. a\nprofile family whose gaps are invariant under refinement at *two* coprime ratios is\nasymptotically self-similar.\n\n*The key insight is* that the dyadic proof (`SelectionExchange.topMass_split_le`) needs\nonly one combinatorial fact \u2014 a set of `2m` positions contains `m` positions carrying half\nthe mass \u2014 whose `r`-fold analogue is an equipartition statement: a set of `rm` positions\nsplits into `r` blocks of `m`, one of which carries at least `1/r` of the mass.\n\n*Why now?* The dyadic case is proved with no positivity, ordering or normalisation\nhypotheses, which is exactly the regime in which the equipartition argument generalises;\nand the `r = 4` case is what the programme's `4\u00d7` context jumps actually measure.\n",
